::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|TELEMETRY AND COMPATIBILITY SCHEDULED TASKS
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.003|  What it is      : Seven scheduled tasks. Compatibility Appraiser
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.004|                    inventories your apps and drivers for upgrade-
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.005|                    readiness. Consolidator and UsbCeip are the Customer
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.006|                    Experience Improvement Program uploaders. DmClient and
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.007|                    DmClientOnScenarioDownload drive the feedback prompts.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.008|                    PcaPatchDbTask and StartupAppTask are local
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.009|                    housekeeping for the Program Compatibility Assistant
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.010|                    and for startup-app measurement. On Pro these tasks
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.011|                    are what actually moves data, because the
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.012|                    AllowTelemetry registry policy is clamped to Required
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.013|                    on non-Enterprise editions and writing 0 to it
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.014|                    achieves nothing.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.015|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.016|  Actual effect   : Runs schtasks /Change /Disable on each of the seven.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.017|                    They stay installed and can be switched back on from
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.018|                    Task Scheduler at any time.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.019|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.020|  Gain            : Partial, and worth being precise about. The only one
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.021|                    with a measurable machine cost is Compatibility
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.022|                    Appraiser: it can hold a core and the disk busy for
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.023|                    several minutes when it runs, which you notice on a
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.024|                    slow disk. The other six are cheap enough that turning
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.025|                    them off has no measurable gain in CPU, RAM or boot
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.026|                    time - the gain there is purely that less data leaves
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.027|                    the machine. Anyone who tells you this makes a PC
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.028|                    "telemetry-free" is wrong: DiagTrack itself is
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.029|                    untouched and the plain Compatibility Appraiser
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.030|                    variant may not even be matched by name.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.031|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.032|  Cost            : Windows Update reads appraiser signals to decide
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.033|                    whether your exact hardware and drivers are safe for
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.034|                    the next feature update. With the appraiser off those
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.035|                    signals go stale, so a feature update that a known
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.036|                    incompatibility would have held back can be offered to
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.037|                    you anyway. Nothing breaks day to day.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.038|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.039|  Windows default : All seven ship Enabled.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.040|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.041|  Possible values:
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.042|    enabled              : The shipped state. Each task fires on its own
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.043|                           triggers - mostly idle-time and logon.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.044|                           Compatibility Appraiser walks the installed
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.045|                           application and driver inventory and writes it
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.046|                           under %windir%\appcompat\Programs, where
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.047|                           DiagTrack picks it up; Consolidator and UsbCeip
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.048|                           upload CEIP samples; the two DmClient tasks
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.049|                           schedule feedback prompts and download feedback
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.050|                           scenarios.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.051|    disabled             : schtasks /Change /Disable. The task definition
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.052|                           stays on disk with all its triggers and actions
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.053|                           intact - only the enabled flag flips. Nothing
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.054|                           else demand-starts these tasks, so a disabled
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.055|                           one simply never runs. One click in Task
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.056|                           Scheduler, or the restore branch of this
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.057|                           script, puts it back.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|  Why these profiles : Three profiles agree and one dissents, and the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|                       dissent is the interesting one. Gaming, server and
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.003|                       laptop all disable: none of them wants an idle-time
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.004|                       inventory sweep, and none of them relies on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.005|                       Microsoft to gate feature updates. Office is the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.006|                       profile that keeps them, because the office
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.007|                       archetype is exactly the machine you want Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.008|                       to be conservative with - a family PC or a work
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.009|                       machine that installs feature updates automatically
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.010|                       is the one case where the appraiser's signal earns
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.011|                       its keep, and where a support tool or an IT audit
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.012|                       may expect a stock task set. Laptop is not a
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.013|                       battery decision: most of these tasks already carry
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.014|                       a "start only if on AC power" condition, so on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.015|                       battery they were not running anyway - laptop
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.016|                       disables them for the same privacy reason as
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.017|                       gaming, not for runtime.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.018|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.019|  Known problems  : None confirmed. See uncertain for the Startup impact
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.020|                    claim, which I have deliberately not asserted.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.021|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.022|  Unverified      : Three things I cannot substantiate. (1) The task is
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.023|                    named "Microsoft Compatibility Appraiser Exp" only on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.024|                    some builds; on others it is plain "Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.025|                    Compatibility Appraiser", and schtasks then silently
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.026|                    does nothing - the script should check the exit code
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.027|                    and report which of the seven were actually found. (2)
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.028|                    The widely repeated claim that disabling
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.029|                    StartupAppTask blanks the Startup impact column in
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.030|                    Task Manager / Settings is plausible but I did not
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.031|                    verify it. (3) I have no evidence PcaPatchDbTask sends
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.032|                    anything anywhere; it looks like a local Program
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.033|                    Compatibility Assistant database patch task, so its
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.034|                    presence in a "telemetry" list may simply be folklore
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.035|                    inherited from other scripts.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.036|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.037|  Target          : schtasks /Change /Disable /TN on the seven tasks at
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.038|                    OPTY.bat lines 1556-1562:
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.039|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application Experience\Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.040|                    Compatibility Appraiser Exp",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.041|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.042|                    Experience\PcaPatchDbTask",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.043|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.044|                    Experience\StartupAppTask",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.045|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Customer Experience Improvement
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.046|                    Program\Consolidator", "\Microsoft\Windows\Customer
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.047|                    Experience Improvement Program\UsbCeip",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.048|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Feedback\Siuf\DmClient", "\Microso
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.049|                    ft\Windows\Feedback\Siuf\DmClientOnScenarioDownload"
::
:: ---- gr.allowtelemetry.del (repair) -----------------------------
::P|gr.allowtelemetry.del|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.001|RETIRER LES FORCAGES DE STRATEGIE ALLOWTELEMETRY
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.002|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.003|  Ce que c est    : Deux valeurs de registre qui figent le niveau de
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.004|                    donnees de diagnostic par strategie au lieu de laisser
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.005|                    la main a l'application Parametres. Le second
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.006|                    emplacement, sous
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.007|                    CurrentVersion\Policies\DataCollection, est un chemin
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.008|                    historique que beaucoup de scripts d'optimisation
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.009|                    ecrivent encore et que personne ne nettoie jamais.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.010|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.011|  Effet reel      : Supprime les deux valeurs. Le niveau redevient celui
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.012|                    affiche dans Commentaires et diagnostics, et la
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.013|                    banniere d'organisation disparait.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.014|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.015|  Gain            : Cote vie privee sur Famille ou Pro : aucun gain
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.016|                    mesurable, puisque 0 n'y a jamais ete respecte. Le
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.017|                    vrai gain, c'est que Parametres cesse de vous mentir :
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.018|                    la liste deroulante redevient utilisable et la machine
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.019|                    n'affirme plus a tort qu'une organisation la gere.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.020|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.021|  Cout            : Rien. C'est une simple remise en ordre, appliquee sans
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.022|                    poser de question.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.023|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.024|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux absentes. Le niveau est un choix de
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.025|                    l'utilisateur, fait dans Parametres.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.026|
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.028|    DELETE               : Les deux valeurs sont supprimees. Le niveau de
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.029|                           donnees de diagnostic redevient celui
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.030|                           qu'affiche Parametres > Confidentialite et
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.031|                           securite > Commentaires et diagnostics, et la
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.032|                           mention "Certains parametres sont geres par
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.033|                           votre organisation" disparait de cette page.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.034|                           C'est l'etat d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.035|    0                    : "Securite" : donnees de diagnostic reduites a
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.036|                           ce dont Windows Update et Defender ont besoin.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.037|                           Respecte uniquement sur Entreprise, Education
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.038|                           et IoT Entreprise. Sur Famille et Pro, le
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.039|                           systeme le remonte silencieusement a 1 : la
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.040|                           machine envoie exactement autant qu'avant, et
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.041|                           vous heritez seulement de la banniere "gere par
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.042|                           votre organisation" et d'une liste deroulante
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.043|                           grisee.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.044|    1                    : "Requis" (anciennement Basique). Le plancher
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.045|                           sur Famille et Pro. Uniquement les donnees
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.046|                           d'appareil, de pilotes et de fiabilite.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.047|    2                    : "Ameliore". Abandonne : Windows 11 le ramene a
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.048|                           Requis. L'ecrire ne fait rien, mais produit
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.049|                           quand meme la banniere de strategie.
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.050|    3                    : "Facultatif" (anciennement Complet). Ajoute la
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.051|                           navigation, l'usage des applications et le
::T|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.052|                           detail des vidages sur incident.
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils suppriment, et il n'y a aucune
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.002|                         distinction a inventer. C'est une reparation, pas
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.003|                         une preference : la valeur ne pouvait pas faire
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.004|                         ce qu'elle promettait sur cette edition, et la
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.005|                         laisser en place ne fait que decrire faussement
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.006|                         l'etat de la machine. Si vous etes sur Entreprise
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.007|                         ou Education et voulez reellement le niveau
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.008|                         Securite, passez par la strategie "Autoriser les
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.009|                         donnees de diagnostic" dans gpedit : le reglage
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.010|                         sera visible et reversible la ou un
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.011|                         administrateur le cherchera, pas sous forme de
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.012|                         valeur brute deposee par un fichier batch.
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.013|
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.014|  Cible           : reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\D
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.015|                    ataCollection" /v "AllowTelemetry" /f and reg delete "
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.016|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policie
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.017|                    s\DataCollection" /v "AllowTelemetry" /f (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|gr.allowtelemetry.del.018|                    lines 1619-1620)
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.001|REMOVE THE ALLOWTELEMETRY POLICY OVERRIDES
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.002|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.003|  What it is      : Two registry values that pin the diagnostic data level
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.004|                    by policy instead of letting the Settings app own it.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.005|                    The second location, under
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.006|                    CurrentVersion\Policies\DataCollection, is a legacy
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.007|                    path that many optimizer scripts still write and that
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.008|                    nothing ever cleans up.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.009|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.010|  Actual effect   : Deletes both values. The level reverts to the one
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.011|                    shown on the Diagnostics & feedback page and the
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.012|                    organization banner goes away.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.013|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.014|  Gain            : For privacy on Home or Pro: no measurable gain,
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.015|                    because 0 was never honoured there in the first place.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.016|                    The real gain is that Settings stops lying to you -
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.017|                    you get an ungreyed dropdown and no false claim that
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.018|                    an organization manages this machine.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.019|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.020|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair and applies without
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.021|                    asking.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.022|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.023|  Windows default : Both absent. The level is a user choice made in
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.024|                    Settings.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.025|
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.027|    DELETE               : Both values removed. The diagnostic data level
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.028|                           goes back to whatever Settings > Privacy &
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.029|                           security > Diagnostics & feedback shows, and
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.030|                           the "Some settings are managed by your
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.031|                           organization" note on that page disappears.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.032|                           This is the shipped state.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.033|    0                    : "Security" - diagnostic data limited to what
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.034|                           Windows Update and Defender need. Honoured only
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.035|                           on Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise. On
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.036|                           Home and Pro the OS silently clamps it back up
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.037|                           to 1, so the machine sends exactly as much as
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.038|                           before; all you get is the managed-by-your-
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.039|                           organization banner and a greyed dropdown.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.040|    1                    : "Required" (formerly Basic). The floor on Home
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.041|                           and Pro. Device, driver and reliability data
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.042|                           only.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.043|    2                    : "Enhanced". Retired - Windows 11 maps it onto
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.044|                           Required. Writing it is a no-op that still
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.045|                           produces the policy banner.
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.046|    3                    : "Optional" (formerly Full). Adds browsing, app
::T|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.047|                           usage and crash-dump detail.
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.001|  Why these profiles : All five profiles delete, and there is no
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.002|                       distinction to draw. This is a repair, not a taste:
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.003|                       the value could not do what it advertised on this
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.004|                       edition, and leaving it behind only misreports the
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.005|                       machine's state. If you are on Enterprise or
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.006|                       Education and genuinely want the Security level,
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.007|                       set it through gpedit's Allow Diagnostic Data
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.008|                       policy so the setting is visible and reversible
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.009|                       where an administrator would look for it, not as a
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.010|                       raw value dropped by a batch file.
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.011|
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.012|  Known problems  : With either value present, the Diagnostics & feedback
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.013|                    page in Settings shows a managed-by-your-organization
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.014|                    note and greys the level dropdown, even though on Home
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.015|                    and Pro the policy is not actually changing the level.
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.016|
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.017|  Target          : reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\D
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.018|                    ataCollection" /v "AllowTelemetry" /f and reg delete "
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.019|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policie
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.020|                    s\DataCollection" /v "AllowTelemetry" /f (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|gr.allowtelemetry.del.021|                    lines 1619-1620)
::
:: ---- gr.telemetry.tasks.enable (preference) -------------------------
::P|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable|disabled|disabled|enabled|disabled|enabled|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.001|LES SEPT TACHES DE TELEMETRIE ET COMPATIBILITE, COTE RESTAURATION
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.002|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.003|  Ce que c est    : Les memes sept taches que dans la question de
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.004|                    configuration, abordees depuis le menu de
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.005|                    restauration. Ce n'est pas un second reglage : ce sont
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.006|                    les memes sept indicateurs d'activation, donc ils
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.007|                    doivent obeir au meme chiffre.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.008|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.009|  Effet reel      : Repasse les sept en Active, quel que soit l'etat
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.010|                    laisse par une execution precedente.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.011|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.012|  Gain            : Windows Update retrouve son signal d'aptitude a la
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.013|                    mise a niveau, et la machine presente un jeu de taches
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.014|                    d'origine a tout outil de support ou audit qui vient
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.015|                    regarder.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.016|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.017|  Cout            : Compatibility Appraiser represente une vraie charge
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.018|                    CPU et disque ponctuelle, quelques minutes a
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.019|                    l'inactivite. Les six autres ne coutent rien de
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.020|                    mesurable.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.021|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.022|  Defaut Windows  : Les sept activees.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.023|
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.024|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.025|    enabled              : schtasks /Change /Enable. Les declencheurs se
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.026|                           reactivent selon leur planning normal, surtout
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.027|                           a l'inactivite et a l'ouverture de session.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.028|                           Compatibility Appraiser reprend l'inventaire
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.029|                           des applications et des pilotes et rafraichit
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.030|                           le signal d'aptitude a la mise a niveau.
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.031|    disabled             : La tache reste installee, declencheurs intacts,
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.032|                           mais ne se declenche jamais. Rien ne demarre
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.033|                           ces taches a la demande : desactive veut donc
::T|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.034|                           vraiment dire jamais.
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Identique a la carte de configuration, par
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.002|                         construction : un indicateur d'activation par
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.003|                         tache ne peut pas contenir deux reponses selon le
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.004|                         menu par lequel vous etes entre. Bureautique et
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.005|                         le defaut Windows activent ; gaming, serveur et
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.006|                         portable desactivent. Si vous avez repondu 3 ou 5
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.007|                         ailleurs dans le script, cette restauration ne
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.008|                         fait qu'appliquer ce que votre reponse impliquait
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.009|                         deja, et ne change donc rien.
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.010|
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.011|  Problemes connus : Aucun confirme.
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.012|
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.013|  Non verifie (en)  : Same naming problem as the disable side: if the
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.014|                      build ships the task as "Microsoft Compatibility
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.015|                      Appraiser" without the Exp suffix, this /Enable call
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.016|                      finds nothing and reports success anyway, so the
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.017|                      restore silently under-delivers. The script should
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.018|                      surface the schtasks exit code per task instead of
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.019|                      swallowing it with 2>&1.
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.020|
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.021|  Cible           : schtasks /Change /Enable /TN on the same seven tasks,
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.022|                    OPTY.bat lines 1628-1634 (Microsoft Compatibility
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.023|                    Appraiser Exp, PcaPatchDbTask, StartupAppTask,
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.024|                    Consolidator, UsbCeip, DmClient,
::X|FR|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.025|                    DmClientOnScenarioDownload)
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.001|THE SEVEN TELEMETRY AND COMPATIBILITY TASKS, RESTORE SIDE
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.002|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.003|  What it is      : The same seven tasks as the setup question, approached
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.004|                    from the restore menu. This is not a second setting -
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.005|                    it is the same seven enabled flags, so it has to
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.006|                    answer to the same digit.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.007|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.008|  Actual effect   : Sets all seven back to Enabled, whatever state a
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.009|                    previous run left them in.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.010|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.011|  Gain            : Windows Update gets its upgrade-readiness signal back,
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.012|                    and the machine reports a stock task set to any
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.013|                    support tool or audit that looks.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.014|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.015|  Cost            : Compatibility Appraiser is a real, occasional CPU and
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.016|                    disk load - a few minutes at idle. The other six cost
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.017|                    nothing you can measure.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.018|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.019|  Windows default : All seven enabled.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.020|
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.021|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.022|    enabled              : schtasks /Change /Enable. Triggers fire again
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.023|                           on their normal schedule - mostly idle-time and
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.024|                           logon. Compatibility Appraiser resumes
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.025|                           inventorying apps and drivers and refreshing
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.026|                           the upgrade-readiness signal.
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.027|    disabled             : The task stays installed with its triggers
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.028|                           intact but never fires. Nothing demand-starts
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.029|                           these tasks, so disabled really does mean
::T|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.030|                           never.
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.001|  Why these profiles : Identical to the setup card by construction: one
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.002|                       enabled flag per task cannot hold two answers
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.003|                       depending on which menu you came in through. Office
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.004|                       and the Windows default enable; gaming, server and
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.005|                       laptop disable. If you answered 3 or 5 anywhere in
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.006|                       this script, this restore is what your answer
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.007|                       already implies, and running it changes nothing.
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.008|
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.009|  Known problems  : None confirmed.
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.010|
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.011|  Unverified      : Same naming problem as the disable side: if the build
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.012|                    ships the task as "Microsoft Compatibility Appraiser"
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.013|                    without the Exp suffix, this /Enable call finds
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.014|                    nothing and reports success anyway, so the restore
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.015|                    silently under-delivers. The script should surface the
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.016|                    schtasks exit code per task instead of swallowing it
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.017|                    with 2>&1.
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.018|
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.019|  Target          : schtasks /Change /Enable /TN on the same seven tasks,
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.020|                    OPTY.bat lines 1628-1634 (Microsoft Compatibility
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.021|                    Appraiser Exp, PcaPatchDbTask, StartupAppTask,
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.022|                    Consolidator, UsbCeip, DmClient,
::X|EN|gr.telemetry.tasks.enable.023|                    DmClientOnScenarioDownload)
::
:: ---- gr.recall.policy.del (preference) ------------------------------
::P|gr.recall.policy.del|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.001|WINDOWS RECALL, COTE RESTAURATION
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.002|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.003|  Ce que c est    : Les trois valeurs de strategie WindowsAI et le type de
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.004|                    demarrage de WSAIFabricSvc, abordes depuis le menu de
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.005|                    restauration. Recall capture regulierement l'ecran et
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.006|                    l'indexe pour rendre l'activite passee consultable ;
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.007|                    il n'existe que sur materiel Copilot+ et s'y active
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.008|                    volontairement.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.009|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.010|  Effet reel      : Supprime les trois valeurs de strategie et remet
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.011|                    WSAIFabricSvc en demarrage a la demande : Recall
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.012|                    redevient activable la ou le materiel le permet.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.013|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.014|  Gain            : Aucun sur du materiel ordinaire : il n'y a pas de
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.015|                    Recall a restaurer. Sur une machine Copilot+, vous
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.016|                    retrouvez la recherche dans la chronologie, si vous la
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.017|                    voulez vraiment.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.018|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.019|  Cout            : Sur Copilot+, les captures d'ecran redeviennent
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.020|                    possibles une fois que vous les activez. Partout
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.021|                    ailleurs, rien ne change dans un sens comme dans
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.022|                    l'autre.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.023|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.024|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois valeurs absentes, et le service laisse tel
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.025|                    que l'image l'a livre.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.026|
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.028|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=1 :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.029|        Recall n'a pas le droit d'enregistrer d'instantanes.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.030|        L'interrupteur Recall dans Parametres est grise avec une mention
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.031|        de strategie, et la chaine de capture ne demarre jamais.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.032|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=0 :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.033|        Ce n'est pas le defaut : c'est un "autorise par strategie"
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.034|        explicite, donc un troisieme etat. L'interrupteur reste grise
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.035|        comme gere, simplement dans le sens permissif. N'ecrivez jamais
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.036|        cette valeur pour annuler le blocage : supprimez-la.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.037|    AllowRecallEnablement=0 :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.038|        Recall ne peut plus etre active du tout, meme par un
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.039|        administrateur qui le souhaite. C'est la plus dure des deux
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.040|        strategies : DisableAIDataAnalysis stoppe les captures, celle-ci
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.041|        retire l'option.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.042|    AllowRecallEnablement=1 :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.043|        "Peut etre active" explicite. La encore un troisieme etat, pas la
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.044|        condition d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.045|    DELETE               : L'etat d'origine des trois valeurs : absentes.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.046|                           Sur materiel Copilot+, Recall se comporte alors
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.047|                           comme prevu - inactif tant que l'utilisateur ne
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.048|                           l'active pas, instantanes stockes en local et
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.049|                           proteges par Windows Hello. Sur materiel non
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.050|                           Copilot+, absent et 1 sont indiscernables : il
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.051|                           n'y a rien avec quoi capturer.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.052|    WSAIFabricSvc=disabled :
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.053|        sc config start= disabled. Le service qui heberge la couche IA
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.054|        locale ne peut plus etre demarre par quoi que ce soit, y compris
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.055|        la chaine Recall. Sur une machine ou le service n'existe pas, la
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.056|        commande echoue et ne change rien.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.057|    WSAIFabricSvc=demand : Demarrage a la demande : le service reste
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.058|                           inerte tant que la chaine IA ne le reclame pas.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.059|                           C'est ce qu'ecrit la branche de restauration,
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.060|                           et c'est une supposition raisonnable plus qu'un
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.061|                           defaut verifie.
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.062|    SKIP                 : Laisse le type de demarrage du service tel
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.063|                           quel. La reponse honnete quand on ne connait
::T|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.064|                           pas la valeur d'origine.
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Meme tableau que la carte de configuration,
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.002|                         puisqu'il s'agit des memes trois valeurs. Gaming,
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.003|                         serveur et portable bloquent : l'ecriture ne
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.004|                         coute rien nulle part, et sur la seule categorie
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.005|                         de machine ou Recall existe vraiment - un
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.006|                         portable Copilot+ - la capture continue plus
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.007|                         l'indexation sur NPU representent une vraie
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.008|                         charge de fond, raison pour laquelle le profil
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.009|                         oriente autonomie n'est pas l'exception ici.
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.010|                         L'exception, c'est la bureautique, et
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.011|                         volontairement : la premisse de ce profil est que
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.012|                         les fonctions restent actives, Recall est deja
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.013|                         optionnel et chiffre localement, et le bloquer
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.014|                         par strategie grise un interrupteur qu'un membre
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.015|                         de la famille peut legitimement vouloir. La
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.016|                         reponse Windows est la suppression, puisque
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.017|                         l'absence est le vrai defaut et qu'ecrire 0
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.018|                         serait un troisieme etat.
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.019|
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.020|  Problemes connus : Sur materiel non Copilot+, les appels sc sur
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.021|                     WSAIFabricSvc echouent en silence et le journal
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.022|                     declare quand meme l'etape faite : c'est un defaut de
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.023|                     rapport du script, pas un probleme Windows.
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.024|
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.025|  Non verifie (en)  : I could not verify the shipped start type of
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.026|                      WSAIFabricSvc, so the restore branch writing start=
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.027|                      demand is an assumption, not a known default - that
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.028|                      is why the profile table leaves the service alone
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.029|                      rather than asserting a value. I also could not
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.030|                      confirm that the service exists at all outside
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.031|                      Copilot+ builds; on a machine without it both sc
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.032|                      calls fail and the >nul 2>&1 hides that, so the log
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.033|                      claims a change that never happened.
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.034|
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.035|  Cible           : reg delete
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.036|                    "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsAI"
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.037|                    /v "DisableAIDataAnalysis" /f ; reg delete same key /v
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.038|                    "AllowRecallEnablement" /f ; reg delete
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.039|                    "HKCU\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsAI"
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.040|                    /v "DisableAIDataAnalysis" /f ; sc config
::X|FR|gr.recall.policy.del.041|                    WSAIFabricSvc start= demand (OPTY.bat lines 1637-1640)
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.001|WINDOWS RECALL, RESTORE SIDE
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.002|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.003|  What it is      : The three WindowsAI policy values and the
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.004|                    WSAIFabricSvc start type, approached from the restore
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.005|                    menu. Recall periodically captures the screen and
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.006|                    indexes it so past activity becomes searchable; it
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.007|                    exists only on Copilot+ hardware and is opt-in there.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.008|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes the three policy values and sets WSAIFabricSvc
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.010|                    back to demand, so Recall becomes available to opt
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.011|                    into where the hardware supports it.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.012|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.013|  Gain            : None on ordinary hardware - there is no Recall to
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.014|                    restore. On a Copilot+ machine you get the timeline
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.015|                    search back if you actually want it.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.016|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.017|  Cost            : On Copilot+, screen snapshots become possible again
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.018|                    once you opt in. On everything else, nothing changes
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.019|                    in either direction.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.020|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.021|  Windows default : All three values absent; the service left at whatever
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.022|                    the image shipped.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.023|
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.025|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=1 :
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.026|        Recall may not save snapshots. The Recall toggle in Settings is
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.027|        greyed with a policy note and the snapshot pipeline never starts.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.028|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=0 :
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.029|        Not the default - it is an explicit "allowed by policy", a third
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.030|        state. It still greys the toggle as managed, just in the
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.031|        permissive direction. Never write this to undo the block; delete
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.032|        the value instead.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.033|    AllowRecallEnablement=0 :
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.034|        Recall cannot be turned on at all, even by an administrator who
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.035|        wants it. This is the harder of the two policies -
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.036|        DisableAIDataAnalysis stops the snapshots, this one removes the
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.037|        option.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.038|    AllowRecallEnablement=1 :
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.039|        Explicit "may be enabled". A third state again, not the shipped
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.040|        condition.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.041|    DELETE               : The shipped state for all three values: absent.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.042|                           On Copilot+ hardware Recall then behaves as
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.043|                           designed - off until the user opts in,
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.044|                           snapshots stored locally and gated behind
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.045|                           Windows Hello. On non-Copilot+ hardware absent
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.046|                           and 1 are indistinguishable, because there is
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.047|                           nothing to snapshot with.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.048|    WSAIFabricSvc=disabled :
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.049|        sc config start= disabled. The service that hosts the on-device AI
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.050|        fabric cannot be started by anything, including the Recall
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.051|        pipeline. On a machine where the service does not exist, this
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.052|        command fails and changes nothing.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.053|    WSAIFabricSvc=demand : Start on demand: the service sits idle until
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.054|                           the AI pipeline asks for it. This is what the
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.055|                           restore branch writes, and it is a reasonable
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.056|                           guess rather than a verified default.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.057|    SKIP                 : Leave the service start type exactly as found.
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.058|                           The honest answer when the shipped value is not
::T|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.059|                           known.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.001|  Why these profiles : Same table as the setup card, because it is the
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.002|                       same three values. Gaming, server and laptop block:
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.003|                       the write costs nothing anywhere, and on the one
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.004|                       machine class where Recall is real - a Copilot+
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.005|                       laptop - continuous snapshotting plus NPU indexing
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.006|                       is a genuine background load, which is why the
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.007|                       battery-first profile is not the exception here.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.008|                       Office is the exception, and deliberately so: that
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.009|                       profile's whole premise is features on, Recall is
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.010|                       already opt-in and locally encrypted, and blocking
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.011|                       it by policy greys a Settings toggle a family
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.012|                       member might legitimately want. The Windows answer
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.013|                       is delete, since absence is the real default and
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.014|                       writing 0 would be a third state.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.015|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.016|  Known problems  : On non-Copilot+ hardware the sc calls against
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.017|                    WSAIFabricSvc fail silently and the log still reports
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.018|                    the step as done - a reporting defect in the script,
