::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.001|WINDOWS SPOTLIGHT, RESTORE SIDE
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.002|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.003|  What it is      : The machine-wide policy that switches off the rotating
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.004|                    lock-screen and desktop imagery together with the tips
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.005|                    shown on it.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.006|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value, so the Spotlight pictures and the
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.008|                    Spotlight desktop theme work again.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.009|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.010|  Gain            : You get the rotating photographs back, if you liked
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.011|                    them, and the Personalization page stops showing a
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.012|                    policy marking.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.013|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.014|  Cost            : The tip overlays and their promotional links come back
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.015|                    with the pictures - they are one feature, not two.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.016|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.017|  Windows default : Absent, Spotlight on.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.018|
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.019|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.020|    1                    : The whole Spotlight family is off: no rotating
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.021|                           lock-screen photographs, no Spotlight desktop
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.022|                           theme, no tip overlays and no "like what you
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.023|                           see" promotional links. The Windows Spotlight
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.024|                           option disappears from the Personalization
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.025|                           dropdown rather than being merely unselected,
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.026|                           and because this is an HKLM policy it applies
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.027|                           to every account on the machine.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.028|    0                    : An explicit "Spotlight allowed by policy". A
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.029|                           third state, not the shipped condition; it
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.030|                           still marks Personalization as managed.
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.031|    DELETE               : Absent, the shipped state. Spotlight is
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.032|                           available and each user picks it, or not, in
::T|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.033|                           Personalization.
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.001|  Why these profiles : Gaming, server and laptop set 1; office and the
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.002|                       Windows answer delete. Be clear about the size of
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.003|                       the win, though: there is no CPU or RAM gain here
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.004|                       at all, and the download is a few images on a slow
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.005|                       rotation, so the honest case for 1 is that you do
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.006|                       not want promotional links on your lock screen and
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.007|                       you set your own image anyway. It also carries a
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.008|                       cost the other privacy knobs do not - because it is
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.009|                       an HKLM policy it stamps Personalization as managed
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.010|                       by your organization for every account, which is
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.011|                       exactly the banner this script otherwise refuses to
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.012|                       cause. Office deletes for that reason as much as
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.013|                       for the photographs. If you also answered 1 on the
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.014|                       sponsored-apps card, this one may already be
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.015|                       redundant.
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.016|
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.017|  Known problems  : With the policy at 1, Windows Spotlight vanishes from
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.018|                    the Personalization > Lock screen dropdown and
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.019|                    Settings displays a managed-by-your-organization note
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.020|                    on that page for every user of the machine.
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.021|
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.022|  Unverified      : I did not measure how much Spotlight actually
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.023|                    downloads. It is a handful of images on a slow
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.024|                    rotation, so calling it a bandwidth saving is
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.025|                    generous; treat the network argument as small rather
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.026|                    than as a headline. Whether
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.027|                    DisableWindowsConsumerFeatures=1 already suppresses
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.028|                    Spotlight on 25H2, making this value redundant, is
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.029|                    also unverified.
::X|EN|gr.spotlight.policy.del.030|
