:: ---- reg.console.vt (preference) ------------------------------------
::P|reg.console.vt|1|1|1|1|DELETE|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.001|COULEURS DANS LA CONSOLE (VIRTUALTERMINALLEVEL)
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.002|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.003|  Ce que c est    : Un simple DWORD sous HKCU\Console qui indique a l'hote
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.004|                    de console classique s'il doit interpreter les
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.005|                    sequences d'echappement ANSI/VT ou les afficher comme
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.006|                    des caracteres bruts. C'est uniquement une question
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.007|                    d'affichage.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.008|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit pour le compte utilisateur courant : cela
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.010|                    s'applique a toutes les fenetres CMD de ce compte, pas
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.011|                    seulement a OPTY. OPTY l'ecrit ligne 90, avant de
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.012|                    detecter le caractere ESC, et ne l'enleve jamais
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.013|                    ensuite.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.014|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.015|  Gain            : La sortie d'OPTY devient lisible et coloree au lieu
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.016|                    d'un mur de codes d'echappement. Aucun gain de
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.017|                    performance, d'aucune sorte : ni processeur, ni
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.018|                    memoire, ni latence. Sous Windows 11 avec Windows
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.019|                    Terminal par defaut, l'ANSI fonctionne deja et cette
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.020|                    ecriture ne change strictement rien de visible.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.021|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.022|  Cout            : Un tres vieux programme console qui emettrait des
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.023|                    octets ESC comme donnees et non comme mise en forme
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.024|                    pourrait s'afficher bizarrement ensuite. En pratique,
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.025|                    rien. La valeur reste apres la fermeture d'OPTY.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.026|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.027|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur absente : l'interpretation VT est desactivee
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.028|                    dans conhost.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.029|
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.031|    1                    : conhost.exe interprete les sequences ESC[ ...
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.032|                           du flux de sortie et les applique : couleurs,
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.033|                           deplacements du curseur, effacement de ligne.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.034|                           C'est l'option ajoutee par Microsoft dans
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.035|                           Windows 10 1511 pour que le CMD classique gere
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.036|                           l'ANSI. Cout nul : un automate de plus dans
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.037|                           l'analyseur de texte de la console.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.038|    0                    : conhost affiche les octets d'echappement tels
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.039|                           quels : la sortie d'OPTY arrive en lignes
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.040|                           commencant par fleche-gauche[1;92m. A noter,
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.041|                           c'est le meme comportement que la valeur
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.042|                           absente : 0 est un « desactive » ecrit
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.043|                           explicitement, pas l'etat d'usine.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.044|    DELETE               : L'etat d'usine : la valeur n'existe pas et le
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.045|                           conhost classique n'interprete pas le VT.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.046|                           Windows Terminal, l'application terminal par
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.047|                           defaut de Windows 11, gere l'ANSI de son cote
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.048|                           et ne lit jamais cette valeur : sur un Windows
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.049|                           11 d'origine, la suppression ne change rien de
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.050|                           visible.
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre reponses identiques, et c'est la vraie
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.002|                         table : c'est un drapeau d'affichage, sans
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.003|                         dimension performance, donc jeu, serveur,
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.004|                         bureautique et portable n'ont rien qui les
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.005|                         distingue - les quatre veulent une sortie
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.006|                         lisible. La seule vraie alternative est le profil
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.007|                         5, pour qui tient a un profil de console
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.008|                         strictement d'origine.
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.009|
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.010|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether the current session actually goes through
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.011|                      conhost or Windows Terminal is not detected by the
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.012|                      script, so on a Windows 11 machine using the default
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.013|                      terminal this write is very likely inert - it is
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.014|                      kept because OPTY cannot know which host it was
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.015|                      launched under.
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.016|
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.017|  Cible           : HKCU\Console /v VirtualTerminalLevel /t REG_DWORD
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.018|                    (OPTY.bat line 90, run unconditionally at startup
::X|FR|reg.console.vt.019|                    before the ESC probe)
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.001|COLOURED CONSOLE OUTPUT (VIRTUALTERMINALLEVEL)
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.002|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.003|  What it is      : A single DWORD under HKCU\Console that tells the
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.004|                    legacy console host whether to interpret ANSI/VT
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.005|                    escape sequences or print them as literal characters.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.006|                    It is purely about how text is drawn on screen.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.007|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.008|  Actual effect   : Written for the current user account, so it applies to
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.009|                    every CMD window that user opens, not just OPTY. OPTY
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.010|                    writes it at line 90, before it probes for the ESC
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.011|                    character, and never removes it afterwards.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.012|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.013|  Gain            : OPTY's own output becomes readable and colour-coded
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.014|                    instead of a wall of escape codes. There is no
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.015|                    performance gain of any kind - not CPU, not memory,
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.016|                    not latency. On Windows 11 with the default Windows
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.017|                    Terminal, ANSI already works and this write changes
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.018|                    nothing visible at all.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.019|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.020|  Cost            : A very old console program that emitted raw ESC bytes
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.021|                    as data rather than as formatting could render oddly
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.022|                    in a CMD window afterwards. In practice, nothing. The
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.023|                    value persists after OPTY exits.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.024|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.025|  Windows default : Value absent, so VT interpretation is off in legacy
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.026|                    conhost.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.027|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.029|    1                    : conhost.exe parses ESC[ ... sequences in the
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.030|                           output stream and acts on them: colour
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.031|                           attributes, cursor moves, erase-line. This is
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.032|                           the flag Microsoft added in Windows 10 1511 so
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.033|                           classic CMD could do ANSI at all. Costs nothing
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.034|                           measurable - it is one extra state machine in
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.035|                           the console text parser.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.036|    0                    : conhost prints the escape bytes as literal
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.037|                           characters, so OPTY's output arrives as lines
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.038|                           starting with left-arrow[1;92m. Note this is
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.039|                           behaviourally the same as the value being
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.040|                           absent - 0 is an explicitly written 'off', not
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.041|                           the shipped state.
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.042|    DELETE               : The shipped state: the value does not exist and
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.043|                           legacy conhost does not interpret VT. Windows
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.044|                           Terminal - the default terminal application on
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.045|                           Windows 11 - interprets ANSI on its own and
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.046|                           never consults this value, so on a stock
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.047|                           Windows 11 the deletion changes nothing you can
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.048|                           see.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical answers, and that is the honest
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.002|                       table: this is a cosmetic display flag with no
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.003|                       performance dimension, so gaming, server, office
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.004|                       and laptop have nothing to distinguish them - all
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.005|                       four want readable output. The only real
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.006|                       alternative is profile 5, which exists for someone
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.007|                       who wants a strictly untouched console profile.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.008|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.009|  Unverified      : Whether the current session actually goes through
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.010|                    conhost or Windows Terminal is not detected by the
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.011|                    script, so on a Windows 11 machine using the default
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.012|                    terminal this write is very likely inert - it is kept
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.013|                    because OPTY cannot know which host it was launched
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.014|                    under.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.015|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.016|  Target          : HKCU\Console /v VirtualTerminalLevel /t REG_DWORD
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.017|                    (OPTY.bat line 90, run unconditionally at startup
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.018|                    before the ESC probe)
