:: ---- reg.menuanimate (preference) -----------------------------------
::P|reg.menuanimate|0|0|1|1|1|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.001|ANIMATIONS DES MENUS ET DES FENETRES
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.002|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.003|  Ce que c est    : MenuAnimate est une chaine « 0 » ou « 1 » sous
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.004|                    HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop qui gere le fondu-
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.005|                    glissement a l'ouverture et a la fermeture des menus
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.006|                    et des fenetres, pour l'utilisateur courant.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.007|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.008|  Effet reel      : « 0 » fait apparaitre les menus instantanement au lieu
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.009|                    d'une animation d'environ 200 ms. Rien n'est desactive
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.010|                    ni decharge : le compositeur tourne toujours, il saute
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.011|                    simplement cette transition. Comme OPTY ecrit le
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.012|                    registre sans prevenir l'Explorateur, l'effet arrive a
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.013|                    la prochaine ouverture de session.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.014|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.015|  Gain            : L'interface parait plus vive parce qu'on n'attend plus
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.016|                    les images d'animation. C'est un gain de latence
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.017|                    ressentie : reel, mais subjectif. Il ne libere ni
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.018|                    processeur, ni GPU, ni memoire de facon mesurable sur
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.019|                    une machine des dix dernieres annees. En revanche, en
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.020|                    Bureau a distance, c'est un vrai gain de bande
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.021|                    passante : les images d'animation sont des pixels qui
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.022|                    doivent traverser le reseau.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.023|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.024|  Cout            : Le bureau parait sec. Certains trouvent les menus
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.025|                    instantanes plus agressifs a l'usage. Rien ne casse.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.026|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.027|  Defaut Windows  : « 1 », animations activees.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.028|
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.030|    1                    : Les menus et fenetres apparaissent en fondu-
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.031|                           glissement sur environ 200 ms. Les images sont
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.032|                           dessinees par DWM sur le GPU ; sur n'importe
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.033|                           quel GPU des dix dernieres annees le cout est
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.034|                           negligeable, et le delai ressenti vient de la
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.035|                           duree de l'animation elle-meme, pas d'un manque
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.036|                           de puissance.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.037|    0                    : La transition est sautee : le menu est dessine
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.038|                           dans son etat final des la premiere image. Rien
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.039|                           n'est decharge, DWM compose toujours le bureau.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.040|                           Le seul gain, ce sont les ~200 ms d'attente.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Le profil jeu choisit 0 pour la sensation, et
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.002|                         uniquement pour ca : il n'y a aucune histoire de
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.003|                         FPS ici et il ne faut pas la vendre comme telle.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.004|                         Le profil serveur choisit 0 pour une raison
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.005|                         mecanique : une machine sans ecran se pilote en
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.006|                         RDP, et chaque image d'animation, c'est de la
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.007|                         bande passante. Bureautique et portable gardent
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.008|                         1, l'animation ne coute rien qui vaille d'etre
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.009|                         recupere et la machine doit avoir l'air finie.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.010|                         Windows livre 1.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.011|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.012|  Non verifie (en)  : OPTY writes the value with reg add and does not
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.013|                      broadcast SPI_SETMENUANIMATION, so a running
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.014|                      Explorer session keeps the old behaviour until the
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.015|                      next sign-out and sign-in. The menu says the change
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.016|                      is applied; strictly it is applied at the next
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.017|                      logon.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.018|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.019|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v MenuAnimate /t REG_SZ /d
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.020|                    "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 481-482, the -ani / +ani
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.021|                    menu toggles)
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.001|MENU AND WINDOW ANIMATIONS
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.002|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.003|  What it is      : MenuAnimate is a REG_SZ '0' or '1' under HKCU\Control
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.004|                    Panel\Desktop that controls the fade-and-slide
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.005|                    transition when menus and windows open and close, for
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.006|                    the current user.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.007|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.008|  Actual effect   : '0' makes menus appear instantly instead of animating
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.009|                    in over roughly 200 ms. Nothing is disabled or
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.010|                    unloaded - the compositor still runs, it simply skips
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.011|                    that one transition. Because OPTY writes the registry
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.012|                    without notifying Explorer, expect it to take effect
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.013|                    at the next sign-in.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.014|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.015|  Gain            : The interface feels snappier because you stop waiting
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.016|                    on animation frames. That is a perceived-latency gain:
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.017|                    real, but subjective. It frees no CPU, no GPU and no
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.018|                    RAM you could measure on any machine built in the last
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.019|                    decade. Over a Remote Desktop session it is a genuine
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.020|                    bandwidth gain, because the animation frames are
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.021|                    pixels that have to cross the wire.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.022|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.023|  Cost            : The desktop looks abrupt. Some people find instant
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.024|                    menus harsher to use. Nothing breaks.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.025|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.026|  Windows default : '1' - animations on.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.027|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.029|    1                    : Menus and windows fade and slide in over
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.030|                           roughly 200 ms. The frames are drawn by DWM on
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.031|                           the GPU; on any GPU of the last decade the cost
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.032|                           is a rounding error, and the delay you feel is
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.033|                           the animation duration itself, not a lack of
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.034|                           horsepower.
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.035|    0                    : The transition is skipped: the menu is drawn in
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.036|                           its final state on the first frame. Nothing is
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.037|                           unloaded, DWM still composites the desktop. The
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.038|                           only thing you get back is the ~200 ms you were
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.039|                           waiting on.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.001|  Why these profiles : Gaming picks 0 for the feel, and only for the feel
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.002|                       - there is no frame-rate story here and it should
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.003|                       not be sold as one. Server picks 0 for a mechanical
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.004|                       reason: a headless box is driven over RDP, and
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.005|                       every animation frame is bandwidth on the wire.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.006|                       Office and laptop keep 1 because the animation
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.007|                       costs nothing worth reclaiming and the machine
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.008|                       should look finished. Windows ships 1.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.009|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.010|  Unverified      : OPTY writes the value with reg add and does not
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.011|                    broadcast SPI_SETMENUANIMATION, so a running Explorer
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.012|                    session keeps the old behaviour until the next sign-
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.013|                    out and sign-in. The menu says the change is applied;
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.014|                    strictly it is applied at the next logon.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.015|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.016|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v MenuAnimate /t REG_SZ /d
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.017|                    "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 481-482, the -ani / +ani
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.018|                    menu toggles)
::
:: ---- reg.dragfullwindows (preference) -------------------------------
::P|reg.dragfullwindows|1|0|1|1|1|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.001|CONTENU DES FENETRES PENDANT LE DEPLACEMENT
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.002|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.003|  Ce que c est    : DragFullWindows, une chaine sous HKCU\Control
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.004|                    Panel\Desktop, determine si vous voyez le contenu reel
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.005|                    d'une fenetre pendant que vous la deplacez, ou
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.006|                    seulement un cadre qui se remplit au relachement.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.007|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.008|  Effet reel      : « 0 » fait dessiner un simple contour pendant le
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.009|                    deplacement au lieu de deplacer la surface composee de
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.010|                    la fenetre a chaque position de la souris.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.011|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.012|  Gain            : Sur du materiel moderne, aucun gain mesurable. Le
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.013|                    deplacement de fenetre est compose par le GPU depuis
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.014|                    Vista. Le seul endroit ou le mecanisme reste reel,
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.015|                    c'est un chemin d'affichage sans aucune acceleration :
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.016|                    RDP sur lien lent, ou machine virtuelle avec carte
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.017|                    d'affichage basique, ou le contour represente une
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.018|                    fraction des pixels.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.019|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.020|  Cout            : Le deplacement devient visuellement grossier : vous ne
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.021|                    voyez pas ce que vous positionnez avant de lacher. Les
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.022|                    dispositions Snap, les apercus et Aero Snap ne sont
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.023|                    pas affectes.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.024|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.025|  Defaut Windows  : « 1 », contenu affiche pendant le deplacement.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.026|
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.028|    1                    : La fenetre est redessinee a chaque position de
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.029|                           la souris pendant le deplacement. Depuis Vista,
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.030|                           ce redessin est un deplacement de surface DWM
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.031|                           sur le GPU, pas un redessin applicatif : le
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.032|                           cout par image sur du materiel reel n'est pas
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.033|                           mesurable.
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.034|    0                    : Windows dessine un simple cadre pendant le
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.035|                           deplacement et ne repeint la fenetre qu'au
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.036|                           relachement. Il envoie beaucoup moins de
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.037|                           pixels, ce qui compte encore sur un chemin
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.038|                           d'affichage sans GPU : Bureau a distance,
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.039|                           machine virtuelle avec carte d'affichage
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.040|                           basique, ou PC tournant sur le pilote
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.041|                           d'affichage generique Microsoft faute de vrai
::T|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.042|                           pilote.
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Trois profils repondent 1 : sur une machine avec
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.002|                         un vrai GPU, ce reglage n'apporte rien et vous
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.003|                         prive de voir ce que vous positionnez. Le serveur
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.004|                         est l'exception, avec un mecanisme derriere : on
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.005|                         s'en sert en RDP, ou le contour represente
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.006|                         vraiment moins de donnees a transmettre. Ce n'est
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.007|                         pas un tweak gaming et il ne faut pas le
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.008|                         presenter comme tel.
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.009|
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.010|  Non verifie (en)  : Same caveat as MenuAnimate: OPTY writes the value
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.011|                      without a SystemParametersInfo broadcast, so the
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.012|                      running session keeps the old behaviour until the
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.013|                      next sign-in.
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.014|
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.015|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v DragFullWindows /t
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.016|                    REG_SZ /d "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 483-484, the -mov
::X|FR|reg.dragfullwindows.017|                    / +mov menu toggles)
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.001|WINDOW CONTENTS WHILE DRAGGING
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.002|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.003|  What it is      : DragFullWindows, a REG_SZ under HKCU\Control
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.004|                    Panel\Desktop, decides whether you see a window's
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.005|                    actual contents while you drag it, or only a
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.006|                    rectangular outline that fills in when you let go.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.007|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.008|  Actual effect   : '0' makes Windows draw an outline during the drag
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.009|                    instead of moving the composited window surface at
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.010|                    every mouse position.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.011|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.012|  Gain            : On modern hardware, none you can measure. Window
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.013|                    dragging has been GPU-composited since Vista. The one
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.014|                    place the mechanism is still real is a display path
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.015|                    with no GPU acceleration at all - RDP over a slow
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.016|                    link, or a VM on a basic display adapter - where the
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.017|                    outline is a fraction of the pixels.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.018|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.019|  Cost            : Dragging becomes visually crude: you cannot see what
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.020|                    you are positioning until you release. Snap layouts,
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.021|                    window previews and Aero Snap are unaffected.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.022|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.023|  Windows default : '1' - contents shown while dragging.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.024|
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.025|  Possible values:
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.026|    1                    : The window is repainted at every mouse position
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.027|                           while you drag it. Since Vista this repaint is
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.028|                           a DWM surface move on the GPU, not an
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.029|                           application redraw, so the per-frame cost on
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.030|                           real hardware is not measurable.
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.031|    0                    : Windows draws a rectangular outline during the
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.032|                           drag and paints the real window only when you
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.033|                           release the button. It sends far fewer pixels,
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.034|                           which is why it still matters on a display path
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.035|                           that has no GPU: Remote Desktop, a VM on the
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.036|                           basic display adapter, or a machine running the
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.037|                           Microsoft Basic Display driver because no real
::T|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.038|                           driver installed.
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.001|  Why these profiles : Three profiles say 1 because on a machine with a
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.002|                       real GPU this setting buys nothing and costs you
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.003|                       the ability to see what you are positioning. Server
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.004|                       is the exception with a mechanism behind it: a
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.005|                       headless box is used over RDP, where the outline
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.006|                       genuinely means less to send. This is not a gaming
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.007|                       tweak and should not be presented as one.
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.008|
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.009|  Unverified      : Same caveat as MenuAnimate: OPTY writes the value
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.010|                    without a SystemParametersInfo broadcast, so the
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.011|                    running session keeps the old behaviour until the next
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.012|                    sign-in.
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.013|
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.014|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v DragFullWindows /t
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.015|                    REG_SZ /d "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 483-484, the -mov
::X|EN|reg.dragfullwindows.016|                    / +mov menu toggles)
::
:: ---- game.mode.on (repair) --------------------------------------
::P|game.mode.on|1|SKIP|1|1|1|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.001|MODE JEU
::T|FR|game.mode.on.002|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.003|  Ce que c est    : Le Mode Jeu est la fonction de Windows qui reconnait
::T|FR|game.mode.on.004|                    un jeu au premier plan et demande a l'ordonnanceur de
::T|FR|game.mode.on.005|                    retenir le travail de fond differable pendant que vous
::T|FR|game.mode.on.006|                    jouez. Il se trouve dans Parametres > Jeux > Mode Jeu
::T|FR|game.mode.on.007|                    et se stocke par utilisateur sous
::T|FR|game.mode.on.008|                    HKCU\Software\Microsoft\GameBar.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.009|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit AutoGameModeEnabled=1 et AllowAutoGameMode=1
::T|FR|game.mode.on.011|                    pour l'utilisateur courant, c'est-a-dire exactement
::T|FR|game.mode.on.012|                    l'interrupteur des Parametres sur ACTIVE. Aucune cle
::T|FR|game.mode.on.013|                    de strategie n'est utilisee : les Parametres restent
::T|FR|game.mode.on.014|                    modifiables et n'affichent aucune banniere « gere par
::T|FR|game.mode.on.015|                    votre organisation ».
::T|FR|game.mode.on.016|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.017|  Gain            : Le gain de FPS mesure est modeste et irregulier : sur
::T|FR|game.mode.on.018|                    un fixe bien equipe, on ne voit souvent aucune
::T|FR|game.mode.on.019|                    difference, et quiconque vous promet des images ici
::T|FR|game.mode.on.020|                    devine. Ce qu'il fait vraiment, c'est empecher une
::T|FR|game.mode.on.021|                    mise a jour Windows ou un installateur de fond de
::T|FR|game.mode.on.022|                    faire saccader la partie - ce qui compte surtout sur
::T|FR|game.mode.on.023|                    portable et sur materiel modeste, la ou il y a le
::T|FR|game.mode.on.024|                    moins de marge pour absorber ca.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.025|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.026|  Cout            : Pratiquement aucun. Historiquement quelques jeux et
::T|FR|game.mode.on.027|                    outils de capture se comportaient mal avec le Mode Jeu
::T|FR|game.mode.on.028|                    - c'est bien pour ca que l'interrupteur existe - mais
::T|FR|game.mode.on.029|                    ce n'est plus un probleme courant depuis des annees.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.030|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.031|  Defaut Windows  : AutoGameModeEnabled=1. Le Mode Jeu est active
::T|FR|game.mode.on.032|                    d'origine sur Windows 11 : sur la plupart des
::T|FR|game.mode.on.033|                    machines, cette ecriture affichera « deja correct ».
::T|FR|game.mode.on.034|
::T|FR|game.mode.on.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|game.mode.on.036|    1                    : AutoGameModeEnabled=1 correspond a
::T|FR|game.mode.on.037|                           l'interrupteur Parametres > Jeux > Mode Jeu sur
::T|FR|game.mode.on.038|                           ACTIVE. Quand un processus est reconnu comme un
::T|FR|game.mode.on.039|                           jeu et passe au premier plan, le systeme
::T|FR|game.mode.on.040|                           retient le travail de fond differable :
::T|FR|game.mode.on.041|                           installations de Windows Update, de pilotes,
::T|FR|game.mode.on.042|                           taches d'arriere-plan des applis empaquetees.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.043|                           Il ne monte pas la priorite des threads du jeu
::T|FR|game.mode.on.044|                           au-dela du boost de premier plan habituel.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.045|                           AllowAutoGameMode=1 est le drapeau
::T|FR|game.mode.on.046|                           d'autorisation qui l'accompagne.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.047|    0                    : L'interrupteur sur DESACTIVE. Les installations
::T|FR|game.mode.on.048|                           et taches de fond sont planifiees normalement
::T|FR|game.mode.on.049|                           pendant que vous jouez, ce qui est exactement
::T|FR|game.mode.on.050|                           la source des saccades en pleine partie qu'on
::T|FR|game.mode.on.051|                           attribue au GPU. Rien d'autre ne change dans
::T|FR|game.mode.on.052|                           l'ordonnancement du jeu.
::T|FR|game.mode.on.053|    SKIP                 : Laisse les deux valeurs intactes. C'est le bon
::T|FR|game.mode.on.054|                           choix sur une machine qui n'a jamais de jeu au
::T|FR|game.mode.on.055|                           premier plan : le Mode Jeu ne s'arme que
::T|FR|game.mode.on.056|                           lorsque le shell reconnait une fenetre de jeu
::T|FR|game.mode.on.057|                           active - sur une machine sans session
::T|FR|game.mode.on.058|                           graphique, le reglage n'a rien sur quoi agir.
::X|FR|game.mode.on.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre profils ecrivent 1, et ce n'est pas de la
::X|FR|game.mode.on.002|                         paresse : 1 est le defaut livre, c'est gratuit,
::X|FR|game.mode.on.003|                         et aucun usage n'a interet a l'avoir coupe. C'est
::X|FR|game.mode.on.004|                         sur portable qu'il rapporte le plus, une machine
::X|FR|game.mode.on.005|                         limitee thermiquement ayant le moins de marge
::X|FR|game.mode.on.006|                         pour encaisser un installateur en arriere-plan.
::X|FR|game.mode.on.007|                         Le serveur est un vrai SKIP : le Mode Jeu ne
::X|FR|game.mode.on.008|                         s'engage que derriere un jeu reconnu au premier
::X|FR|game.mode.on.009|                         plan, donc sur un hote sans session il n'y a rien
::X|FR|game.mode.on.010|                         a engager et ecrire une valeur serait du theatre.
::X|FR|game.mode.on.011|
::X|FR|game.mode.on.012|  Non verifie (en)  : AllowAutoGameMode is often absent on a fresh profile
::X|FR|game.mode.on.013|                      and its absence appears to behave as 'allowed'; its
::X|FR|game.mode.on.014|                      exact shipped state could not be confirmed.
::X|FR|game.mode.on.015|                      AutoGameModeEnabled=1 is confirmed as the Windows 11
::X|FR|game.mode.on.016|                      default, which makes this a re-assert rather than a
::X|FR|game.mode.on.017|                      change on most machines.
::X|FR|game.mode.on.018|
::X|FR|game.mode.on.019|  Cible           : HKCU\Software\Microsoft\GameBar /v AutoGameModeEnabled
::X|FR|game.mode.on.020|                    /t REG_DWORD and /v AllowAutoGameMode /t REG_DWORD
::X|FR|game.mode.on.021|                    (OPTY.bat lines 1513-1514, :gaming_perf)
::T|EN|game.mode.on.001|GAME MODE
::T|EN|game.mode.on.002|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.003|  What it is      : Game Mode is the Windows feature that recognises a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.004|                    game in the foreground and tells the scheduler to hold
::T|EN|game.mode.on.005|                    back deferrable background work while you play. It
::T|EN|game.mode.on.006|                    lives in Settings > Gaming > Game Mode and is stored
::T|EN|game.mode.on.007|                    per user under HKCU\Software\Microsoft\GameBar.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.008|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.009|  Actual effect   : Writes AutoGameModeEnabled=1 and AllowAutoGameMode=1
::T|EN|game.mode.on.010|                    for the current user, which is exactly the Settings
::T|EN|game.mode.on.011|                    toggle in the ON position. No policy key is involved,
::T|EN|game.mode.on.012|                    so Settings stays editable and shows no 'managed by
::T|EN|game.mode.on.013|                    your organisation' banner.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.014|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.015|  Gain            : Measured FPS benefit is modest and inconsistent - on a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.016|                    well-specced desktop you will often see no difference
::T|EN|game.mode.on.017|                    at all, and anyone promising you frames from this is
::T|EN|game.mode.on.018|                    guessing. What it genuinely does is stop a Windows
::T|EN|game.mode.on.019|                    Update or a background installer from stuttering your
::T|EN|game.mode.on.020|                    session, which matters most on laptops and lower-end
::T|EN|game.mode.on.021|                    hardware where there is less headroom to absorb it.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.022|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.023|  Cost            : Practically none. Historically a handful of games and
::T|EN|game.mode.on.024|                    capture tools misbehaved with Game Mode - that is why
::T|EN|game.mode.on.025|                    the toggle exists at all - but that has not been a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.026|                    common problem for years.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.027|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.028|  Windows default : AutoGameModeEnabled=1. Game Mode ships enabled on
::T|EN|game.mode.on.029|                    Windows 11, so on most machines this write reports
::T|EN|game.mode.on.030|                    'was already correct'.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.031|
::T|EN|game.mode.on.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|game.mode.on.033|    1                    : AutoGameModeEnabled=1 is the Settings > Gaming
::T|EN|game.mode.on.034|                           > Game Mode toggle in the ON position. When a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.035|                           process is recognised as a game and takes the
::T|EN|game.mode.on.036|                           foreground, the OS holds back deferrable
::T|EN|game.mode.on.037|                           background work: Windows Update installs,
::T|EN|game.mode.on.038|                           driver installs, background task activity for
::T|EN|game.mode.on.039|                           packaged apps. It does not raise the game's
::T|EN|game.mode.on.040|                           thread priority beyond what the normal
::T|EN|game.mode.on.041|                           foreground boost already does.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.042|                           AllowAutoGameMode=1 is the companion permission
::T|EN|game.mode.on.043|                           flag.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.044|    0                    : The toggle OFF. Background installs and
::T|EN|game.mode.on.045|                           background tasks are scheduled as usual while
::T|EN|game.mode.on.046|                           you play, which is precisely the source of the
::T|EN|game.mode.on.047|                           mid-match stutter people blame on their GPU.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.048|                           Nothing else about the game's scheduling
::T|EN|game.mode.on.049|                           changes.
::T|EN|game.mode.on.050|    SKIP                 : Leaves both values untouched. Correct on a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.051|                           machine that never has a game in the foreground
::T|EN|game.mode.on.052|                           - Game Mode only ever arms when the shell
::T|EN|game.mode.on.053|                           recognises a foreground game window, so on a
::T|EN|game.mode.on.054|                           headless box the setting has nothing to act on
::T|EN|game.mode.on.055|                           either way.
::X|EN|game.mode.on.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles write 1 and that is not laziness: 1
::X|EN|game.mode.on.002|                       is the shipped default, it is free, and there is no
::X|EN|game.mode.on.003|                       workload where having it on is a liability. Laptop
::X|EN|game.mode.on.004|                       is where it earns the most, because a thermally
::X|EN|game.mode.on.005|                       limited machine has the least headroom to absorb a
::X|EN|game.mode.on.006|                       background installer. Server is a true SKIP - Game
::X|EN|game.mode.on.007|                       Mode only engages behind a recognised foreground
::X|EN|game.mode.on.008|                       game, so on a headless host there is nothing to
::X|EN|game.mode.on.009|                       engage and writing a value would be theatre.
::X|EN|game.mode.on.010|
::X|EN|game.mode.on.011|  Unverified      : AllowAutoGameMode is often absent on a fresh profile
::X|EN|game.mode.on.012|                    and its absence appears to behave as 'allowed'; its
::X|EN|game.mode.on.013|                    exact shipped state could not be confirmed.
::X|EN|game.mode.on.014|                    AutoGameModeEnabled=1 is confirmed as the Windows 11
::X|EN|game.mode.on.015|                    default, which makes this a re-assert rather than a
::X|EN|game.mode.on.016|                    change on most machines.
::X|EN|game.mode.on.017|
::X|EN|game.mode.on.018|  Target          : HKCU\Software\Microsoft\GameBar /v AutoGameModeEnabled
::X|EN|game.mode.on.019|                    /t REG_DWORD and /v AllowAutoGameMode /t REG_DWORD
::X|EN|game.mode.on.020|                    (OPTY.bat lines 1513-1514, :gaming_perf)
