:: ---- gr.letapps.bg.del (repair) ---------------------------------
::P|gr.letapps.bg.del|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.001|RETIRER LA STRATEGIE FORCE DENY SUR L'ARRIERE-PLAN
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.002|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.003|  Ce que c est    : LetAppsRunInBackground est une strategie a portee
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.004|                    machine, sous HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies, qui ecrase le
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.005|                    choix de chaque utilisateur concernant l'execution en
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.006|                    arriere-plan des applis empaquetees. Contrairement a
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.007|                    beaucoup de valeurs de strategie, elle n'est pas
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.008|                    reservee a une edition : elle s'applique sur Famille
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.009|                    et Pro, pas seulement sur Entreprise.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.010|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.011|  Effet reel      : La supprime : la page Parametres redevient modifiable
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.012|                    et n'affiche plus « gere par votre organisation ».
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.013|                    L'autorisation par application de chaque utilisateur
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.014|                    reprend son role normal.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.015|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.016|  Gain            : Fait disparaitre un grisage qui fait croire que le PC
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.017|                    personnel est verrouille par un tiers, et rend le
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.018|                    controle appli par appli. Sur une machine ou la
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.019|                    strategie n'a jamais ete posee, cette etape ne signale
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.020|                    rien et ne change rien - autant savoir dans quel cas
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.021|                    on est.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.022|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.023|  Cout            : Rien sur une machine personnelle. Sur un PC reellement
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.024|                    gere par une entreprise, la supprimer revient a lutter
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.025|                    contre l'outil de gestion, qui la reecrira au prochain
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.026|                    rafraichissement. Le controle :ismanaged d'OPTY existe
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.027|                    justement pour ne pas toucher aux cles de strategie
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.028|                    sur une machine jointe a un domaine ou inscrite en
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.029|                    MDM.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.030|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.031|  Defaut Windows  : Absente.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.032|
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.034|    DELETE               : Supprime la strategie. La page Parametres
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.035|                           redevient modifiable, la banniere « gere par
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.036|                           votre organisation » disparait, et le choix par
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.037|                           application de chaque utilisateur reprend
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.038|                           effet. C'est l'etat livre sur toute machine non
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.039|                           geree de facon centralisee.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.040|    0                    : « L'utilisateur decide », au sens des
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.041|                           strategies - mais la valeur existe toujours,
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.042|                           donc le controle des Parametres peut rester
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.043|                           grise et la banniere « organisation »
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.044|                           persister. Ecrire 0 est l'erreur classique ici
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.045|                           : ca neutralise la strategie sans supprimer le
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.046|                           symptome dont les gens se plaignent.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.047|    1                    : Autorisation forcee : toutes les applis
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.048|                           empaquetees peuvent tourner en arriere-plan et
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.049|                           aucun utilisateur ne peut le desactiver.
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.050|    2                    : Refus force : a l'echelle de la machine, aucune
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.051|                           appli empaquetee ne tourne en arriere-plan,
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.052|                           aucun utilisateur ne peut passer outre, et
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.053|                           l'interrupteur par application est grise sous
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.054|                           la banniere « organisation ». C'est ce qu'OPTY
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.055|                           ecrivait autrefois et a volontairement retire :
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.056|                           c'est exactement l'etat que cette etape
::T|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.057|                           nettoie.
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils la suppriment, et il n'y a
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.002|                         aucune distinction a inventer. Un ecrasement a
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.003|                         l'echelle machine qui masque le vrai reglage est
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.004|                         mauvais sur un PC de jeu, un hote, un PC familial
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.005|                         comme sur un portable - le choix par utilisateur
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.006|                         appartient aux deux autres fiches, pas a une cle
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.007|                         de strategie qui grise l'interface.
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.008|
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.009|  Problemes connus : Avec la strategie a 2, Parametres > Applications >
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.010|                     Applications installees > Options avancees affiche la
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.011|                     liste d'autorisation d'arriere-plan grisee avec la
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.012|                     banniere « Certains parametres sont geres par votre
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.013|                     organisation », sur une machine sans aucune
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.014|                     organisation derriere.
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.015|
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.016|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\AppPrivacy /v
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.017|                    LetAppsRunInBackground - reg delete (OPTY.bat line
::X|FR|gr.letapps.bg.del.018|                    1618, :gaming_restore)
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.001|REMOVE THE BACKGROUND-APPS FORCE DENY POLICY
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.002|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.003|  What it is      : LetAppsRunInBackground is a machine-scope policy under
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies that overrides every user's own
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.005|                    choice about packaged-app background execution. Unlike
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.006|                    many policy values it is not edition-gated, so it
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.007|                    takes effect on Home and Pro, not only Enterprise.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.008|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes it, so the Settings page is editable again and
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.010|                    stops saying it is managed by your organization. Each
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.011|                    user's per-app permission then applies as normal.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.012|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.013|  Gain            : Removes a grey-out that makes people believe their
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.014|                    personal PC is locked down by someone else, and gives
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.015|                    back per-app control. On a machine where the policy
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.016|                    was never set, this reports nothing and changes
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.017|                    nothing - be honest about which of the two you are on.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.018|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.019|  Cost            : Nothing on a personal machine. On a genuinely company-
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.020|                    managed PC, deleting it fights the management system,
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.021|                    which will usually just write it back at the next
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.022|                    policy refresh. OPTY's :ismanaged check exists
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.023|                    precisely to stay out of policy keys on domain-joined
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.024|                    or MDM-enrolled machines.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.025|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.026|  Windows default : Absent.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.027|
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.029|    DELETE               : Removes the policy. The Settings page becomes
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.030|                           editable again, the 'managed by your
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.031|                           organization' banner disappears, and each
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.032|                           user's own per-app choice takes effect. This is
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.033|                           the shipped state on any machine that is not
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.034|                           centrally managed.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.035|    0                    : 'User is in control' in policy terms - but the
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.036|                           value still exists, so the Settings control can
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.037|                           stay greyed out and the organisation banner can
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.038|                           remain. Writing 0 is the classic mistake here:
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.039|                           it neutralises the policy without removing the
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.040|                           symptom people actually complain about.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.041|    1                    : Force Allow: every packaged app may run in the
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.042|                           background and no user can turn it off.
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.043|    2                    : Force Deny: machine-wide, no packaged app may
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.044|                           run in the background, no user can override it,
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.045|                           and the per-app toggle is greyed out under the
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.046|                           organisation banner. This is what OPTY used to
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.047|                           write and deliberately removed - it is the
::T|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.048|                           exact state this step exists to clean up.
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete it, and there is no distinction to
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.002|                       invent. A machine-wide override that hides the real
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.003|                       control is wrong on a gaming rig, a host box, a
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.004|                       family PC and a laptop alike - the per-user choice
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.005|                       belongs on the other two cards, not in a policy key
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.006|                       that greys out the UI.
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.007|
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.008|  Known problems  : With the policy at 2, Settings > Apps > Installed apps
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.009|                    > Advanced options shows the background permission
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.010|                    dropdown greyed out with the 'Some settings are
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.011|                    managed by your organization' banner, on a machine
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.012|                    with no organization behind it.
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.013|
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.014|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\AppPrivacy /v
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.015|                    LetAppsRunInBackground - reg delete (OPTY.bat line
::X|EN|gr.letapps.bg.del.016|                    1618, :gaming_restore)
::
:: ---- rad.maintenance.killswitch (repair) ------------------------
::P|rad.maintenance.killswitch|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.001|REACTIVER LA MAINTENANCE AUTOMATIQUE
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.002|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.003|  Ce que c est    : Un seul DWORD qui coupe en silence toute la fenetre de
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.004|                    maintenance automatique. C'est un favori des scripts
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.005|                    de debloat parce qu'il fait paraitre une machine
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.006|                    calme, et c'est l'un des tres rares reglages de
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.007|                    Windows sans aucune representation dans l'interface.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.008|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.009|  Effet reel      : Supprime la valeur si elle est presente : TRIM ou
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.010|                    defragmentation planifies, chkdsk proactif, compactage
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.011|                    de l'index et nettoyage WinSxS peuvent a nouveau
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.012|                    s'executer pendant l'inactivite. La suppression est la
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.013|                    bonne restauration ici, puisque le defaut livre est
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.014|                    l'absence de la valeur.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.015|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.016|  Gain            : Rien sur une machine ou elle n'a jamais ete posee - et
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.017|                    c'est le cas de la plupart des machines, donc
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.018|                    n'attendez pas de ligne dans le rapport. Sur une
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.019|                    machine ou un outil de tweak l'a posee, c'est la
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.020|                    valeur au plus fort effet de tout le script : des mois
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.021|                    de TRIM sautes, un index qui ne se compacte jamais et
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.022|                    un magasin WinSxS qui ne fait que grossir, pendant que
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.023|                    le Planificateur affiche « Pret » partout.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.024|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.025|  Cout            : La maintenance utilise le disque et le processeur
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.026|                    quand la machine est inactive, et s'arrete des que
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.027|                    vous y touchez. Sur un portable, ce travail
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.028|                    d'inactivite consomme reellement de la batterie ecran
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.029|                    ouvert - Windows restreint deja la fenetre sur
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.030|                    batterie.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.031|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.032|  Defaut Windows  : Absente, la maintenance tourne.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.033|
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.035|    DELETE               : L'etat livre. La fenetre de maintenance
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.036|                           automatique s'execute quand la machine est
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.037|                           inactive : defragmentation ou TRIM selon le
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.038|                           support, chkdsk ProactiveScan, compactage de
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.039|                           l'index de recherche, nettoyage des composants
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.040|                           WinSxS, et les taches d'entretien de la
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.041|                           restauration systeme et des mises a jour. Elle
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.042|                           se retire des que vous touchez le clavier.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.043|    1                    : Coupe toute la fenetre de maintenance. Les
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.044|                           taches individuelles ne sont PAS desactivees :
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.045|                           chacune affiche toujours « Pret » dans le
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.046|                           Planificateur de taches et son heure de
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.047|                           prochaine execution avance encore - c'est
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.048|                           simplement l'ordonnanceur de maintenance qui ne
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.049|                           les lance jamais. C'est cette combinaison qui
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.050|                           la rend invisible : aucune interface de Windows
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.051|                           ne la revele, et tous les diagnostics que vous
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.052|                           lanceriez ont l'air sains.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.053|    0                    : Maintenance activee, ecrite explicitement.
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.054|                           Comportement identique a la valeur absente. Ce
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.055|                           n'est pas faux, ca laisse simplement dans le
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.056|                           registre une valeur que le systeme n'a jamais
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.057|                           ecrite - d'ou la suppression plutot que
::T|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.058|                           l'ecriture de 0.
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques, et aucune facon honnete
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.002|                         de les differencier. TRIM, chkdsk et nettoyage de
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.003|                         composants ne sont pas affaire de gout : un SSD
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.004|                         de PC de jeu qui ne recoit jamais de TRIM se
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.005|                         degrade exactement comme celui d'un poste
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.006|                         bureautique, et une machine hote inactive la
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.007|                         majeure partie de la journee est le cas ideal
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.008|                         pour une fenetre de maintenance, pas un argument
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.009|                         contre.
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.010|
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.011|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.012|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Schedule\Maintenance /v
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.013|                    MaintenanceDisabled - via call :killkey (OPTY.bat line
::X|FR|rad.maintenance.killswitch.014|                    1760, :reassert_defaults)
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.001|RE-ENABLE AUTOMATIC MAINTENANCE
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.002|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.003|  What it is      : A single DWORD that silently switches off the whole
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.004|                    Automatic Maintenance window. It is a favourite of
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.005|                    debloat scripts because it makes a machine look quiet,
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.006|                    and it is one of the very few settings in Windows with
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.007|                    no GUI representation at all.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.008|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value if present, so scheduled TRIM or
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.010|                    defrag, proactive chkdsk, index compaction and WinSxS
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.011|                    cleanup are allowed to run again during idle time.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.012|                    Deleting is the correct restore here because the
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.013|                    shipped default is the value not existing.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.014|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.015|  Gain            : Nothing on a machine where it was never set - and that
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.016|                    is most machines, so do not expect a report line. On a
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.017|                    machine where a tweak tool set it, this is the
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.018|                    highest-leverage single value in the whole script:
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.019|                    months of skipped TRIM, an index that never compacts
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.020|                    and a WinSxS store that only ever grows, all while
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.021|                    Task Scheduler shows everything Ready.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.022|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.023|  Cost            : Maintenance uses the disk and CPU while the machine is
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.024|                    idle and backs off as soon as you touch it. On a
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.025|                    laptop, that idle work is real battery when the lid is
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.026|                    open and the machine is plugged in - Windows already
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.027|                    restricts the window on battery.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.028|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.029|  Windows default : Absent, maintenance runs.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.030|
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.032|    DELETE               : The shipped state. The Automatic Maintenance
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.033|                           window runs when the machine is idle: scheduled
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.034|                           defrag or TRIM depending on the media, chkdsk
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.035|                           ProactiveScan, search-index compaction, WinSxS
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.036|                           component cleanup, and the system-restore and
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.037|                           update housekeeping tasks. It yields the moment
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.038|                           you touch the keyboard.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.039|    1                    : Stops the entire maintenance window. The
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.040|                           individual tasks are NOT disabled - every one
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.041|                           of them still shows 'Ready' in Task Scheduler
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.042|                           and its next-run time still advances - the
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.043|                           maintenance scheduler simply never launches
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.044|                           them. That combination is why this is
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.045|                           invisible: there is no GUI anywhere in Windows
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.046|                           that reveals it, and every diagnostic you would
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.047|                           run looks healthy.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.048|    0                    : Maintenance enabled, written explicitly.
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.049|                           Behaviourally the same as the value being
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.050|                           absent. It is not wrong, it just leaves a value
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.051|                           in the registry that the OS never wrote, which
::T|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.052|                           is why this step deletes rather than writing 0.
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers and no honest way to
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.002|                       differentiate. TRIM, chkdsk and component cleanup
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.003|                       are not a taste - a gaming SSD that never gets TRIM
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.004|                       degrades exactly like an office one, and a host box
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.005|                       that is idle most of the day is the ideal case for
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.006|                       a maintenance window, not an argument against it.
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.007|
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.008|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.009|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Schedule\Maintenance /v
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.010|                    MaintenanceDisabled - via call :killkey (OPTY.bat line
::X|EN|rad.maintenance.killswitch.011|                    1760, :reassert_defaults)
::
:: ---- rad.spectre.restore (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.spectre.restore|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.001|REACTIVER LES MITIGATIONS SPECTRE ET MELTDOWN
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.002|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.003|  Ce que c est    : Deux DWORD sous Memory Management capables de couper
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.004|                    au demarrage les protections contre les canaux
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.005|                    auxiliaires du processeur. Aucune interface ne les
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.006|                    montre ; on lit l'etat reel avec Get-
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.007|                    SpeculationControlSettings ou un inspecteur tiers.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.008|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.009|  Effet reel      : Supprime les deux, mais uniquement si elles valent
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.010|                    exactement 3 et 3, le motif de desactivation connu. La
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.011|                    recette de durcissement 0/3 de Microsoft et toute
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.012|                    autre combinaison sont laissees intactes. Un
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.013|                    redemarrage est necessaire.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.014|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.015|  Gain            : Retablit la protection contre les attaques par
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.016|                    execution speculative, qui lisent la memoire au
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.017|                    travers des frontieres de processus et de machines
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.018|                    virtuelles. Sur une machine ou ces valeurs n'ont
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.019|                    jamais ete posees, l'etape ne fait rien et ne dit rien
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.020|                    : le gain n'existe que la ou un outil de tweak les
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.021|                    avait coupees.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.022|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.023|  Cout            : Sur d'anciens processeurs Intel, les couper valait
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.024|                    quelques pour cent sur les charges riches en appels
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.025|                    systeme et en E/S ; vous les rendez. Sur les
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.026|                    processeurs corriges materiellement - Intel a partir
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.027|                    de la 9e generation environ, AMD depuis Zen 2 pour les
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.028|                    variantes concernees - le cout mesurable est quasi
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.029|                    nul. Et dans les deux cas, ce n'est pas une histoire
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.030|                    de FPS.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.031|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.032|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs absentes, mitigations appliquees
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.033|                    selon ce qu'exige le processeur.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.034|
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.036|    DELETE (both values) : L'etat livre : aucune des deux valeurs n'existe
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.037|                           et le systeme applique les mitigations que le
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.038|                           processeur et le microcode reclament. C'est la
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.039|                           seule vraie restauration - aucun nombre ne
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.040|                           signifie « defaut ».
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.041|    3 / 3 (Override=3, Mask=3) :
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.042|        Le motif de desactivation connu, celui qu'ecrit chaque script «
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.043|        FPS tweak ». Le bit 0 desactive la mitigation contre l'injection
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.044|        de cible de branchement (Spectre v2), le bit 1 desactive
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.045|        l'isolation des tables de pages du noyau utilisee contre Meltdown
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.046|        ; le masque indique que les deux bits sont significatifs. C'est le
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.047|        SEUL motif sur lequel OPTY agit, et il agit parce qu'il est sans
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.048|        ambiguite.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.049|    0 / 3 (Override=0, Mask=3) :
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.050|        La recette officielle de Microsoft (KB4073119) pour forcer les
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.051|        mitigations ACTIVEES. C'est un troisieme etat, pas le defaut, et
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.052|        c'est un durcissement volontaire. OPTY n'y touche pas du tout : le
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.053|        supprimer annulerait en silence la decision de quelqu'un.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.054|    other combinations   : Il en existe beaucoup, pour un controle
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.055|                           variante par variante sur des charges serveur
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.056|                           et des scenarios d'hyperviseur. OPTY ne cherche
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.057|                           pas a les interpreter et n'y touche pas, ce qui
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.058|                           est le bon choix pour un outil qui ne peut pas
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.059|                           demander pourquoi elles sont la.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques. Aucun profil ne
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.002|                         justifie d'echanger quelques pour cent de debit
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.003|                         d'appels systeme contre une classe de fuite de
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.004|                         memoire noyau laissee ouverte sur une machine qui
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.005|                         ouvre un navigateur - et les cinq en ouvrent un.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.006|                         La vraie exception, c'est un banc de test isole
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.007|                         ne faisant tourner que le benchmark : ce n'est
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.008|                         pas l'un des cinq profils et il ne faut pas faire
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.009|                         semblant du contraire.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.010|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.011|  Cible           : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.012|                    Manager\Memory Management /v FeatureSettingsOverride
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.013|                    and /v FeatureSettingsOverrideMask - both deleted via
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.014|                    :killkey, and only when both read exactly 0x3
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.015|                    (OPTY.bat lines 1865-1877)
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.001|RE-ENABLE THE SPECTRE AND MELTDOWN MITIGATIONS
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.002|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.003|  What it is      : Two DWORDs under Memory Management that can switch off
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.004|                    the CPU side-channel mitigations at boot. They have no
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.005|                    GUI; the usual way to see the real state is Get-
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.006|                    SpeculationControlSettings or a third-party inspector.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.007|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes both, but only when they read exactly 3 and 3
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.009|                    - the known disable pattern. Microsoft's own 0/3
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.010|                    hardening recipe and any other combination are left
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.011|                    alone. A reboot is required before the change takes
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.012|                    effect.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.013|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.014|  Gain            : Restores protection against speculative-execution
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.015|                    attacks that read memory across process and VM
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.016|                    boundaries. On a machine where the values were never
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.017|                    set, this step does nothing and says nothing - the
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.018|                    gain only exists where a tweak tool disabled them.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.019|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.020|  Cost            : On older Intel CPUs, disabling the mitigations was
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.021|                    worth a few percent in syscall-heavy and I/O-heavy
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.022|                    work, and you give that back. On CPUs with the in-
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.023|                    silicon fixes - Intel from roughly 9th gen, and AMD
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.024|                    Zen 2 onward for the variants that apply - the
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.025|                    measurable cost is close to nothing, and it is not a
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.026|                    frame-rate story on either.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.027|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.028|  Windows default : Both values absent, mitigations applied as the CPU
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.029|                    requires.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.030|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.032|    DELETE (both values) : The shipped state: neither value exists and the
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.033|                           OS applies whatever mitigations the CPU and
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.034|                           microcode call for. This is the only true
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.035|                           restore - there is no single number that means
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.036|                           'default'.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.037|    3 / 3 (Override=3, Mask=3) :
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.038|        The known disable pattern, and the one every 'FPS tweak' script
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.039|        writes. Bit 0 disables the branch-target-injection mitigation
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.040|        (Spectre v2), bit 1 disables the kernel-page-table isolation used
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.041|        against Meltdown; the mask says both bits are meaningful. This is
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.042|        the ONLY pattern OPTY acts on, and it acts on it because it is
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.043|        unambiguous.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.044|    0 / 3 (Override=0, Mask=3) :
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.045|        Microsoft's own KB4073119 recipe to force the mitigations ENABLED.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.046|        It is a third state, not the default, and it is a deliberate
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.047|        hardening choice. OPTY leaves it strictly alone - deleting it
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.048|        would quietly undo somebody's decision.
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.049|    other combinations   : There are many, covering per-variant control on
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.050|                           server workloads and hypervisor scenarios. OPTY
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.051|                           does not attempt to interpret them and leaves
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.052|                           them untouched, which is the right call for a
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.053|                           tool that cannot ask why they are there.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers. There is no profile where a
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.002|                       few percent of syscall throughput is worth leaving
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.003|                       a kernel memory-disclosure class open on a machine
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.004|                       that opens a browser - and every one of these five
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.005|                       does. The genuine exception is an air-gapped
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.006|                       benchmark box running nothing but the benchmark,
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.007|                       which is not one of the five profiles and should
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.008|                       not be pretended into one.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.009|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.010|  Target          : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.011|                    Manager\Memory Management /v FeatureSettingsOverride
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.012|                    and /v FeatureSettingsOverrideMask - both deleted via
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.013|                    :killkey, and only when both read exactly 0x3
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.014|                    (OPTY.bat lines 1865-1877)
::
:: ---- rad.do.mode.killkey (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.do.mode.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|RETIRER UN MODE DELIVERY OPTIMIZATION CASSE
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : DODownloadMode est la strategie qui choisit comment
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.004|                    Windows recupere les mises a jour et le contenu du
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.005|                    Store : pair a pair, reseau local seul, HTTP simple,
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.006|                    ou contournement complet.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.007|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.008|  Effet reel      : Ne supprime la strategie que si elle vaut 100
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.009|                    (Bypass), mode deprecie par Microsoft et cause connue
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.010|                    de mises a jour qui ne se telechargent jamais. Un mode
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.011|                    0, 1, 2, 3 ou 99 (Simple : sans service cloud ni
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.012|                    partage) choisi deliberement reste intact. Note pour
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.013|                    le mainteneur : la ligne 1966 supprime aussi 0x63
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.014|                    aujourd'hui, ce qui contredit cette regle - elle doit
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.015|                    disparaitre.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.016|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.017|  Gain            : Sur une machine bloquee en mode 100, les mises a jour
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.018|                    se telechargent a nouveau. Sur toute autre machine,
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.019|                    l'etape ne fait rien et ne doit rien signaler.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.020|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.021|  Cout            : Aucun. Si vous vouliez couper le partage entre PC, les
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.022|                    modes 0 et 99 le font toujours, et aucun des deux
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.023|                    n'est touche.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.024|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.025|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur de strategie absente ; Delivery Optimization
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.026|                    retombe sur le partage en reseau local sur les
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.027|                    editions grand public.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.028|
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.030|    100 (0x64) - Bypass  : Contourne completement Delivery Optimization et
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.031|                           confie les telechargements a l'ancien mecanisme
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.032|                           BITS. Microsoft l'a deprecie, et sur 24H2 et
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.033|                           suivants c'est une cause documentee de mises a
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.034|                           jour qui restent en file sans jamais se
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.035|                           telecharger. C'est la SEULE valeur que cette
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.036|                           etape supprime.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.037|    99 (0x63) - Simple   : Un mode documente, pris en charge et
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.038|                           fonctionnel : les telechargements viennent
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.039|                           directement des serveurs Microsoft, sans
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.040|                           service cloud Delivery Optimization et sans
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.041|                           partage entre machines. C'est l'un des modes
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.042|                           que Microsoft recommande en remplacement du
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.043|                           Bypass deprecie. Le supprimer inverserait en
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.044|                           silence une configuration deliberee : il ne
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.045|                           faut donc PAS y toucher - c'est la correction
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.046|                           apportee a la version precedente de cette
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.047|                           fiche.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.048|    0 - HTTP only        : Aucun partage, simple HTTP depuis Microsoft,
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.049|                           mais le service Delivery Optimization gere
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.050|                           quand meme le transfert. C'est la valeur a
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.051|                           utiliser si ce que vous voulez est « aucun
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.052|                           partage avec d'autres PC » - pas 100.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.053|    1 - LAN peering      : Partage uniquement avec les autres PC du meme
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.054|                           reseau local. C'est le comportement effectif
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.055|                           par defaut sur une installation grand public.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.056|    2 - Group peering    : Partage au sein du meme site Active Directory
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.057|                           ou groupe de domaine. Concu pour des parcs
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.058|                           geres.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.059|    3 - Internet peering : Partage avec des PC sur Internet en plus du
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.060|                           reseau local. Utilise votre bande passante
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.061|                           montante pour servir des inconnus - c'est
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.062|                           generalement ce reglage que les gens cherchent
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.063|                           quand ils fouillent dans cette cle.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.064|    DELETE               : L'etat livre : aucune valeur de strategie, et
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.065|                           Delivery Optimization utilise son defaut
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.066|                           (partage sur le reseau local sur les editions
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.067|                           grand public).
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils ne suppriment que le 100. Un
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|                         mode deprecie qui empeche les mises a jour de
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.003|                         securite d'arriver est casse sur n'importe quel
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.004|                         type de machine, et aucun profil n'a de raison de
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.005|                         le vouloir. Tout le reste de cette cle est un
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.006|                         choix delibere de bande passante, et une etape de
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.007|                         reparation n'a pas a l'ecraser.
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.008|
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.009|  Problemes connus : Avec le mode 100 sur 24H2 ou plus recent, Windows
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.010|                     Update reste a 0 % ou signale un echec de
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.011|                     telechargement generique et reessaie indefiniment ;
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.012|                     les mises a jour d'applis du Store calent de la meme
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.013|                     facon. Rien dans l'interface de Windows Update ne
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.014|                     designe Delivery Optimization comme la cause.
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.015|
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.016|  Non verifie (en)  : The exact failure mode of DODownloadMode 100 on 24H2
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.017|                      and later is reported rather than measured here;
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.018|                      what is solid is that Microsoft documents mode 100
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.019|                      as deprecated.
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.020|
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.021|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DeliveryOptim
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.022|                    ization /v DODownloadMode - deleted via :killkey only
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.023|                    when the read value is 0x64 (OPTY.bat lines 1960-1966;
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.024|                    line 1966 currently also deletes 0x63 and should be
::X|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.025|                    removed)
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|REMOVE A BROKEN DELIVERY OPTIMIZATION MODE
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.003|  What it is      : DODownloadMode is the policy that selects how Windows
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.004|                    fetches updates and Store content: peer to peer, LAN
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.005|                    only, plain HTTP, or bypassed entirely.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.006|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes the policy only when it reads 100 (Bypass),
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.008|                    which Microsoft has deprecated and which is a known
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.009|                    cause of updates that never download. A deliberate
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.010|                    mode 0, 1, 2, 3 or 99 (Simple - no cloud service, no
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.011|                    peering) is left alone. Note for the maintainer: line
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.012|                    1966 currently also deletes 0x63 and contradicts this
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.013|                    rule - it should be removed.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.014|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.015|  Gain            : On a machine stuck on mode 100, updates start
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.016|                    downloading again. On any other machine this step is a
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.017|                    no-op and should report nothing.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.018|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.019|  Cost            : Nothing. If what you wanted was no peer sharing, mode
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.020|                    0 and mode 99 both still do that and neither is
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.021|                    touched.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.022|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.023|  Windows default : Policy value absent; Delivery Optimization falls back
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.024|                    to LAN peering on consumer editions.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.025|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.027|    100 (0x64) - Bypass  : Bypasses Delivery Optimization entirely and
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.028|                           hands downloads to the old BITS path. Microsoft
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.029|                           has deprecated it, and on 24H2 and later it is
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.030|                           a documented cause of updates that queue
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.031|                           forever without ever downloading. This is the
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.032|                           ONLY value this step removes.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.033|    99 (0x63) - Simple   : A documented, supported, working mode:
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.034|                           downloads come straight from the Microsoft
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.035|                           servers, with no Delivery Optimization cloud
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.036|                           service and no peering. It is one of the modes
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.037|                           Microsoft points people to as the replacement
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.038|                           for the deprecated Bypass. Deleting it would
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.039|                           silently reverse a deliberate configuration, so
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.040|                           it must NOT be touched - this is the correction
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.041|                           applied to the previous version of this card.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.042|    0 - HTTP only        : No peering at all, plain HTTP from Microsoft,
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.043|                           but the Delivery Optimization service still
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.044|                           manages the transfer. This is the value to use
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.045|                           if what you want is 'no sharing with other PCs'
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.046|                           - not 100.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.047|    1 - LAN peering      : Peers with other PCs on the same local network
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.048|                           only. The effective default behaviour on a
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.049|                           consumer Windows install.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.050|    2 - Group peering    : Peers within the same Active Directory site or
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.051|                           domain group. Intended for managed fleets.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.052|    3 - Internet peering : Peers with PCs on the internet as well as the
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.053|                           LAN. Uses your upload bandwidth to serve
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.054|                           strangers, which is the setting people are
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.055|                           usually looking for when they go hunting in
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.056|                           this key.
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.057|    DELETE               : The shipped state: no policy value, and
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.058|                           Delivery Optimization uses its own default (LAN
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.059|                           peering on consumer editions).
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete on 100 alone. A deprecated mode
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|                       that stops security updates from arriving is broken
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.003|                       on every kind of machine, and no profile has a
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.004|                       reason to want it. Everything else in this key is a
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.005|                       deliberate bandwidth choice, and a repair step has
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.006|                       no business overwriting one.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.007|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.008|  Known problems  : With mode 100 on 24H2 or later, Windows Update sits at
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.009|                    0% or reports a generic download failure and retries
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.010|                    indefinitely; Store app updates stall the same way.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.011|                    Nothing in the Windows Update UI points at Delivery
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.012|                    Optimization as the cause.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.013|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.014|  Unverified      : The exact failure mode of DODownloadMode 100 on 24H2
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.015|                    and later is reported rather than measured here; what
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.016|                    is solid is that Microsoft documents mode 100 as
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.017|                    deprecated.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.018|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.019|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DeliveryOptim
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.020|                    ization /v DODownloadMode - deleted via :killkey only
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.021|                    when the read value is 0x64 (OPTY.bat lines 1960-1966;
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.022|                    line 1966 currently also deletes 0x63 and should be
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.023|                    removed)
