:: ---- regsvr32.update.dlls (repair) ------------------------------
::P|regsvr32.update.dlls|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|REENREGISTREMENT DE ATL, URLMON ET MSHTML
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|  Ce que c est    : Reenregistre trois bibliotheques COM - atl.dll,
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                    urlmon.dll et mshtml.dll - en reecrivant leurs entrees
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                    CLSID sous HKCR. Ca vient de la recette classique de «
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                    reinitialisation de Windows Update » de l'epoque
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|                    Windows 7, ou elle voisinait avec le renommage de
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|                    SoftwareDistribution et de catroot2.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|  Effet reel      : Chaque appel a DllRegisterServer reecrit les cles
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                    d'enregistrement COM de la bibliotheque. Sur une
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|                    machine ou ces cles sont intactes, les memes valeurs
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|                    reviennent. A noter : le bloc environnant vient
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|                    d'arreter bits, wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc et
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|                    appidsvc, donc tout ceci tourne avec les services
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|                    cryptographiques a l'arret.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable sur une installation Windows 11
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|                    saine. Ca repare un degat qu'une machine moderne n'a
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    pour ainsi dire jamais, et aucun mecanisme ne fait
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    qu'une CLSID deja correcte, reecrite a l'identique,
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|                    rende les mises a jour plus rapides ou plus fiables.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.023|                    Dire que « ca aide Windows Update » est precisement le
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.024|                    genre d'affirmation que ce script ne doit pas
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.025|                    produire.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.026|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.027|  Cout            : Quasiment nul, mais pas sans zone d'ombre. mshtml.dll
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.028|                    appartient au moteur d'Internet Explorer que Microsoft
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.029|                    retire progressivement de Windows 11 : ce troisieme
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.030|                    appel peut simplement echouer. Le /s masque le
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.031|                    resultat dans les deux cas, donc OPTY ne peut rien
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.032|                    vous en dire, et il ne pretend pas le contraire. Le
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.033|                    vrai cout, c'est le temps et la fausse assurance
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.034|                    d'avoir « fait quelque chose ».
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.035|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.036|  Defaut Windows  : Deja enregistrees sur toute installation saine. Sans
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.037|                    effet, sauf si les entrees COM etaient reellement
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.038|                    abimees.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.039|
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.040|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.041|    RUN                  : regsvr32 /s charge chaque DLL et appelle son
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.042|                           point d'entree DllRegisterServer, qui reecrit
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.043|                           les entrees CLSID, ProgID et InprocServer32 de
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.044|                           la bibliotheque sous HKCR. Sur une machine ou
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.045|                           ces cles sont intactes, il reecrit des valeurs
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.046|                           strictement identiques. Le /s supprime la boite
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.047|                           de dialogue de resultat : cote OPTY, un succes
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.048|                           et un echec se ressemblent exactement.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.049|    SKIP                 : Rien ne s'execute. Les enregistrements COM
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.050|                           restent tels que Windows les a livres, ce qui,
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.051|                           sur toute installation saine, donne exactement
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.052|                           le meme resultat que RUN.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils repondent SKIP, et c'est la
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|                         conclusion, pas un trou dans le tableau. Cette
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|                         etape n'a aucune dimension performance, energie
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                         ou debit : gaming, serveur, bureautique et
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                         portable n'ont rien sur quoi diverger - et comme
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                         c'est une operation sans effet sur une machine
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|                         intacte, WINDOWS arrive au meme endroit pour une
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|                         autre raison. Le seul cas ou elle a sa place
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|                         n'est pas un profil : c'est une machine dont
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|                         Windows Update echoue vraiment avec des erreurs
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                         COM ou en 0x8007, qu'on repare a la main etape
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|                         par etape.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether mshtml.dll still registers successfully on
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|                      current Windows 11 25H2 builds was not verified, and
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|                      /s hides the result either way, so OPTY cannot
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|                      report whether any of the three calls did anything.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|  Cible           : OPTY.bat lines 525-529, inside the Windows Update
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    reset block that has just run net stop on bits,
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc and appidsvc:
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|                    regsvr32.exe /s atl.dll ; regsvr32.exe /s urlmon.dll ;
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.023|                    regsvr32.exe /s mshtml.dll
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|RE-REGISTER ATL, URLMON AND MSHTML
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|  What it is      : Re-registers three COM libraries - atl.dll, urlmon.dll
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                    and mshtml.dll - by rewriting their CLSID entries
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                    under HKCR. It comes from the classic 'reset Windows
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                    Update' recipe that circulated in the Windows 7 era,
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|                    where it sat alongside renaming SoftwareDistribution
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|                    and catroot2.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|  Actual effect   : Each DllRegisterServer call writes that library's COM
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                    registration keys again. On a machine where those keys
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|                    are intact, the same values go back. Note that the
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|                    surrounding block has already stopped bits, wuauserv,
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|                    msiserver, cryptsvc and appidsvc, so this runs with
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|                    cryptographic services down.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|  Gain            : No measurable gain on a healthy Windows 11 install.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|                    This repairs damage a modern machine essentially never
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|                    has, and there is no mechanism by which re-writing an
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    already-correct CLSID makes updates faster, more
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    reliable, or anything else. Saying it 'helps Windows
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|                    Update' is exactly the kind of claim this script must
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.023|                    not make.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.024|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.025|  Cost            : Essentially none, but not free of doubt. mshtml.dll
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.026|                    belongs to the Internet Explorer engine that Microsoft
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.027|                    has been removing from Windows 11, so that third call
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.028|                    can simply fail; /s hides the result either way, so
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.029|                    OPTY cannot tell you and does not claim to. The real
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.030|                    cost is time and the false confidence of having 'done
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.031|                    something'.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.032|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.033|  Windows default : Already registered on any healthy install. This is a
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.034|                    no-op unless the COM entries were genuinely damaged.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.035|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.036|  Possible values:
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.037|    RUN                  : regsvr32 /s loads each DLL and calls its
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.038|                           DllRegisterServer entry point, which rewrites
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.039|                           that library's CLSID, ProgID and InprocServer32
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.040|                           entries under HKCR. On a machine where those
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.041|                           keys are intact it writes byte-identical values
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.042|                           back. /s suppresses the result dialog, so a
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.043|                           success and a failure look exactly the same
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.044|                           from OPTY's side.
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.045|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs. The COM registrations stay
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.046|                           exactly as Windows shipped them, which on any
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.047|                           healthy install is the same thing RUN would
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.048|                           have produced.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|  Why these profiles : All five are SKIP, and that is the finding rather
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|                       than a gap in the table. This step has no
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|                       performance, power or throughput dimension at all,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                       so there is nothing for gaming, server, office and
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                       laptop to disagree about - and since it is a no-op
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                       on an undamaged machine, WINDOWS lands on the same
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|                       answer for a different reason. The one case where
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|                       it belongs is not a profile: it is a machine whose
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|                       Windows Update is genuinely failing with COM or
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|                       0x8007 errors, being worked through a repair
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                       sequence by hand.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|  Unverified      : Whether mshtml.dll still registers successfully on
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|                    current Windows 11 25H2 builds was not verified, and
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|                    /s hides the result either way, so OPTY cannot report
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|                    whether any of the three calls did anything.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|  Target          : OPTY.bat lines 525-529, inside the Windows Update
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|                    reset block that has just run net stop on bits,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc and appidsvc:
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    regsvr32.exe /s atl.dll ; regsvr32.exe /s urlmon.dll ;
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|                    regsvr32.exe /s mshtml.dll
