::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.001|RESTORE CHROME'S OWN UPDATE DEFAULT
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.002|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.003|  What it is      : Google Update reads policies from
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Google\Update. On a stock
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.005|                    Windows install that key does not exist at all, and
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.006|                    its absence already means automatic updates. The step
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.007|                    matters only on a machine where a GPO, an admin, or a
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.008|                    'debloat' script switched updates off by writing 0.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.009|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.010|  Actual effect   : As the table calls for it: delete UpdateDefault. If a
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.011|                    block was present, automatic updates resume; if
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.012|                    nothing was there, nothing happens. The code as
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.013|                    written today does something different - it writes 1 -
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.014|                    and that difference is the point of this card. Before
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.015|                    the write, OPTY checks whether chrome.exe is running
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.016|                    and asks you to close it yourself; it never force-
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.017|                    kills the browser, and the registry write does not
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.018|                    need Chrome closed at all.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.019|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.020|  Gain            : On a blocked machine: a browser that updates itself
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.021|                    again. Chrome is the most exposed piece of software on
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.022|                    most machines and an out-of-date Chrome is a genuine
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.023|                    security problem. On a machine that was never blocked:
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.024|                    no measurable gain, because absence already means
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.025|                    automatic updates - and this is exactly where the old
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.026|                    'write 1' version was pretending to fix something.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.027|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.028|  Cost            : Deleting costs nothing on an unblocked machine and
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.029|                    cannot break an update. Writing 1, which is what the
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.030|                    code does today, creates a policy entry where none
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.031|                    existed: chrome://policy then shows a managed value,
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.032|                    cosmetically different from the shipped 'not
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.033|                    configured' state even though the behaviour is
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.034|                    identical. On a domain-joined PC the real GPO
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.035|                    overwrites either choice at the next policy refresh.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.036|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.037|  Windows default : The key does not exist on a stock install. Absence
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.038|                    means Google's own default: automatic updates on.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.039|
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.040|  Possible values:
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.041|    DELETE               : Remove UpdateDefault entirely. Google Update
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.042|                           then falls back to its built-in default, which
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.043|                           is 'always allow updates' - the same behaviour
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.044|                           as 1, but with no policy entry, so
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.045|                           chrome://policy shows nothing and Chrome does
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.046|                           not report itself as managed. On a machine
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.047|                           where a debloat script had set 0, deleting
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.048|                           restores automatic updates just as effectively
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.049|                           as writing 1. This is the one action that is
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.050|                           correct in both the blocked and the untouched
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.051|                           case.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.052|    0                    : Updates disabled. Google Update never installs
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.053|                           anything, including security fixes. This is
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.054|                           what a 'debloat' script or a corporate GPO
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.055|                           writes when it wants to freeze the browser, and
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.056|                           it is the state this step exists to undo.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.057|    1                    : Always allow updates - what the code writes
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.058|                           today. Behaviourally identical to the value
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.059|                           being absent, but it materialises
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.060|                           HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Google\Update on a
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.061|                           machine that had no such key, so
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.062|                           chrome://policy now lists a managed value where
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.063|                           the shipped state was 'not configured'. That is
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.064|                           a third state, not the default.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.065|    2                    : Manual updates only. Automatic background
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.066|                           updates stop; the browser only updates when
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.067|                           someone opens the About page and clicks.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.068|                           Practically this means an unattended machine
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.069|                           stays on whatever build it had.
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.070|    3                    : Automatic silent updates only - background
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.071|                           updates run, but a manual check from the About
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.072|                           page is refused. Rare, and mainly used to stop
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.073|                           users pulling a newer build ahead of a
::T|EN|google.update.policy.on.074|                           deployment schedule.
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, because once you accept
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.002|                       that absence already means automatic updates, there
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.003|                       is no profile for which writing a policy is better
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.004|                       than having none - a gaming rig, a hosting box, an
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.005|                       office desktop and a laptop all want Chrome
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.006|                       patching itself, and all four get that from the
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.007|                       shipped state. This is one of the cases where the
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.008|                       reviewer's objection was right about the write:
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.009|                       forcing 1 turns a non-state into a managed state.
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.010|                       Deleting instead is what the correction called
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.011|                       'gate the write and only then call it a repair',
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.012|                       done more simply - it is a no-op on a healthy
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.013|                       machine and a real repair on a blocked one. On a
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.014|                       work PC you do not administer, expect the corporate
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.015|                       GPO to put the block straight back.
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.016|
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.017|  Unverified      : UpdateDefault is not the only way Chrome updates get
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.018|                    blocked. AutoUpdateCheckPeriodMinutes=0 in the same
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.019|                    key disables the update check outright,
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.020|                    DisableAutoUpdateChecksCheckboxValue=1 greys out the
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.021|                    user's control, and per-application
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.022|                    Update{8A69D345-D564-463C-AFF1-A69D9E530F96} values
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.023|                    override the global default for Chrome specifically.
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.024|                    OPTY touches none of these, so on a machine blocked by
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.025|                    one of them this step will report success and change
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.026|                    nothing.
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.027|
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.028|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Google\Update /v
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.029|                    "UpdateDefault" /t REG_DWORD - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.030|                    :enable_google_update, line 1427. The code currently
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.031|                    writes 1 with REG ADD; the profile table below calls
::X|EN|google.update.policy.on.032|                    for :killkey instead (delete the value).
::
:: ---- windows.update.policy.reenable (repair) --------------------
::P|windows.update.policy.reenable|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|DEBLOQUER LES STRATEGIES DE WINDOWS UPDATE
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|  Ce que c est    : Quatre strategies de registre servent couramment - aux
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                    GPO d'entreprise comme aux scripts de « debloat » - a
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                    paralyser Windows Update : DisableWindowsUpdateAccess
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                    et SetDisableUXWUAccess (masquent ou desactivent la
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                    page des Parametres), ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|                    (bloque les pilotes) et UseWUServer (pointe le PC vers
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|                    un serveur WSUS parfois disparu).
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|  Effet reel      : L'effet correct est la suppression, pas la mise a
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                    zero, parce que l'etat d'origine est que ces valeurs
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|                    n'existent pas. Ecrire 0 fonctionne en comportement
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|                    mais materialise une cle de strategie qui etait
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|                    absente, et une cle WindowsUpdate peuplee est
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                    justement une des choses qui font afficher « Certains
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                    parametres sont geres par votre organisation » dans
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                    les Parametres. Le code actuel ecrit 0 dans les quatre
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                    : c'est un ecart assume par rapport a l'etat
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                    d'origine, et la doctrine d'OPTY ailleurs (l'assistant
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                    killkey, et l'etape rad-wu-policy-purge qui supprime
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|                    exactement ces noms) dit l'inverse. Le service est
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|                    arrete et redemarre autour du changement pour qu'il
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|                    soit pris en compte.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|  Gain            : Windows Update refonctionne : la page des Parametres
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|                    s'ouvre, les mises a jour viennent de Microsoft et non
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|                    d'un serveur WSUS mort, et les pilotes sont de nouveau
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|                    autorises. Sur un PC d'occasion venant d'une
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|                    entreprise, c'est souvent la difference entre une
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|                    machine qui se met a jour et une qui ne le fait plus
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|                    jamais en silence.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|  Cout            : Reautoriser les pilotes est un vrai compromis :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                    certains mettent volontairement
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 pour empecher
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|                    Windows d'ecraser un pilote graphique ou audio choisi
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|                    a la main. Si c'est votre cas, remettez-le a 1 ensuite
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|                    - deliberément, comme votre choix, et non comme un
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|                    residu. Sur un PC dans un domaine, la GPO d'entreprise
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|                    reecrit les quatre au prochain rafraichissement :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|                    l'effet y est de toute facon temporaire.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.043|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.044|  Defaut Windows  : Aucune de ces valeurs n'existe sur une installation
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.045|                    Windows 11 d'origine - la cle de strategie est
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.046|                    absente, ce qui signifie « non configure ».
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.047|
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.048|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.049|    DELETE               : Supprimer les quatre valeurs. C'est la seule
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.050|                           ecriture qui reproduise l'etat d'origine,
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.051|                           puisque sur une installation Windows 11
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.052|                           d'origine la cle de strategie WindowsUpdate
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.053|                           n'existe pas. Une fois la cle vide, Parametres
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.054|                           > Windows Update cesse d'afficher « Certains
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.055|                           parametres sont geres par votre organisation »
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.056|                           et les controles grises redeviennent
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.057|                           cliquables. Sur une machine jamais bloquee, la
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.058|                           suppression ne fait rien et ne dit rien.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.059|    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess=1 :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.060|        L'utilisateur ne peut plus atteindre Windows Update du tout : la
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.061|        page des Parametres refuse de s'ouvrir et les recherches manuelles
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.062|        sont bloquees. Mise a 0 elle autorise l'acces, mais la valeur
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.063|        existe toujours, donc la cle de strategie reste peuplee.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.064|    SetDisableUXWUAccess=1 :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.065|        Masque ou desactive la partie Windows Update des Parametres -
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.066|        l'equivalent moderne de la valeur precedente. 0 reactive la page
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.067|        et, la encore, laisse une valeur de strategie derriere elle.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.068|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 :
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.069|        Windows Update ne propose plus jamais de pilotes ni de firmware
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.070|        dans une mise a jour de qualite. Certains la posent volontairement
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.071|        pour que Windows n'ecrase pas un pilote graphique ou audio choisi
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.072|        a la main. 0 reautorise les pilotes ; l'absence signifie la meme
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.073|        chose, sans la marque « gere ».
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.074|    UseWUServer=1        : Windows prend ses mises a jour sur le serveur
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.075|                           WSUS indique dans WUServer au lieu de
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.076|                           Microsoft. Si ce serveur n'existe plus - le cas
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.077|                           normal sur une machine d'occasion venue d'une
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.078|                           entreprise - chaque recherche echoue et le PC
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.079|                           cesse discretement de se corriger. 0 le renvoie
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.080|                           vers Microsoft ; la suppression fait de meme et
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.081|                           retire la strategie.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq DELETE identiques, et cette uniformite est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|                         la conclusion : ce n'est pas un reglage fin,
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|                         c'est le retrait d'un blocage. Aucun role de
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                         machine ne gagne a ce que Windows Update soit
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                         inaccessible ou pointe vers un serveur WSUS qui
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                         ne repond plus. Le seul endroit ou un profil
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                         pourrait diverger est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|                         ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, ou une machine
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|                         de jeu voudrait figer ses pilotes - mais cet
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|                         argument appartient aux fiches sur la livraison
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|                         des pilotes, et le rearmer doit etre une decision
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                         prise ensuite, pas un residu conserve par
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|                         accident.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|  Problemes connus : Deux cas concrets. D'abord, ecrire 0 au lieu de
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                     supprimer laisse la cle de strategie peuplee, et le
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                     symptome se voit dans l'application Parametres :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                     Windows Update conserve le bandeau « Certains
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                     parametres sont geres par votre organisation » et
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                     certains controles restent grises alors que les mises
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                     a jour fonctionnent de nouveau. Ensuite, le bloc
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|                     execute « Net stop wuauserv » et « Net start wuauserv
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|                     » sans redirection de sortie ni controle d'erreur :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|                     sur une machine ou wuauserv a ete mis sur Desactive -
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|                     c'est-a-dire exactement la machine visee par cette
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|                     etape - net start echoue avec « Le service demande a
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|                     deja ete demarre » ou « Le service n'a pas pu
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|                     demarrer », et l'erreur defile pendant qu'OPTY n'en
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|                     dit rien.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether an empty-but-present WindowsUpdate policy
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|                      key alone is enough to keep the 'managed by your
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|                      organization' banner, or whether the key must
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|                      contain at least one value, was not tested byte-for-
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                      byte on 25H2. Deleting the values is correct either
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                      way.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|                    /v DisableWindowsUpdateAccess, /v
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|                    SetDisableUXWUAccess, /v
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU /v UseWUServer
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.043|                    - OPTY.bat :enable_windows_update, lines 1447-1453,
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.044|                    currently four REG ADD ... /d 0 /f between Net stop
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.045|                    wuauserv and Net start wuauserv. The profile table
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.046|                    below calls for :killkey on all four instead.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|UNBLOCK WINDOWS UPDATE POLICIES
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|  What it is      : Four registry policies are commonly used - by company
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                    GPOs and by 'debloat' scripts - to cripple Windows
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                    Update: DisableWindowsUpdateAccess and
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                    SetDisableUXWUAccess (both hide or disable the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                    Settings page), ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|                    (blocks driver updates), and UseWUServer (points the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|                    PC at a WSUS server that may no longer exist).
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|  Actual effect   : The correct effect is deletion, not zeroing, because
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                    the shipped state is that none of these values exist.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|                    Writing 0 works behaviourally but materialises a
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|                    policy key that was absent, and a populated
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|                    WindowsUpdate policy key is one of the things that
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                    makes Settings display 'Some settings are managed by
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                    your organization'. The code as it stands today writes
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                    0 to all four - that is a known deviation from the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                    shipped state, and OPTY's own doctrine elsewhere (the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                    killkey helper, and the rad-wu-policy-purge step,
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                    which does delete these very names) says the opposite.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|                    The service is stopped and restarted around the change
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|                    so it is picked up.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|  Gain            : Windows Update works again: the Settings page opens,
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|                    updates come from Microsoft instead of a dead WSUS
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|                    server, and driver updates are allowed. On an ex-
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|                    corporate second-hand PC this is often the difference
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|                    between a machine that patches itself and one that
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|                    silently never does.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|  Cost            : Re-allowing driver updates is a real trade-off: some
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|                    people set ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 on
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|                    purpose to stop Windows overwriting a hand-picked GPU
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                    or audio driver. If that is you, put it back to 1
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                    afterwards - deliberately, as your own choice rather
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|                    than as a leftover. On a domain-joined PC the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|                    corporate GPO rewrites all four at the next refresh,
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|                    so the effect there is temporary either way.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|  Windows default : None of these values exist on a stock Windows 11
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|                    install - the policy key is absent, which is 'not
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.043|                    configured'.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.044|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.045|  Possible values:
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.046|    DELETE               : Remove all four values. This is the only write
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.047|                           that reproduces the shipped state, because on a
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.048|                           stock Windows 11 install the WindowsUpdate
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.049|                           policy key does not exist. When the key ends up
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.050|                           empty, Settings > Windows Update stops showing
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.051|                           'Some settings are managed by your
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.052|                           organization' and the greyed-out controls
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.053|                           become clickable again. On a machine that was
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.054|                           never blocked, deleting is a silent no-op.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.055|    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess=1 :
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.056|        The user cannot reach Windows Update at all: the Settings page
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.057|        refuses to open and manual checks are blocked. Set to 0 it permits
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.058|        access, but the value still exists, so the policy key remains
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.059|        populated.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.060|    SetDisableUXWUAccess=1 :
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.061|        Hides or disables the Windows Update surface in Settings - the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.062|        modern counterpart of the value above. 0 re-enables the page and,
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.063|        again, leaves a policy value behind.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.064|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 :
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.065|        Windows Update never offers drivers or firmware as part of a
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.066|        quality update. Some people set this deliberately so Windows
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.067|        cannot overwrite a hand-picked GPU or audio driver. 0 re-allows
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.068|        drivers; absent means the same thing, without the managed marker.
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.069|    UseWUServer=1        : Windows takes its updates from the WSUS server
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.070|                           named in WUServer instead of from Microsoft. If
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.071|                           that server no longer exists - the normal case
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.072|                           on an ex-corporate machine sold on - every scan
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.073|                           fails and the PC quietly stops patching. 0
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.074|                           sends it back to Microsoft; deleting does the
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.075|                           same and removes the policy.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, and the uniformity is the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|                       finding: this is not a tuning knob, it is the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|                       removal of a block. There is no machine role that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                       benefits from Windows Update being unreachable or
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                       from pointing at a WSUS server that answers
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                       nothing. The one place a profile could plausibly
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                       differ is ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, where a
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|                       gaming rig might want drivers pinned - but that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|                       argument belongs to the driver-delivery cards, and
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|                       re-arming it should be a deliberate decision made
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|                       afterwards, not a leftover this step preserved by
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                       accident.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|  Known problems  : Two concrete ones. First, writing 0 rather than
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|                    deleting leaves the policy key populated, and the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                    symptom is visible in the Settings app: Windows Update
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                    keeps the 'Some settings are managed by your
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                    organization' banner and some controls stay greyed
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                    even though updates now work. Second, the block runs
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                    'Net stop wuauserv' and 'Net start wuauserv' with no
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                    output redirection and no error check - on a machine
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|                    where wuauserv has been set to Disabled, which is
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|                    exactly the machine this step is aimed at, net start
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|                    fails with 'The requested service has already been
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|                    started' or 'The service cannot be started' and the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|                    error scrolls past while OPTY reports nothing.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|  Unverified      : Whether an empty-but-present WindowsUpdate policy key
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|                    alone is enough to keep the 'managed by your
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|                    organization' banner, or whether the key must contain
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|                    at least one value, was not tested byte-for-byte on
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|                    25H2. Deleting the values is correct either way.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                    /v DisableWindowsUpdateAccess, /v
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                    SetDisableUXWUAccess, /v
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU /v UseWUServer
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|                    - OPTY.bat :enable_windows_update, lines 1447-1453,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|                    currently four REG ADD ... /d 0 /f between Net stop
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|                    wuauserv and Net start wuauserv. The profile table
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|                    below calls for :killkey on all four instead.
::
:: ---- gr.searchorderconfig (repair) ------------------------------
::P|gr.searchorderconfig|1|1|1|1|1|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.001|AUTORISER WINDOWS UPDATE A FOURNIR LES PILOTES
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.002|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.003|  Ce que c est    : SearchOrderConfig decide si Windows peut recuperer
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.004|                    pilotes et firmwares via Windows Update. C'est la meme
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.005|                    valeur de registre que celle ecrite par l'etape «
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.006|                    pilotes » du menu RESTORE ; elle apparait deux fois
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.007|                    dans OPTY parce que deux menus differents en ont
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.008|                    besoin.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.009|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 1, la valeur d'origine : un peripherique branche
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.011|                    peut recevoir son pilote automatiquement. Sur une
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.012|                    machine qui avait deja 1, l'ecriture ne change rien et
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.013|                    le rapport le dit.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.014|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.015|  Gain            : Evite qu'un nouveau peripherique finisse en Code 28,
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.016|                    sans pilote et sans explication. Aucun gain de
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.017|                    vitesse, et aucun n'est revendique : il s'agit d'un
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.018|                    peripherique qui fonctionne ou pas.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.019|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.020|  Cout            : Windows peut poser un pilote generique ou WHQL par-
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.021|                    dessus celui du fabricant. C'est rare, et c'est
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.022|                    surtout visible sur les cartes graphiques, ou une
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.023|                    version Adrenalin ou NVIDIA installee a la main peut
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.024|                    etre remplacee par celle de Microsoft apres une mise a
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.025|                    jour de qualite.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.026|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.027|  Defaut Windows  : 1 - presente et a 1 sur une installation Windows 11
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.028|                    d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.029|
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.031|    1                    : Windows est autorise a chercher un pilote sur
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.032|                           Windows Update. C'est la valeur d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.033|                           Quand vous branchez un peripherique sans pilote
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.034|                           integre, Windows en demande un au service de
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.035|                           mise a jour et l'installe en arriere-plan :
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.036|                           c'est le mecanisme derriere « Installation du
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.037|                           peripherique... pret a etre utilise ».
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.038|    0                    : Ne jamais chercher sur Windows Update. Windows
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.039|                           se limite au magasin de pilotes deja present
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.040|                           sur le disque. Tout ce qui n'est pas couvert
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.041|                           d'origine reste inutilisable : c'est de la que
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.042|                           viennent les entrees en Code 28 « Les pilotes
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.043|                           de ce peripherique ne sont pas installes » dans
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.044|                           le Gestionnaire de peripheriques. Residu
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.045|                           classique d'outil d'optimisation.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.046|    2                    : « Chercher sur Windows Update uniquement si le
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.047|                           pilote est introuvable localement » - l'ancien
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.048|                           reglage a trois positions de Windows 7. Windows
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.049|                           10 et 11 en ont retire l'interface et traitent
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.050|                           cette branche comme un heritage ; ce n'est pas
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.051|                           une valeur que le systeme actuel ecrit lui-
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.052|                           meme.
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.053|    DELETE               : Valeur absente. Windows se comporte comme avec
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.054|                           1, mais sur une installation d'origine la
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.055|                           valeur est presente et vaut 1 : la retirer
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.056|                           serait donc une troisieme position, pas un
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.057|                           retour a l'origine. C'est pour cela que le
::T|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.058|                           tableau ecrit 1 et non DELETE.
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques, et c'est l'un des cas
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.002|                         les plus nets ou une distinction serait inventee
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.003|                         et non constatee. Un serveur sans ecran a besoin
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.004|                         d'un pilote pour un nouveau peripherique USB ou
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.005|                         une carte reseau exactement comme une machine de
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.006|                         jeu ; un portable qui ne peut pas installer le
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.007|                         pilote de sa station d'accueil est casse de la
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.008|                         meme facon qu'un poste bureautique. Le seul
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.009|                         profil qui pourrait plaider pour 0 est le gaming,
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.010|                         pour proteger un pilote graphique fige - mais 0
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.011|                         sacrifie tous les autres peripheriques pour en
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.012|                         proteger un seul, et le reglage qui gouverne
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.013|                         reellement le cas GPU est
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.014|                         ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, pas celui-ci.
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.015|                         WINDOWS s'aligne sur 1 parce que 1 est la valeur
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.016|                         livree.
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.017|
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.018|  Problemes connus : Avec SearchOrderConfig a 0, un peripherique
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.019|                     nouvellement branche apparait dans le Gestionnaire de
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.020|                     peripheriques sous « Autres peripheriques » avec un
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.021|                     point d'exclamation jaune et un Code 28, et « Mettre
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.022|                     a jour le pilote > Rechercher automatiquement »
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.023|                     repond « Les meilleurs pilotes pour votre appareil
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.024|                     sont deja installes » - ce qui est faux. C'est ce
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.025|                     message trompeur qui justifie cette reparation.
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.026|
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.027|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\DriverS
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.028|                    earching /v "SearchOrderConfig" /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f -
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.029|                    OPTY.bat line 1669, inside the map_only undo block
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.030|                    that restores services, GameDVR and hibernation to
::X|FR|gr.searchorderconfig.031|                    Windows defaults
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.001|ALLOW WINDOWS UPDATE TO DELIVER DRIVERS
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.002|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.003|  What it is      : SearchOrderConfig decides whether Windows may fetch
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.004|                    drivers and firmware from Windows Update. This is the
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.005|                    same registry value the RESTORE menu's driver step
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.006|                    writes; it appears twice in OPTY because two different
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.007|                    menus need it.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.008|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.009|  Actual effect   : Writes 1, the shipped value, so a newly attached
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.010|                    device can get a driver automatically. On a machine
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.011|                    that already had 1 the write is a no-op and the report
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.012|                    says so.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.013|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.014|  Gain            : Prevents a new device landing on Code 28 with no
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.015|                    driver and no explanation. There is no speed benefit
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.016|                    and none is claimed - this is about a device working
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.017|                    at all versus not working.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.018|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.019|  Cost            : Windows may install a generic or WHQL driver over a
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.020|                    vendor one. It is rare, and it happens most visibly on
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.021|                    GPUs, where a hand-installed Adrenalin or NVIDIA build
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.022|                    can be replaced by the Microsoft-supplied one after a
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.023|                    quality update.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.024|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.025|  Windows default : 1 - present and set to 1 on a stock Windows 11
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.026|                    install.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.027|
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.029|    1                    : Windows may search Windows Update for a driver.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.030|                           This is the shipped value. When you plug in a
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.031|                           device that has no in-box driver, Windows asks
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.032|                           the update service for one and installs it in
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.033|                           the background; that is the mechanism behind
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.034|                           'Setting up device... ready to use'.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.035|    0                    : Never search Windows Update. Windows is limited
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.036|                           to the driver store already on the disk.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.037|                           Anything not covered in-box stays unusable,
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.038|                           which is where the Code 28 'The drivers for
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.039|                           this device are not installed' entries in
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.040|                           Device Manager come from. Classic optimizer
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.041|                           leftover.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.042|    2                    : 'Search Windows Update only if the driver is
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.043|                           not found locally' - the old Windows 7 three-
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.044|                           way setting. Windows 10 and 11 removed the UI
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.045|                           for it and treat this branch as legacy; it is
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.046|                           not a value the current OS writes for itself.
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.047|    DELETE               : Value absent. Windows behaves as 1, but on a
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.048|                           stock install the value is present and set to
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.049|                           1, so removing it would be a third state rather
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.050|                           than a restore. That is why the table writes 1
::T|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.051|                           and not DELETE.
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers, and this is one of the
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.002|                       clearest cases where a split would be invented
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.003|                       rather than found. A headless server needs a driver
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.004|                       for a new USB or NIC just as much as a gaming
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.005|                       desktop does; a laptop that cannot install a driver
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.006|                       for a docking station is broken in exactly the same
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.007|                       way as an office PC. The one profile that might
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.008|                       argue for 0 is gaming, to protect a pinned GPU
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.009|                       driver - but 0 costs every other device on the
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.010|                       machine to protect one, and the knob that actually
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.011|                       governs the GPU case is
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.012|                       ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, not this one.
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.013|                       WINDOWS agrees at 1 because 1 is what Windows
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.014|                       ships.
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.015|
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.016|  Known problems  : With SearchOrderConfig at 0, a newly attached device
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.017|                    appears in Device Manager under 'Other devices' with a
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.018|                    yellow mark and Code 28, and 'Update driver > Search
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.019|                    automatically' returns 'The best drivers for your
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.020|                    device are already installed' - which is false. That
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.021|                    misleading message is the reason this repair exists.
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.022|
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.023|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\DriverS
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.024|                    earching /v "SearchOrderConfig" /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f -
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.025|                    OPTY.bat line 1669, inside the map_only undo block
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.026|                    that restores services, GameDVR and hibernation to
::X|EN|gr.searchorderconfig.027|                    Windows defaults
::
:: ---- rad.wu.driversearch (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.wu.driversearch|1|1|1|1|1|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.001|REAUTORISER LA LIVRAISON DES PILOTES PAR WINDOWS UPDATE
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.002|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.003|  Ce que c est    : La version « menu RESTORE » du correctif pilotes. Elle
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.004|                    ecrit le meme SearchOrderConfig=1 que l'autre fiche
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.005|                    pilotes, et supprime en plus les trois valeurs de
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.006|                    strategie capables de bloquer la livraison meme quand
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.007|                    SearchOrderConfig est correct.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.008|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit SearchOrderConfig=1, la valeur d'origine, puis
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.010|                    supprime ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.011|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate et
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.012|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork si elles existent.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.013|                    Suppression et non mise a zero, parce que la valeur
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.014|                    par defaut des trois est l'absence.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.015|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.016|  Gain            : Un peripherique branche demain obtient son pilote au
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.017|                    lieu de rester en erreur dans le Gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.018|                    peripheriques. C'est la version la plus complete :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.019|                    SearchOrderConfig=1 seul ne suffit pas si
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.020|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate est encore la, puisque la
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.021|                    strategie l'emporte.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.022|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.023|  Cout            : Windows peut parfois poser un pilote generique par-
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.024|                    dessus celui du fabricant. Si vous figez votre pilote
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.025|                    graphique, notez qu'ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.026|                    est supprimee ici : remettez-la a 1 vous-meme ensuite
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.027|                    si cette protection vous tient reellement a coeur.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.028|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.029|  Defaut Windows  : SearchOrderConfig=1, presente. Les trois valeurs de
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.030|                    strategie absentes.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.031|
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.033|    1                    : SearchOrderConfig=1, la valeur d'origine :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.034|                           Windows peut chercher sur Windows Update le
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.035|                           pilote dont un peripherique a besoin. Ecrite
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.036|                           sans condition, et les trois residus de
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.037|                           strategie ci-dessous sont supprimes en meme
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.038|                           temps, car un seul d'entre eux suffit a
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.039|                           maintenir les pilotes bloques.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.040|    0                    : SearchOrderConfig=0 : Windows ne regarde jamais
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.041|                           sur Windows Update. Combine a
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.042|                           ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1, cela empeche
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.043|                           l'arrivee de tout pilote ou firmware, et c'est
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.044|                           l'etat que cette etape existe pour defaire.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.045|    2                    : SearchOrderConfig=2, l'ancienne branche Windows
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.046|                           7 « seulement si introuvable localement ».
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.047|                           Aucune interface Windows actuelle ne l'ecrit.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.048|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 -> DELETE :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.049|        Tant qu'elle est presente et a 1, les mises a jour de qualite ne
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.050|        transportent plus ni pilote ni firmware. Sa vraie valeur par
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.051|        defaut est l'ABSENCE : elle est donc supprimee plutot que mise a 0
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.052|        - ecrire 0 laisserait une cle de strategie et conserverait la
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.053|        mention « gere par votre organisation » dans les Parametres.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.054|    DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 -> DELETE :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.055|        La strategie de groupe « Specifier l'ordre de recherche des
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.056|        emplacements sources des pilotes » reglee sur « Ne pas rechercher
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.057|        sur Windows Update ». Elle prime sur SearchOrderConfig : corriger
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.058|        SearchOrderConfig seul en laissant celle-ci en place ne change
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.059|        rien. Valeur par defaut : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.060|    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1 -> DELETE :
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.061|        Bloque le telechargement des metadonnees de peripherique - icones,
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.062|        noms lisibles et informations constructeur qui font apparaitre une
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.063|        imprimante sous son modele plutot qu'en boitier generique. Elle ne
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.064|        bloque pas les pilotes, et aucun gain n'est revendique pour sa
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.065|        suppression au-dela de peripheriques a nouveau correctement
::T|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.066|        nommes. Valeur par defaut : absente.
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques, pour la meme raison que
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.002|                         l'autre fiche pilotes : un peripherique obtient
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.003|                         son pilote ou ne l'obtient pas, et aucun role de
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.004|                         machine ne gagne au second cas. Le contenu
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.005|                         supplementaire de cette fiche - les trois
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.006|                         suppressions de strategie - ne change rien au
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.007|                         calcul : leur valeur par defaut a toutes les
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.008|                         trois est l'absence, donc DELETE est a la fois la
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.009|                         reparation et l'etat d'origine. L'exception
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.010|                         gaming qu'on invoque volontiers (figer le pilote
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.011|                         graphique) est un choix deliberé a poser ensuite,
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.012|                         pas une raison de laisser trois strategies
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.013|                         bloquantes en place.
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.014|
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.015|  Problemes connus : Corriger SearchOrderConfig sans supprimer
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.016|                     DontSearchWindowsUpdate est une operation sans effet,
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.017|                     et silencieuse : la valeur de strategie de groupe
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.018|                     prime, donc la machine refuse toujours de chercher
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.019|                     des pilotes alors que le registre a l'air correct et
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.020|                     qu'OPTY annonce l'ecriture reussie. C'est precisement
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.021|                     le scenario que les suppressions supplementaires de
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.022|                     cette etape evitent, et c'est pour cela que les deux
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.023|                     fiches pilotes ne sont pas interchangeables.
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.024|
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.025|  Cible           : OPTY.bat lines 1815-1818: call :regset "HKLM\SOFTWARE\
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.026|                    Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\DriverSearching"
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.027|                    "SearchOrderConfig" REG_DWORD 1, then :killkey on HKLM
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.028|                    \SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\Exc
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.029|                    ludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\M
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.030|                    icrosoft\Windows\DriverSearching\DontSearchWindowsUpda
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.031|                    te and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|FR|rad.wu.driversearch.032|                    Metadata\PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.001|RE-ALLOW DRIVER DELIVERY FROM WINDOWS UPDATE
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.002|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.003|  What it is      : The RESTORE-menu version of the driver fix. It writes
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.004|                    the same SearchOrderConfig=1 as the other driver card,
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.005|                    and additionally removes the three policy values that
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.006|                    can block driver delivery even when SearchOrderConfig
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.007|                    is correct.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.008|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.009|  Actual effect   : Writes SearchOrderConfig=1, the shipped value, then
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.010|                    deletes ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.011|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate and
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.012|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork if they exist.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.013|                    Deletion rather than zeroing, because the default for
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.014|                    all three is that the value does not exist.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.015|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.016|  Gain            : A device you plug in tomorrow gets a driver instead of
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.017|                    sitting in Device Manager with an error and no clue
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.018|                    why. This version is the more complete one:
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.019|                    SearchOrderConfig=1 alone is not enough if
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.020|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate is still present, because the
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.021|                    policy wins.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.022|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.023|  Cost            : Windows may occasionally install a generic driver over
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.024|                    a vendor one. If you pin your own GPU driver, note
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.025|                    that ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate is deleted here -
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.026|                    re-set it to 1 yourself afterwards if that protection
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.027|                    is something you actually want.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.028|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.029|  Windows default : SearchOrderConfig=1, present. All three policy values
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.030|                    absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.031|
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.033|    1                    : SearchOrderConfig=1, the shipped value: Windows
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.034|                           may look on Windows Update for a driver a
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.035|                           device needs. Written unconditionally, and the
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.036|                           three policy leftovers below are deleted
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.037|                           alongside it, because any one of them alone is
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.038|                           enough to keep drivers blocked.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.039|    0                    : SearchOrderConfig=0: Windows never looks on
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.040|                           Windows Update. Combined with
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.041|                           ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 this stops
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.042|                           ANY driver or firmware ever arriving, which is
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.043|                           the state this step exists to undo.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.044|    2                    : SearchOrderConfig=2, the legacy Windows 7 'only
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.045|                           if not found locally' branch. No current
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.046|                           Windows UI writes it.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.047|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 -> DELETE :
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.048|        While present and set to 1, quality updates never carry drivers or
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.049|        firmware. Its true default is ABSENT, so it is deleted rather than
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.050|        set to 0 - writing 0 would leave a policy key behind and keep the
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.051|        'managed by your organization' marker in Settings.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.052|    DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 -> DELETE :
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.053|        The Group Policy 'Specify search order for device driver source
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.054|        locations' set to 'Do not search Windows Update'. It overrides
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.055|        SearchOrderConfig, so fixing SearchOrderConfig alone while leaving
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.056|        this in place changes nothing. Default is absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.057|    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1 -> DELETE :
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.058|        Blocks the device metadata download - the icons, friendly names
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.059|        and manufacturer info that make a printer show as its model
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.060|        instead of a generic box. It does not block drivers, and no gain
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.061|        is claimed for removing it beyond devices being named properly
::T|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.062|        again. Default is absent.
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers for the same reason as the
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.002|                       other driver card: a device either gets a driver or
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.003|                       it does not, and no machine role benefits from the
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.004|                       second outcome. The extra content of this card -
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.005|                       the three policy deletes - does not change the
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.006|                       calculus either; all three defaults are absence, so
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.007|                       DELETE is simultaneously the repair and the shipped
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.008|                       state. The gaming exception people reach for (pin
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.009|                       the GPU driver) is a deliberate opt-in to be made
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.010|                       afterwards, not a reason to leave three blocking
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.011|                       policies in place.
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.012|
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.013|  Known problems  : Fixing SearchOrderConfig without removing
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.014|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate is a silent no-op: the Group
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.015|                    Policy value takes precedence, so the machine still
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.016|                    refuses to fetch drivers while the registry looks
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.017|                    correct and OPTY reports the write as successful. That
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.018|                    is exactly the failure mode this step's extra deletes
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.019|                    prevent, and it is why the two driver cards are not
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.020|                    interchangeable.
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.021|
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.022|  Target          : OPTY.bat lines 1815-1818: call :regset "HKLM\SOFTWARE\
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.023|                    Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\DriverSearching"
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.024|                    "SearchOrderConfig" REG_DWORD 1, then :killkey on HKLM
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.025|                    \SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\Exc
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.026|                    ludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\M
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.027|                    icrosoft\Windows\DriverSearching\DontSearchWindowsUpda
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.028|                    te and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|EN|rad.wu.driversearch.029|                    Metadata\PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork
::
:: ---- rad.wu.policy.purge (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.wu.policy.purge|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.001|NETTOYER LES STRATEGIES WINDOWS UPDATE RESIDUELLES
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.002|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.003|  Ce que c est    : 22 valeurs de strategie sous
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.005|                    et sa sous-cle AU : reports, pauses, pointeur vers un
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.006|                    serveur WSUS, version cible figee. Sur une machine
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.007|                    Windows 11 non geree, aucune ne devrait exister.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.008|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.009|  Effet reel      : Les supprime une par une via l'assistant killkey, qui
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.010|                    ne touche qu'une valeur reellement presente et
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.011|                    journalise chaque retrait par son nom. Tout le bloc
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.012|                    est saute si la machine est detectee jointe a un
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.013|                    domaine, jointe a Entra ID ou inscrite en MDM - dans
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.014|                    ce cas OPTY affiche une ligne SKIPPED nommant la
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.015|                    raison, au lieu de ne rien faire en silence.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.016|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.017|  Gain            : Un pointeur WSUS oublie ou un TargetReleaseVersionInfo
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.018|                    fige arrete en silence les mises a jour de securite,
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.019|                    sans aucune erreur affichee : les Parametres annoncent
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.020|                    que tout va bien. Les retirer, c'est la difference
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.021|                    entre une machine qui se corrige et une machine qui en
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.022|                    a seulement l'air.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.023|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.024|  Cout            : Vous perdez vos reports volontaires. Si vous mettiez
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.025|                    en pause exprès, ou reteniez les mises a jour de
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.026|                    qualite quelques jours, refaites-le dans Parametres >
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.027|                    Windows Update > Options avancees. Aucun cout de
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.028|                    performance, d'aucune sorte.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.029|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.030|  Defaut Windows  : Les 22 absentes sur une machine non geree - la cle de
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.031|                    strategie elle-meme n'existe pas sur une installation
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.032|                    d'origine.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.033|
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.035|    DELETE               : Supprimer les 22 valeurs. L'etat d'origine de
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.036|                           chacune est de ne pas exister : la suppression
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.037|                           est donc le seul vrai retour a l'origine ;
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.038|                           ecrire 0 laisserait une cle de strategie
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.039|                           peuplee et Windows continuerait d'annoncer des
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.040|                           mises a jour gerees. Rien n'est supprime qui
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.041|                           n'etait pas la.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.042|    KEEP / SKIP          : Laisser les strategies en place. Correct sur
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.043|                           une machine dont vous avez pose les reports
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.044|                           vous-meme et voulez les garder, et correct sur
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.045|                           toute machine geree - c'est pour cela que
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.046|                           l'etape verifie d'abord et refuse d'agir quand
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.047|                           ISMANAGED est defini.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.048|    NoAutoUpdate=1       : Mise a jour automatique entierement coupee.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.049|                           Windows ne telecharge ni n'installe ; rien dans
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.050|                           l'interface ne l'annonce clairement. Par defaut
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.051|                           : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.052|    AUOptions=2 / 3 / 4 / 5 :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.053|        2 = prevenir avant le telechargement, 3 = telecharger mais
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.054|        prevenir avant l'installation, 4 = installer selon un calendrier,
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.055|        5 = laisser l'administrateur local choisir. N'importe laquelle
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.056|        remplace l'experience de mise a jour moderne par le comportement
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.057|        de l'epoque Windows 7. Par defaut : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.058|    UseWUServer=1 + WUServer / WUStatusServer :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.059|        Envoie chaque recherche vers le serveur WSUS indique au lieu de
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.060|        Microsoft. Sur un PC d'occasion venu d'une entreprise, ce serveur
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.061|        est injoignable : les recherches echouent en 0x8024401c ou
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.062|        0x80244022 et aucune mise a jour ne s'installe. Par defaut :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.063|        absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.064|    TargetReleaseVersion=1 + TargetReleaseVersionInfo=<build> :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.065|        Fige la machine sur une version nommee de Windows, par exemple
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.066|        21H2. Une fois cette version en fin de support, le PC ne recoit
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.067|        plus rien du tout, pendant que les Parametres annoncent
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.068|        tranquillement « Vous etes a jour ». C'est le residu le plus
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.069|        destructeur de la liste. Par defaut : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.070|    DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays / DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.071|        Retiennent les mises a jour de securite ou de fonctionnalite un
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.072|        certain nombre de jours, jusqu'a 30 et 365 respectivement.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.073|        Legitime en entreprise, poison lent sur un PC personnel ou plus
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.074|        personne ne se souvient de les avoir posees. Par defaut :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.075|        absentes.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.076|    PauseQualityUpdatesStartTime / PauseFeatureUpdatesStartTime :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.077|        Un horodatage marquant le debut d'une pause. Un horodatage oublie
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.078|        peut laisser les mises a jour suspendues bien apres la fin prevue.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.079|        Par defaut : absent.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.080|    BranchReadinessLevel=16 / 32 :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.081|        Choisit la branche de maintenance que suit la machine. Laissee par
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.082|        des outils qui essayaient de basculer une edition Famille sur une
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.083|        branche dont elle ne dispose pas. Par defaut : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.084|    DisableDualScan=1    : Empeche une machine geree par WSUS de puiser
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.085|                           aussi dans Microsoft Update. Sans objet une
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.086|                           fois UseWUServer parti, et pur residu sur un PC
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.087|                           domestique. Par defaut : absente.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.088|    NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers=1 :
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.089|        Bloque le redemarrage automatique tant qu'une session est ouverte.
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.090|        Ca parait courtois, et c'est la raison pour laquelle certaines
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.091|        machines accumulent des mois de mises a jour installees mais non
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.092|        finalisees, en attente d'un redemarrage que personne ne fait. Par
::T|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.093|        defaut : absente.
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq DELETE identiques, parce qu'ici l'etat
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.002|                         d'origine et la reparation sont la meme action :
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.003|                         chacune de ces valeurs a pour defaut de ne pas
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.004|                         exister, donc la supprimer c'est simultanement «
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.005|                         remettre Windows » et « reparer la machine ».
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.006|                         Aucun role de machine ne veut d'un pointeur WSUS
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.007|                         vers un serveur qui ne repond plus, ni d'une
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.008|                         version figee en fin de support. Le vrai garde-
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.009|                         fou n'est pas le profil : c'est le controle
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.010|                         ISMANAGED, et c'est pourquoi une machine
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.011|                         reellement geree est ecartee plutot que de
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.012|                         recevoir une reponse de profil differente.
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.013|
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.014|  Problemes connus : Deux symptomes concrets que ceci corrige. Avec
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.015|                     TargetReleaseVersionInfo fige sur une version sortie
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.016|                     du support, Parametres > Windows Update affiche «
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.017|                     Vous etes a jour » indefiniment alors qu'aucune mise
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.018|                     a jour de securite n'arrive - aucun avertissement
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.019|                     nulle part dans l'interface. Avec UseWUServer=1
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.020|                     pointant vers un WSUS demantele, chaque recherche
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.021|                     echoue en 0x8024401c ou 0x80244022, que les
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.022|                     Parametres presentent comme un generique « Des
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.023|                     problemes sont survenus lors de l'installation des
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.024|                     mises a jour », sans jamais mentionner WSUS. Les deux
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.025|                     sont courants sur du materiel d'occasion venu
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.026|                     d'entreprise.
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.027|
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.028|  Non verifie (en)  : The ISMANAGED probe checks USERDNSDOMAIN, the Group
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.029|                      Policy machine Distinguished-Name, the
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.030|                      CloudDomainJoin JoinInfo key and the Enrollments
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.031|                      DMClient key. A machine managed only through a
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.032|                      mechanism none of those four cover would not be
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.033|                      detected, and the purge would run on it.
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.034|
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.035|  Cible           : OPTY.bat lines 1951-1959. Guarded by 'if defined
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.036|                    ISMANAGED goto rad_wu_report'. WUP=HKLM\SOFTWARE\Polic
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.037|                    ies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate; :killkey on
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.038|                    NoAutoUpdate, AUOptions, UseWUServer, WUServer,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.039|                    WUStatusServer, SetDisableUXWUAccess,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.040|                    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.041|                    SetUpdateNotificationLevel, DeferQualityUpdates,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.042|                    DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays, DeferFeatureUpdates,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.043|                    DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays, TargetReleaseVersion,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.044|                    TargetReleaseVersionInfo,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.045|                    PauseQualityUpdatesStartTime,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.046|                    PauseFeatureUpdatesStartTime, BranchReadinessLevel,
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.047|                    DisableDualScan; then :killkey on %WUP%\AU for
::X|FR|rad.wu.policy.purge.048|                    NoAutoUpdate, AUOptions, UseWUServer,
