::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.001|CLEAR WINDOWS UPDATE POLICY LEFTOVERS
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.002|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.003|  What it is      : 22 policy values under
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.005|                    and its AU subkey, covering deferrals, pauses, a WSUS
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.006|                    server pointer and a pinned target version. On an
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.007|                    unmanaged Windows 11 machine none of them should
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.008|                    exist.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.009|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.010|  Actual effect   : Deletes them one at a time through the killkey helper,
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.011|                    which only touches a value that is actually present
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.012|                    and logs each removal by name. The whole block is
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.013|                    skipped when the machine is detected as domain-joined,
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.014|                    Entra ID joined or MDM enrolled - in that case OPTY
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.015|                    prints a SKIPPED line naming the reason instead of
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.016|                    quietly doing nothing.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.017|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.018|  Gain            : A stale WSUS pointer or a TargetReleaseVersionInfo
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.019|                    left behind silently stops security updates, and
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.020|                    Settings shows no error at all - it says you are up to
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.021|                    date. Removing them is the difference between a
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.022|                    machine that patches itself and one that only appears
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.023|                    to.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.024|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.025|  Cost            : You lose deliberate deferrals. If you were pausing
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.026|                    feature updates on purpose, or holding quality updates
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.027|                    back by a few days, set that again in Settings >
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.028|                    Windows Update > Advanced options. There is no
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.029|                    performance cost of any kind.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.030|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.031|  Windows default : All 22 absent on an unmanaged machine - the policy key
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.032|                    itself does not exist on a stock install.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.033|
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.034|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.035|    DELETE               : Remove all 22 values. Each one's shipped state
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.036|                           is that it does not exist, so deletion is the
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.037|                           only true restore; writing 0 would leave a
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.038|                           populated policy key and keep Settings
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.039|                           reporting that updates are managed. Nothing is
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.040|                           deleted that was not there.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.041|    KEEP / SKIP          : Leave the policies in place. Correct on a
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.042|                           machine whose deferrals you set yourself and
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.043|                           want to keep, and correct on any managed
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.044|                           machine - which is why the step checks first
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.045|                           and refuses to run when ISMANAGED is set.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.046|    NoAutoUpdate=1       : Automatic updating off entirely. Windows
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.047|                           neither downloads nor installs; nothing in the
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.048|                           UI announces this clearly. Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.049|    AUOptions=2 / 3 / 4 / 5 :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.050|        2 = notify before download, 3 = download but notify before
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.051|        install, 4 = install on a schedule, 5 = let the local admin
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.052|        choose. Any of these overrides the modern update experience with
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.053|        the Windows 7-era behaviour. Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.054|    UseWUServer=1 + WUServer / WUStatusServer :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.055|        Sends every scan to the named WSUS server instead of Microsoft. On
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.056|        a second-hand ex-corporate PC that server is unreachable, so scans
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.057|        fail with 0x8024401c or 0x80244022 and no update ever installs.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.058|        Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.059|    TargetReleaseVersion=1 + TargetReleaseVersionInfo=<build> :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.060|        Pins the machine to a named Windows release, for example 21H2.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.061|        Once that release is out of support the PC receives nothing at
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.062|        all, while Settings cheerfully reports 'You're up to date'. This
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.063|        is the single most damaging leftover in the list. Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.064|    DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays / DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.065|        Hold security or feature updates back by a number of days, up to
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.066|        30 and 365 respectively. Legitimate in a business, and a slow
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.067|        poison on a personal PC where nobody remembers setting it.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.068|        Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.069|    PauseQualityUpdatesStartTime / PauseFeatureUpdatesStartTime :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.070|        A timestamp marking the start of a pause. A stale one can leave
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.071|        updates suspended long after the pause was meant to end. Default:
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.072|        absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.073|    BranchReadinessLevel=16 / 32 :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.074|        Selects the servicing branch the machine follows. Left behind by
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.075|        tools that tried to move a Home edition onto a branch it does not
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.076|        have. Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.077|    DisableDualScan=1    : Stops a WSUS-managed machine also pulling from
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.078|                           Microsoft Update. Meaningless once UseWUServer
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.079|                           is gone, and pure residue on a home PC.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.080|                           Default: absent.
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.081|    NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers=1 :
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.082|        Blocks the automatic restart while somebody is signed in. Sounds
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.083|        friendly, and it is the reason some machines accumulate months of
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.084|        installed-but-not-activated updates waiting for a restart nobody
::T|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.085|        performs. Default: absent.
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, because the shipped state
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.002|                       and the repair are the same action here: every one
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.003|                       of these values defaults to not existing, so
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.004|                       deleting is simultaneously 'restore Windows' and
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.005|                       'fix the machine'. No machine role wants a WSUS
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.006|                       pointer to a server that no longer answers or a
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.007|                       version pin to an out-of-support build. The real
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.008|                       gate is not the profile at all - it is the
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.009|                       ISMANAGED check, which is why a genuinely managed
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.010|                       machine is skipped rather than being given a
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.011|                       different profile answer.
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.012|
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.013|  Known problems  : Two concrete symptoms this fixes. With
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.014|                    TargetReleaseVersionInfo pinned to a release that has
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.015|                    left support, Settings > Windows Update reports
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.016|                    'You're up to date' indefinitely while no security
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.017|                    update ever arrives - there is no warning anywhere in
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.018|                    the UI. With UseWUServer=1 pointing at a
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.019|                    decommissioned WSUS host, every check fails with error
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.020|                    0x8024401c or 0x80244022, which Settings shows as a
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.021|                    generic 'There were some problems installing updates'
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.022|                    with no mention of WSUS. Both are common on second-
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.023|                    hand ex-corporate hardware.
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.024|
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.025|  Unverified      : The ISMANAGED probe checks USERDNSDOMAIN, the Group
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.026|                    Policy machine Distinguished-Name, the CloudDomainJoin
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.027|                    JoinInfo key and the Enrollments DMClient key. A
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.028|                    machine managed only through a mechanism none of those
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.029|                    four cover would not be detected, and the purge would
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.030|                    run on it.
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.031|
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.032|  Target          : OPTY.bat lines 1951-1959. Guarded by 'if defined
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.033|                    ISMANAGED goto rad_wu_report'. WUP=HKLM\SOFTWARE\Polic
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.034|                    ies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate; :killkey on
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.035|                    NoAutoUpdate, AUOptions, UseWUServer, WUServer,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.036|                    WUStatusServer, SetDisableUXWUAccess,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.037|                    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.038|                    SetUpdateNotificationLevel, DeferQualityUpdates,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.039|                    DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays, DeferFeatureUpdates,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.040|                    DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays, TargetReleaseVersion,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.041|                    TargetReleaseVersionInfo,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.042|                    PauseQualityUpdatesStartTime,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.043|                    PauseFeatureUpdatesStartTime, BranchReadinessLevel,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.044|                    DisableDualScan; then :killkey on %WUP%\AU for
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.045|                    NoAutoUpdate, AUOptions, UseWUServer,
::X|EN|rad.wu.policy.purge.046|                    NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers
