:: ---- rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys (cleanup) -------------------------
::P|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|RETIRER LES LIMITES DE DEBIT RESIDUELLES DES MISES A JOUR
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.003|  Ce que c est    : Trois valeurs de strategie Delivery Optimization qui
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.004|                    servaient a brider la vitesse de telechargement et
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.005|                    d'envoi des mises a jour. Les trois sont l'ecriture
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.006|                    d'avant Windows 10 version 2004.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.007|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.008|  Effet reel      : Les supprime si elles sont presentes, via l'assistant
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.009|                    killkey : une valeur qui n'a jamais existe est laissee
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.010|                    tranquille et rien n'est annonce. A cote de ca, le
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.011|                    code supprime separement DODownloadMode, mais
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.012|                    uniquement s'il vaut actuellement 100 (Bypass,
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.013|                    obsolete et connu pour casser les telechargements sur
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.014|                    24H2 et suivants) ou 99 (Simple, le mode hors ligne).
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.015|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.016|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable sur Windows 11, et c'est la
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.017|                    reponse honnete plutot que la reponse flatteuse. Ces
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.018|                    trois noms ont ete remplaces par les paires Background
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.019|                    et Foreground dans Windows 10 version 2004 : une
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.020|                    machine moderne les ignore tres probablement, ce qui
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.021|                    veut dire que les supprimer n'est pas ce qui debride
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.022|                    une mise a jour lente. C'est du nettoyage de residus :
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.023|                    ca retire des entrees de strategie perimees et ca
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.024|                    evite la confusion de voir dans le registre une limite
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.025|                    qui ne fait plus rien. Si vos mises a jour trainent
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.026|                    reellement, les valeurs a regarder sont
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.027|                    DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth et
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.028|                    DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, auxquelles OPTY ne
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.029|                    touche pas.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.030|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.031|  Cout            : Aucun, sur aucune machine. Si vous aviez
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.032|                    volontairement bride Delivery Optimization, sachez que
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.033|                    ces trois-la n'etaient sans doute pas ce qui
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.034|                    l'appliquait ; posez une limite fiable dans Parametres
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.035|                    > Windows Update > Options avancees > Optimisation de
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.036|                    la distribution > Options avancees.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.037|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.038|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois absentes, aucune limite.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.039|
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.040|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.041|    DELETE               : Supprimer les trois valeurs. Leur etat
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.042|                           d'origine est l'absence : c'est donc un vrai
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.043|                           retour a l'origine et non une reecriture. Sur
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.044|                           une machine qui ne les a jamais eues, killkey
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.045|                           verifie d'abord et ne fait strictement rien.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.046|    DOMaxDownloadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.047|        Un plafond absolu, en kilooctets par seconde, sur ce que Delivery
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.048|        Optimization peut telecharger ; 0 signifiait « sans limite ».
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.049|        C'est l'ecriture d'avant la version 2004 : Windows actuel lit
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.050|        plutot DOMaxBackgroundDownloadBandwidth et
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.051|        DOMaxForegroundDownloadBandwidth, donc sur Windows 11 ce nom est
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.052|        tres probablement inerte.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.053|    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth=1..100 :
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.054|        Un pourcentage de la vitesse de ligne mesuree. C'est la valeur
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.055|        qu'un outil d'optimisation met a 5 ou 10, apres quoi les mises a
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.056|        jour trainent des heures. Meme histoire : les noms actuels sont
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.057|        DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth et
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.058|        DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, et ce sont eux qui mordent
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.059|        aujourd'hui.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.060|    DOMaxUploadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.061|        Limite ce que ce PC envoie aux autres machines qui partagent le
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.062|        contenu des mises a jour. Ca n'a jamais touche vos
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.063|        telechargements, seulement votre contribution au cache entre
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.064|        pairs. Egalement remplacee : le controle pratique aujourd'hui,
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.065|        c'est DOMonthlyUploadDataCap et la page Delivery Optimization dans
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.066|        les Parametres.
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq DELETE identiques, et il n'y a vraiment rien
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|                         sur quoi diverger. Supprimer une valeur dont le
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.003|                         defaut est l'absence, c'est la meme action que
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.004|                         remettre Windows d'aplomb : WINDOWS arrive donc a
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.005|                         DELETE pour la meme raison que les quatre autres.
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.006|                         Le seul profil qui aurait un interet reel a une
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.007|                         limite d'envoi, c'est le serveur - une seedbox ne
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.008|                         veut pas que le partage des mises a jour lui
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.009|                         mange son lien montant - mais ces trois noms ne
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.010|                         sont pas ceux qui l'appliquent sur Windows 11 :
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.011|                         les garder reviendrait a proteger une limite qui
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.012|                         n'opere pas. Posez plutot la limite dans les
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.013|                         Parametres, ou elle est visible et a jour.
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.014|
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.015|  Non verifie (en)  : That DOMaxDownloadBandwidth,
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.016|                      DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth and
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.017|                      DOMaxUploadBandwidth are fully ignored by Delivery
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.018|                      Optimization on Windows 11 25H2 was not re-tested on
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.019|                      a live machine; the deprecation dates from Windows
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.020|                      10 version 2004 documentation. Deleting them is
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.021|                      correct either way, but the gain claim would need a
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.022|                      live measurement to become a claim rather than a
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.023|                      reading of the documentation.
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.024|
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.025|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DeliveryOptim
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.026|                    ization - OPTY.bat line 1967: for %%V in
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.027|                    (DOMaxDownloadBandwidth
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.028|                    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth DOMaxUploadBandwidth)
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.029|                    do call :killkey "%DOP%" "%%V". Lines 1964-1966
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.030|                    additionally delete DODownloadMode, but only when it
::X|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.031|                    currently reads 0x64 (100) or 0x63 (99).
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|CLEAR UPDATE BANDWIDTH CAP LEFTOVERS
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.003|  What it is      : Three Delivery Optimization policy values that were
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.004|                    the way to throttle how fast updates download and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.005|                    upload. All three are the pre-Windows-10-2004 spelling
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.006|                    of the setting.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.007|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes all three if present, using the killkey helper
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.009|                    so a value that was never there is left alone and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.010|                    nothing is reported. Alongside this, the code
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.011|                    separately deletes DODownloadMode but only when it
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.012|                    currently reads 100 (Bypass, deprecated and known to
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.013|                    break downloads on 24H2 and later) or 99 (Simple, the
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.014|                    offline mode).
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.015|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.016|  Gain            : No measurable gain on Windows 11, and this is the
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.017|                    honest answer rather than the flattering one. These
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.018|                    three names were superseded by the Background and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.019|                    Foreground pairs in Windows 10 version 2004, so a
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.020|                    modern machine most likely ignores them - which means
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.021|                    deleting them is not what un-throttles a slow update.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.022|                    It is leftover cleanup: it removes stale policy
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.023|                    entries and it removes the confusion of seeing a cap
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.024|                    in the registry that no longer does anything. If your
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.025|                    updates genuinely crawl, the values to look at are
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.026|                    DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.027|                    DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, which OPTY does
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.028|                    not touch.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.029|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.030|  Cost            : None on any machine. If you had deliberately capped
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.031|                    Delivery Optimization, note that these three probably
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.032|                    were not what was enforcing it anyway; set a cap you
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.033|                    can trust in Settings > Windows Update > Advanced
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.034|                    options > Delivery Optimization > Advanced options.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.035|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.036|  Windows default : All three absent, no cap.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.037|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.038|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.039|    DELETE               : Remove all three values. Their shipped state is
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.040|                           absence, so this is a true restore rather than
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.041|                           a rewrite. On a machine that never had them,
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.042|                           killkey checks first and does nothing at all.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.043|    DOMaxDownloadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.044|        An absolute ceiling in kilobytes per second on what Delivery
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.045|        Optimization may download; 0 meant unlimited. This is the pre-2004
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.046|        spelling of the setting - current Windows reads
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.047|        DOMaxBackgroundDownloadBandwidth and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.048|        DOMaxForegroundDownloadBandwidth instead, so on Windows 11 this
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.049|        name is most likely inert.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.050|    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth=1..100 :
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.051|        A percentage of the measured link speed. This is the value a
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.052|        tweaking tool sets to 5 or 10, after which updates crawl for
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.053|        hours. Same story as above: the current names are
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.054|        DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth and
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.055|        DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, and those are the ones that
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.056|        bite today.
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.057|    DOMaxUploadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.058|        Caps what this PC uploads to other machines sharing update
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.059|        content. It never affected your downloads, only your contribution
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.060|        to the peer cache. Also superseded, and today the practical
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.061|        control is DOMonthlyUploadDataCap plus the Delivery Optimization
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.062|        page in Settings.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, and there is genuinely
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|                       nothing to split on. Deleting a value whose default
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.003|                       is absence is the same action as restoring Windows,
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.004|                       so WINDOWS lands on DELETE for the same reason the
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.005|                       other four do. The one profile with a real interest
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.006|                       in an upload cap is the server - a seedbox does not
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.007|                       want update peering eating its uplink - but these
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.008|                       three names are not the ones enforcing that on
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.009|                       Windows 11, so keeping them would be protecting a
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.010|                       cap that is not operating. Set the cap in Settings
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.011|                       instead, where it is visible and current.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.012|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.013|  Unverified      : That DOMaxDownloadBandwidth,
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.014|                    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.015|                    DOMaxUploadBandwidth are fully ignored by Delivery
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.016|                    Optimization on Windows 11 25H2 was not re-tested on a
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.017|                    live machine; the deprecation dates from Windows 10
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.018|                    version 2004 documentation. Deleting them is correct
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.019|                    either way, but the gain claim would need a live
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.020|                    measurement to become a claim rather than a reading of
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.021|                    the documentation.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.022|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.023|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DeliveryOptim
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.024|                    ization - OPTY.bat line 1967: for %%V in
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.025|                    (DOMaxDownloadBandwidth
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.026|                    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth DOMaxUploadBandwidth)
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.027|                    do call :killkey "%DOP%" "%%V". Lines 1964-1966
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.028|                    additionally delete DODownloadMode, but only when it
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.029|                    currently reads 0x64 (100) or 0x63 (99).
::
:: ---- helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging (risky) -----------------
::P|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging|RUN|RUN|RUN|RUN|SKIP|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.001|SUPPRIMER LES DOSSIERS DE MISE A NIVEAU WINDOWS
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.002|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.003|  Ce que c est    : $WINDOWS.~BT, $Windows.~WS et $WinREAgent a la racine
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.004|                    de chaque disque fixe. Ce sont les dossiers de travail
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.005|                    d'une mise a niveau de Windows sur place et de la
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.006|                    maintenance de WinRE. Windows les pose sur le volume
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.007|                    qu'il a choisi, pas toujours C:, d'ou un balayage
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.008|                    disque par disque.
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.009|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.010|  Effet reel      : rd /S /Q sur chacun, sans condition des qu'il est
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.011|                    present. Libere typiquement plusieurs Go. Ca supprime
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.012|                    aussi ce dont depend l'option « Revenir a la version
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.013|                    precedente de Windows », la ou elle est encore
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.014|                    disponible.
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.015|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.016|  Gain            : Une grosse recuperation d'espace ponctuelle apres une
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.017|                    mise a niveau reussie ou ratee, ou ces dossiers ne
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.018|                    sont plus que des restes sans aucune utilite. Sur un
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.019|                    portable de 256 Go, quelques Go font la difference
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.020|                    entre un disque capable d'encaisser la prochaine mise
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.021|                    a jour et un disque qui n'y arrive pas.
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.022|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.023|  Cout            : Le vrai risque, c'est le moment : rien ne protege
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.024|                    contre une mise a niveau actuellement preparee et en
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.025|                    attente de redemarrage. Supprimez ces dossiers en
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.026|                    pleine mise a niveau et Windows retelecharge toute la
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.027|                    mise a jour de fonctionnalite - plusieurs Go et une
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.028|                    longue attente. Sur une machine qui vient de faire une
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.029|                    mise a niveau, ca supprime aussi le retour arriere de
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.030|                    10 jours, deliberément et sur instruction explicite du
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.031|                    mainteneur.
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.032|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.033|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - ce sont des dossiers de preparation
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.034|                    temporaires. Windows supprime normalement $WINDOWS.~BT
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.035|                    lui-meme une dizaine de jours apres une mise a niveau,
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.036|                    et $WinREAgent une fois la maintenance de WinRE
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.037|                    terminee.
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.038|
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.039|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.040|    RUN                  : rd /S /Q sur chaque dossier present, sur tous
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.041|                           les disques fixes, sans autre condition que
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.042|                           l'existence. $WINDOWS.~BT et $Windows.~WS sont
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.043|                           les dossiers de travail qu'une mise a niveau
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.044|                           sur place utilise pour preparer la nouvelle
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.045|                           version ; $WinREAgent est l'espace de travail
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.046|                           de la maintenance de WinRE. Plusieurs Go en
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.047|                           general. Les trois se recreent tout seuls a la
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.048|                           prochaine occasion, ce qui leur fait passer le
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.049|                           test « temps de regeneration » du mainteneur -
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.050|                           ce qui ne se recree pas, c'est le chemin «
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.051|                           Revenir a la version precedente de Windows ».
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.052|    SKIP                 : Les laisser. Windows supprime $WINDOWS.~BT et
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.053|                           $Windows.~WS tout seul une dizaine de jours
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.054|                           apres une mise a niveau, une fois la fenetre de
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.055|                           retour arriere fermee, et $WinREAgent une fois
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.056|                           la maintenance de WinRE terminee. Ne rien faire
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.057|                           vous coute la place disque pendant ces dix
::T|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.058|                           jours, et rien d'autre.
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre profils l'executent, et pour la meme
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.002|                         raison, qui n'a rien a voir avec la performance :
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.003|                         plusieurs Go de donnees de preparation mortes
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.004|                         valent autant sur une machine de jeu, un serveur
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.005|                         multimedia, un poste bureautique ou un portable,
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.006|                         et selon la regle du mainteneur lui-meme - ca
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.007|                         revient tout seul, aucune donnee utilisateur
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.008|                         n'est perdue - ces dossiers sont dans le
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.009|                         perimetre. WINDOWS fait exception, et pour de bon
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.010|                         : le comportement d'origine est de les laisser et
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.011|                         de laisser Windows les effacer vers le dixieme
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.012|                         jour, ce qui est une vraie reponse differente et
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.013|                         non un remplissage. A noter : le profil n'est pas
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.014|                         le vrai garde-fou ici. Le garde-fou, c'est l'etat
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.015|                         de la machine - entierement a jour, redemarree
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.016|                         depuis, plus de 10 jours apres une mise a niveau
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.017|                         - et OPTY n'en teste aucun, d'ou la question
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.018|                         explicite sans reponse par defaut.
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.019|
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.020|  Problemes connus : Concret et reproductible : supprimez $WINDOWS.~BT
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.021|                     alors qu'une mise a jour de fonctionnalite est
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.022|                     telechargee et attend un redemarrage, et le bouton «
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.023|                     Redemarrer maintenant » de Parametres > Windows
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.024|                     Update ne termine plus la mise a niveau - Windows
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.025|                     Update relance le telechargement de plusieurs
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.026|                     gigaoctets depuis zero, sans aucun message expliquant
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.027|                     pourquoi. Par ailleurs, Parametres > Systeme >
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.028|                     Recuperation perd le bouton « Revenir en arriere »,
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.029|                     qui affiche alors « Cette option n'est plus
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.030|                     disponible sur ce PC ». Un garde-fou peu couteux
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.031|                     existe et n'est pas implemente : sauter les trois
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.032|                     suppressions quand HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Cu
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.033|                     rrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.034|                     ou ...\Component Based Servicing\RebootPending est
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.035|                     present.
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.036|
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.037|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether removing $WinREAgent while a WinRE servicing
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.038|                      update is mid-flight causes that update to fail
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.039|                      rather than simply restart was not reproduced. The
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.040|                      claim made here is the conservative one: it starts
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.041|                      over.
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.042|
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.043|  Cible           : OPTY.bat :drivesweep, lines 3231-3233, called once per
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.044|                    fixed drive from the FIXEDLIST loop: if exist
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.045|                    "%~1:\$WINDOWS.~BT" rd /S /Q ... ; if exist
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.046|                    "%~1:\$Windows.~WS" rd /S /Q ... ; if exist
::X|FR|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.047|                    "%~1:\$WinREAgent" rd /S /Q ...
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.001|REMOVE FEATURE-UPDATE STAGING FOLDERS
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.002|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.003|  What it is      : $WINDOWS.~BT, $Windows.~WS and $WinREAgent at the root
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.004|                    of every fixed drive. They are the working folders for
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.005|                    an in-place Windows upgrade and for WinRE servicing.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.006|                    Windows puts them on whichever volume it picked, not
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.007|                    always C:, which is why the sweep runs per drive.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.008|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.009|  Actual effect   : rd /S /Q on each of them, unconditionally when
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.010|                    present. Typically frees several GB. It also removes
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.011|                    what the 'Go back to the previous version of Windows'
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.012|                    option depends on, where that option is still
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.013|                    available.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.014|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.015|  Gain            : A large one-off space recovery after a completed or
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.016|                    failed feature update, where these folders are pure
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.017|                    leftovers with no further purpose. On a 256 GB laptop
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.018|                    several GB is the difference between a disk that can
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.019|                    take the next update and one that cannot.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.020|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.021|  Cost            : The real risk is timing: there is no guard against an
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.022|                    upgrade that is currently staged and waiting for a
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.023|                    reboot. Delete these mid-upgrade and Windows re-
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.024|                    downloads the entire feature update - several GB and a
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.025|                    long wait. On a machine that has just taken a feature
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.026|                    update, it also removes the 10-day rollback path,
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.027|                    deliberately and on the maintainer's explicit
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.028|                    instruction.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.029|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.030|  Windows default : Not applicable - these are transient staging folders.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.031|                    Windows normally removes $WINDOWS.~BT itself about 10
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.032|                    days after an upgrade, and $WinREAgent once WinRE
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.033|                    servicing completes.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.034|
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.035|  Possible values:
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.036|    RUN                  : rd /S /Q on each folder that exists, on every
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.037|                           fixed drive, with no condition beyond
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.038|                           existence. $WINDOWS.~BT and $Windows.~WS are
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.039|                           the working folders an in-place upgrade uses to
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.040|                           stage the new build; $WinREAgent is scratch
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.041|                           space for WinRE servicing. Typically several
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.042|                           GB. All three regenerate on their own the next
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.043|                           time they are needed, which is why they pass
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.044|                           the maintainer's regeneration-time test - what
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.045|                           they do not regenerate is the Go back to the
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.046|                           previous version of Windows path.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.047|    SKIP                 : Leave them. Windows removes $WINDOWS.~BT and
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.048|                           $Windows.~WS by itself about ten days after an
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.049|                           upgrade, once the rollback window closes, and
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.050|                           $WinREAgent once WinRE servicing finishes.
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.051|                           Doing nothing costs you the disk space for
::T|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.052|                           those ten days and nothing else.
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles run it and they do so for the same
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.002|                       reason, which is not a performance reason: several
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.003|                       GB of dead staging data is worth the same on a
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.004|                       gaming rig, a media server, an office desktop and a
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.005|                       laptop, and by the maintainer's own rule - it comes
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.006|                       back on its own and loses no user data - these
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.007|                       folders are in scope. WINDOWS is the odd one out
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.008|                       and genuinely so: the shipped behaviour is to leave
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.009|                       them and let Windows clear them itself around day
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.010|                       ten, which is a real, different answer rather than
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.011|                       a placeholder. Note that the profile is not the
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.012|                       actual gate here. The gate is machine state - fully
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.013|                       updated, rebooted since, more than 10 days since a
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.014|                       feature update - and OPTY does not test any of it,
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.015|                       which is why this step keeps its explicit no-
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.016|                       default question.
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.017|
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.018|  Known problems  : Concrete and reproducible: delete $WINDOWS.~BT while a
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.019|                    feature update is downloaded and waiting for a
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.020|                    restart, and the 'Restart now' button in Settings >
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.021|                    Windows Update no longer completes the upgrade -
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.022|                    Windows Update starts the multi-gigabyte download
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.023|                    again from zero, with no message explaining why.
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.024|                    Separately, Settings > System > Recovery loses the Go
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.025|                    back button, which then reads 'This option is no
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.026|                    longer available on this PC'. A cheap guard exists and
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.027|                    is not implemented: skip the three deletes when HKLM\S
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.028|                    OFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.029|                    \Auto Update\RebootRequired or ...\Component Based
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.030|                    Servicing\RebootPending is present.
::X|EN|helper.drivesweep.upgrade.staging.031|
