:: ---- powercfg.ultimate.create (preference) --------------------------
::P|powercfg.ultimate.create|ACTIVATE|CREATE|SKIP|SKIP|DELETE|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|LE MODE D'ALIMENTATION MASQUE PERFORMANCES ULTIMES
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.003|  Ce que c est    : Un mode d'alimentation que Microsoft livre mais masque
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.004|                    sur la plupart des editions grand public. Il cloue
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.005|                    l'etat minimal du processeur a 100 pour cent,
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.006|                    desactive le parcage des coeurs et ne met jamais le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.007|                    disque au repos. Il ne desactive pas les etats de
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.008|                    repos du CPU. OPTY duplique le modele sous un GUID
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.009|                    fixe, NE l'active PAS, puis ouvre powercfg.cpl pour
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.010|                    que vous choisissiez vous-meme.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.011|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.012|  Effet reel      : powercfg -duplicatescheme cree le mode, qui apparait
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.013|                    dans la liste des options d'alimentation. Sur les
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.014|                    builds ou le modele est masque ou absent, la commande
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.015|                    echoue simplement et OPTY l'annonce comme indisponible
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.016|                    plutot que de faire semblant. Le GUID de destination
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.017|                    fixe a son importance : une version anterieure
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.018|                    cherchait le GUID du modele, qui n'apparait jamais
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.019|                    dans powercfg /list puisque chaque copie recoit un
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.020|                    GUID aleatoire neuf - le garde-fou ne se declenchait
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.021|                    donc jamais, et cette machine s'est retrouvee avec
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.022|                    trois modes Performances ultimes identiques.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.023|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.024|  Gain            : Si vous le selectionnez ensuite, le comportement
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.025|                    d'inactivite s'arrete : aucun coeur n'est jamais
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.026|                    parque, donc rien n'a besoin d'etre deparque avant de
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.027|                    tourner, et le CPU ne descend jamais sous sa frequence
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.028|                    de base. C'est le mecanisme etaye, et c'est un
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.029|                    mecanisme de latence. Ce qui n'est pas etaye, c'est
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.030|                    l'affirmation habituelle : aucune mesure publiee ne
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.031|                    montre Performances ultimes battant Utilisation
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.032|                    normale avec le curseur de mode d'alimentation sur
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.033|                    Performances optimales, ni en images par seconde ni en
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.034|                    1 % low, sur un fixe moderne. Les cas qui tiennent
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.035|                    debout sont l'audio temps reel et les charges
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.036|                    sensibles a la latence de deparcage des coeurs. Se
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.037|                    contenter de creer le mode n'apporte strictement rien.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.038|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.039|  Cout            : Le creer ne coute qu'une ligne dans une liste. Le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.040|                    selectionner coute une consommation au repos continue,
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.041|                    de la chaleur, du bruit de ventilateur, et une
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.042|                    autonomie nettement reduite sur portable - et sur un
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.043|                    portable limite thermiquement, cette chaleur peut
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.044|                    rendre la machine plus lente, pas plus rapide. Le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.045|                    disque qui ne se repose jamais est un petit cout
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.046|                    supplementaire sur une machine a disques mecaniques.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.047|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.048|  Defaut Windows  : Le mode n'existe pas. Il est masque sur la plupart des
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.049|                    editions grand public, et sur certains systemes sur
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.050|                    batterie le modele n'est meme pas expose, auquel cas
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.051|                    duplicatescheme echoue.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.052|
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.053|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.054|    CREATE               : powercfg -duplicatescheme copie le modele
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.055|                           masque Performances ultimes vers le GUID fixe
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.056|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. Le mode
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.057|                           apparait dans les Options d'alimentation et
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.058|                           rien d'autre ne change : votre mode actif, vos
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.059|                           frequences et votre courbe de ventilateur
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.060|                           restent intacts tant que vous ne le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.061|                           selectionnez pas. Le gain de la seule creation
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.062|                           est nul : ca ajoute une ligne dans une liste.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.063|    ACTIVATE             : Le creer, puis powercfg /setactive
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.064|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. La, il
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.065|                           fait quelque chose : l'etat minimal du
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.066|                           processeur est cloue a 100 pour cent, le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.067|                           parcage des coeurs est desactive donc aucun
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.068|                           coeur n'a jamais a etre deparque a la demande,
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.069|                           et le disque ne se met jamais au repos. Notez
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.070|                           ce qu'il ne fait PAS : il ne desactive pas les
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.071|                           etats de repos du CPU - c'est le forcage
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.072|                           PROCIDLEDISABLE, separe, et la carte rad-pwr-
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.073|                           idledisable explique pourquoi vous n'en voulez
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.074|                           pas.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.075|    SKIP                 : Ne pas le creer. La liste des Options
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.076|                           d'alimentation reste telle que Windows l'a
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.077|                           livree. Sur une machine qui ne selectionnera
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.078|                           jamais ce mode, c'est la reponse qui laisse le
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.079|                           moins de traces.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.080|    DELETE               : powercfg -delete
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.081|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f retire une
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.082|                           copie creee par une execution precedente. La
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.083|                           commande echoue si ce mode est actif : basculez
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.084|                           d'abord sur Utilisation normale avec powercfg
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.085|                           /setactive
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.086|                           381b4222-f694-41f0-9685-ff5bb260df2e, puis
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.087|                           supprimez.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Gaming est le seul profil a l'activer, et pour la
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|                         bonne raison plutot que pour la raison populaire
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.003|                         : ce profil est defini par la latence, le seul
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.004|                         effet etaye du mode est la suppression de la
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.005|                         latence de deparcage des coeurs, et un fixe sur
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.006|                         secteur a deja accepte le cout en consommation au
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.007|                         repos. N'en attendez pas des images par seconde.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.008|                         Serveur le cree mais reste dessus : sous charge
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.009|                         soutenue, Windows deparque de toute facon tous
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.010|                         les coeurs, donc une machine de debit allumee en
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.011|                         permanence n'en tire rien tout en payant la
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.012|                         consommation au repos jour et nuit - avoir
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.013|                         l'option sous la main ne coute rien, en revanche.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.014|                         Bureau et Portable le sautent entierement : un PC
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.015|                         familial silencieux n'y gagne rien, et un
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.016|                         portable doit rester sur Utilisation normale,
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.017|                         branche ou non. Windows repond DELETE, parce
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.018|                         qu'une machine livree n'a pas ce mode du tout -
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.019|                         et pensez a basculer sur Utilisation normale
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.020|                         avant de supprimer, sinon powercfg refuse.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.021|
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.022|  Problemes connus : Avant le garde-fou a GUID fixe de la ligne 2225,
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.023|                     chaque execution creait un nouveau mode Performances
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.024|                     ultimes : cette machine en a accumule trois
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.025|                     identiques. C'est corrige, mais une machine qui a
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.026|                     fait tourner l'ancienne version a toujours ses
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.027|                     doublons, et il faut les retirer a la main avec
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.028|                     powercfg -delete sur chaque GUID en trop releve dans
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.029|                     powercfg /list.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.030|
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.031|  Non verifie (en)  : I can describe what the scheme sets, but I have no
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.032|                      published measurement showing Ultimate Performance
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.033|                      beating Balanced-at-Best-Performance in frame rate
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.034|                      or 1% lows on modern hardware. The core-parking
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.035|                      wake-latency argument is a mechanism, not a
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.036|                      benchmark: I cannot tell you how many microseconds
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.037|                      it is worth on a given CPU, and on a desktop that is
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.038|                      already at the Best Performance slider setting the
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.039|                      practical difference may well be zero.
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.040|
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.041|  Cible           : powercfg -duplicatescheme
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.042|                    e9a42b02-d5df-448d-aa00-03f14749eb61
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.043|                    9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f - OPTY.bat line
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.044|                    2227, guarded by a findstr on ULTGUID over powercfg
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.045|                    /list at line 2225; powercfg.cpl is opened at line
::X|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.046|                    2242 so the user chooses the plan themselves
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|THE HIDDEN ULTIMATE PERFORMANCE POWER PLAN
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.003|  What it is      : A power scheme Microsoft ships but hides on most
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.004|                    consumer SKUs. It pins the minimum processor state at
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.005|                    100 percent, disables core parking, and never idles
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.006|                    the disk. It does not disable CPU idle states. OPTY
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.007|                    duplicates the template to a fixed GUID, does NOT
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.008|                    switch to it, then opens powercfg.cpl so you choose
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.009|                    for yourself.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.010|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.011|  Actual effect   : powercfg -duplicatescheme creates the plan and it
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.012|                    shows up in the power options list. On builds where
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.013|                    the template is hidden or absent the command simply
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.014|                    fails and OPTY reports it as unavailable rather than
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.015|                    pretending. The fixed destination GUID matters: an
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.016|                    earlier version searched for the template GUID, which
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.017|                    never appears in powercfg /list because each copy gets
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.018|                    a fresh random GUID, so the guard never fired and this
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.019|                    machine ended up with three identical Ultimate
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.020|                    Performance plans.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.021|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.022|  Gain            : If you then select it, background-idle behaviour
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.023|                    stops: no core is ever parked, so nothing has to be
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.024|                    unparked before it can run, and the CPU never drops
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.025|                    below its base clock. That is the substantiated
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.026|                    mechanism, and it is a latency mechanism. What is not
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.027|                    substantiated is the usual claim: no published
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.028|                    measurement shows Ultimate Performance beating
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.029|                    Balanced with the Settings power-mode slider at Best
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.030|                    Performance in frame rate or 1% lows on a modern
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.031|                    desktop. The cases with a real argument are real-time
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.032|                    audio and workloads sensitive to core-parking wake
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.033|                    latency. Merely creating the plan gains nothing at
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.034|                    all.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.035|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.036|  Cost            : Creating it costs nothing but a line in a list.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.037|                    Selecting it costs continuous idle power, heat and fan
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.038|                    noise, and noticeably shorter battery life on a laptop
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.039|                    - and on a thermally limited laptop that heat can make
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.040|                    the machine slower, not faster. The disk never idling
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.041|                    is its own small cost on a box with spinning drives.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.042|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.043|  Windows default : The scheme is not present. It is hidden on most
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.044|                    consumer SKUs, and on some battery-powered systems the
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.045|                    template is not exposed at all, in which case
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.046|                    duplicatescheme fails.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.047|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.048|  Possible values:
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.049|    CREATE               : powercfg -duplicatescheme copies the hidden
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.050|                           Ultimate Performance template to the fixed GUID
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.051|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. The plan
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.052|                           appears in Power Options and nothing else
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.053|                           changes - your active plan, your clocks and
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.054|                           your fan curve are untouched until you select
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.055|                           it yourself. The gain from creating it, on its
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.056|                           own, is zero: it adds one line to a list.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.057|    ACTIVATE             : Create it, then powercfg /setactive
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.058|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. Now it
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.059|                           does something: minimum processor state is
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.060|                           pinned at 100 percent, core parking is off so
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.061|                           no core is ever unparked-on-demand, and the
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.062|                           disk never idles down. Note what it does NOT
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.063|                           do: it does not disable CPU idle states - that
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.064|                           is the separate PROCIDLEDISABLE override, and
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.065|                           the card rad-pwr-idledisable explains why you
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.066|                           do not want it.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.067|    SKIP                 : Do not create it. The Power Options list stays
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.068|                           as Windows shipped it. On a machine that will
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.069|                           never select the plan, this is the answer that
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.070|                           leaves the least behind.
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.071|    DELETE               : powercfg -delete
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.072|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f removes a
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.073|                           copy a previous run created. It fails if that
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.074|                           plan is currently active, so switch to Balanced
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.075|                           first: powercfg /setactive
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.076|                           381b4222-f694-41f0-9685-ff5bb260df2e, then
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.077|                           delete.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|  Why these profiles : Gaming is the only profile that activates it, and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|                       for the honest reason rather than the popular one:
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.003|                       the profile is defined by latency, the plan's one
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.004|                       substantiated effect is removing core-parking wake
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.005|                       latency, and a mains desktop has already accepted
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.006|                       the idle-power cost. Do not expect frames from it.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.007|                       Server creates it but stays off it: under sustained
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.008|                       load Windows unparks every core anyway, so a 24/7
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.009|                       throughput box gets nothing from the plan while
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.010|                       paying idle power around the clock - having the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.011|                       option on hand costs nothing, though. Office and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.012|                       Laptop skip it entirely: a quiet family PC gains
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.013|                       nothing, and a laptop should be on Balanced whether
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.014|                       or not it is plugged in. Windows answers DELETE,
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.015|                       because a shipped machine does not have this scheme
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.016|                       at all - and remember to switch to Balanced before
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.017|                       deleting, or powercfg refuses.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.018|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.019|  Known problems  : Before the fixed-GUID guard at line 2225, repeated
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.020|                    runs created a new Ultimate Performance plan every
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.021|                    time - this machine accumulated three identical
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.022|                    copies. That is fixed, but a machine that ran the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.023|                    older build still has the duplicates, and they must be
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.024|                    removed by hand with powercfg -delete on each stray
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.025|                    GUID from powercfg /list.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.026|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.027|  Unverified      : I can describe what the scheme sets, but I have no
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.028|                    published measurement showing Ultimate Performance
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.029|                    beating Balanced-at-Best-Performance in frame rate or
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.030|                    1% lows on modern hardware. The core-parking wake-
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.031|                    latency argument is a mechanism, not a benchmark: I
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.032|                    cannot tell you how many microseconds it is worth on a
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.033|                    given CPU, and on a desktop that is already at the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.034|                    Best Performance slider setting the practical
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.035|                    difference may well be zero.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.036|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.037|  Target          : powercfg -duplicatescheme
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.038|                    e9a42b02-d5df-448d-aa00-03f14749eb61
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.039|                    9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f - OPTY.bat line
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.040|                    2227, guarded by a findstr on ULTGUID over powercfg
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.041|                    /list at line 2225; powercfg.cpl is opened at line
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.042|                    2242 so the user chooses the plan themselves
::
:: ---- rp.throttle.window (repair) --------------------------------
::P|rp.throttle.window|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.001|LA FENETRE DE 24 H DES POINTS DE RESTAURATION, VUE DES CHEMINS SETUP ET REPARATION
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.002|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.003|  Ce que c est    : Windows refuse un deuxieme point de restauration dans
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.004|                    les 1440 minutes qui suivent le precedent.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.005|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency contient cette
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.006|                    fenetre en minutes. OPTY la leve a 0 le temps de son
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.007|                    propre point, puis la remet.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.008|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.009|  Effet reel      : Avec le verrou a 0, le point d'OPTY est cree meme si
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.010|                    Windows Update ou un autre outil en a deja fait un
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.011|                    aujourd'hui. Ce que vous decidez ici, c'est uniquement
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.012|                    ce qui reste apres le point : un 1440 explicite, ou
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.013|                    rien du tout.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.014|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.015|  Gain            : Le point devient fiable au lieu d'etre un coup de des.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.016|                    Sans ca, il echoue en silence exactement les jours ou
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.017|                    vous lancez le script deux fois - c'est-a-dire les
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.018|                    jours ou vous avez le plus besoin d'un retour en
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.019|                    arriere.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.020|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.021|  Cout            : Aucun effet sur le comportement. A 1440 la valeur ne
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.022|                    fait rien ; absente, elle ne fait rien non plus. Le
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.023|                    seul vrai risque, c'est qu'elle reste a 0 si
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.024|                    l'execution est interrompue entre les deux lignes, ce
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.025|                    qui desactive le garde-fou de 24 h de la Protection du
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.026|                    systeme pour toute la machine, pas seulement pour
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.027|                    OPTY.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.028|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.029|  Defaut Windows  : La valeur est absente, et Windows applique alors une
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.030|                    fenetre de 1440 minutes.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.031|
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.033|    0                    : Aucun verrou. N'importe quel appelant peut
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.034|                           creer un point de restauration a tout moment.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.035|                           C'est l'etat dont OPTY a besoin pendant les
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.036|                           deux secondes qui entourent son appel a
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.037|                           Checkpoint-Computer, et l'etat dans lequel il
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.038|                           ne faut pas le laisser.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.039|    1440                 : La fenetre de 24 h, ecrite explicitement.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.040|                           Comportement identique a l'etat livre, mais une
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.041|                           valeur presente la ou une machine d'usine n'a
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.042|                           rien.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.043|    DELETE               : Valeur absente, c'est-a-dire l'etat d'usine,
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.044|                           que Windows traite comme 1440 minutes. Meme
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.045|                           comportement, aucun residu.
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.046|    any integer N        : Une fenetre personnalisee en minutes. Ecrite
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.047|                           uniquement par un script ou a la main - aucune
::T|FR|rp.throttle.window.048|                           boite de dialogue Windows ne la propose.
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques, parce qu'il n'y a
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.002|                         vraiment rien de dependant du profil dans un
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.003|                         point de restauration. DELETE plutot que 1440
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.004|                         pour la meme raison que dans reg-srp-creation-
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.005|                         frequency : absente signifie deja 1440, donc
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.006|                         supprimer donne le comportement d'origine sans
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.007|                         rien laisser dans le registre. Note
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.008|                         d'implementation plus importante que la reponse
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.009|                         elle-meme : cette carte et reg-srp-creation-
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.010|                         frequency decrivent le meme DWORD, ecrit par les
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.011|                         deux memes lignes voisines de :restore_point. Il
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.012|                         faut une seule question. En poser deux, c'est
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.013|                         inviter deux reponses differentes pour une seule
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.014|                         valeur, et c'est la seconde ecriture qui
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.015|                         l'emporte en silence.
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.016|
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.017|  Problemes connus : Sur cette machine, la valeur vaut actuellement 0 :
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.018|                     une execution precedente a ecrit 0 sans jamais
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.019|                     retablir le verrou. Toutes les applications qui
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.020|                     creent des points de restauration sont dereglementees
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.021|                     ici depuis.
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.022|
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.023|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.024|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.025|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency (REG_DWORD) - the
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.026|                    SAME value and the SAME two lines as reg-srp-creation-
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.027|                    frequency: OPTY.bat line 3622 writes 0, line 3631
::X|FR|rp.throttle.window.028|                    writes the throttle back, both inside :restore_point
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.001|THE 24H RESTORE-POINT WINDOW, SEEN FROM THE SETUP AND REPAIR PATHS
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.002|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.003|  What it is      : Windows refuses a second System Restore point within
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.004|                    1440 minutes of the previous one.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.005|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency holds that window
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.006|                    in minutes. OPTY lifts it to 0 for the duration of its
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.007|                    own checkpoint and puts it back afterwards.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.008|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.009|  Actual effect   : With the throttle at 0, OPTY's restore point is
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.010|                    created even if Windows Update or another tool already
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.011|                    made one today. What you decide here is only what is
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.012|                    left behind after the checkpoint: an explicit 1440, or
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.013|                    nothing at all.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.014|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.015|  Gain            : The checkpoint is reliable instead of a coin flip.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.016|                    Without it, the restore point fails silently on
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.017|                    exactly the days you run the script twice - which are
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.018|                    the days you most want a way back.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.019|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.020|  Cost            : Nothing behavioural. At 1440 the value has no effect
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.021|                    whatsoever; absent, it has no effect either. The only
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.022|                    real risk is the value being left at 0 if the run is
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.023|                    interrupted between the two lines, which disables
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.024|                    System Protection's 24-hour guard for everything on
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.025|                    the machine, not just OPTY.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.026|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.027|  Windows default : The value is absent, and Windows then applies a
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.028|                    1440-minute window.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.029|
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.031|    0                    : No throttle. Any caller can create a restore
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.032|                           point at any time. This is the state OPTY needs
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.033|                           for the two seconds around its Checkpoint-
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.034|                           Computer call, and the state you do not want it
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.035|                           left in.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.036|    1440                 : The 24-hour window, written explicitly.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.037|                           Identical behaviour to the shipped state, but a
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.038|                           value present where a factory machine has
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.039|                           nothing.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.040|    DELETE               : Value absent, which is the factory state and
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.041|                           which Windows treats as 1440 minutes. Same
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.042|                           behaviour, no residue.
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.043|    any integer N        : A custom window in minutes. Only ever written
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.044|                           by a script or by hand - no Windows dialog
::T|EN|rp.throttle.window.045|                           offers it.
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers, because there is genuinely
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.002|                       nothing profile-dependent about a restore point.
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.003|                       DELETE rather than 1440 for the same reason as reg-
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.004|                       srp-creation-frequency: absent already means 1440,
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.005|                       so deleting gives the shipped behaviour with
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.006|                       nothing left in the registry. Implementation note
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.007|                       that matters more than the answer itself: this card
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.008|                       and reg-srp-creation-frequency describe the same
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.009|                       DWORD written by the same two adjacent lines of
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.010|                       :restore_point. They should be one question. Asking
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.011|                       twice invites two different answers for one value,
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.012|                       and the second write silently wins.
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.013|
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.014|  Known problems  : On this machine the value currently reads 0, so a
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.015|                    previous run wrote 0 and never restored the throttle.
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.016|                    Every application that creates restore points has been
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.017|                    unthrottled here ever since.
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.018|
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.019|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.020|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.021|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency (REG_DWORD) - the
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.022|                    SAME value and the SAME two lines as reg-srp-creation-
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.023|                    frequency: OPTY.bat line 3622 writes 0, line 3631
::X|EN|rp.throttle.window.024|                    writes the throttle back, both inside :restore_point
::
:: ---- mmcss.network.throttling (preference) --------------------------
::P|mmcss.network.throttling|10|4294967295|10|10|10|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.001|LE PLAFOND RESEAU MMCSS PENDANT LA LECTURE AUDIO
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.002|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.003|  Ce que c est    : NetworkThrottlingIndex limite le nombre de paquets
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.004|                    reseau traites par milliseconde - mais uniquement tant
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.005|                    qu'un flux multimedia ou audio enregistre aupres de
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.006|                    MMCSS est en lecture. Il existe pour que le travail
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.007|                    d'interruption reseau n'affame pas les threads audio
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.008|                    et ne provoque pas de craquements. Quand rien ne joue,
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.009|                    le plafond ne s'applique pas et votre connexion n'est
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.010|                    pas concernee.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.011|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.012|  Effet reel      : 10 est la valeur d'origine et revient a environ
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.013|                    100-120 Mbit/s pendant la lecture. 4294967295
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.014|                    (0xFFFFFFFF) supprime completement le plafond, et la
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.015|                    protection avec.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.016|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.017|  Gain            : Strictement conditionne a deux choses simultanees :
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.018|                    votre lien depasse environ 100 Mbit/s, ET vous
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.019|                    transferez de gros volumes pendant qu'un son joue.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.020|                    Alors lever le plafond vous rend votre debit reel au
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.021|                    lieu d'un plafond invisible dont Windows ne vous dit
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.022|                    rien. C'est honnetement mesurable : lancez un gros
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.023|                    telechargement avec de la musique, avant et apres. Si
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.024|                    votre connexion est sous 100 Mbit/s, ou si vous ne
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.025|                    telechargez pas pendant la lecture, le gain est nul et
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.026|                    vous gardez le risque.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.027|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.028|  Cout            : Des coupures et des craquements audio sous forte
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.029|                    charge reseau, exactement la panne que ce plafond a
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.030|                    ete introduit pour eviter. Rien d'autre n'est touche :
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.031|                    ce n'est pas un reglage reseau general, il ne concerne
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.032|                    ni la latence, ni le trafic de jeu, ni quoi que ce
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.033|                    soit quand l'audio est silencieux.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.034|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.035|  Defaut Windows  : 10 (0xa), et fait rare pour une valeur de reglage,
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.036|                    elle est reellement presente par defaut sur une
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.037|                    installation propre de Windows 11 - ecrire 10 restaure
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.038|                    donc exactement l'etat livre, la supprimer non.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.039|
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.040|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.041|    10                   : 0xa, la valeur d'origine. Tant qu'un flux
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.042|                           multimedia enregistre aupres de MMCSS est en
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.043|                           lecture, le systeme traite environ 10 paquets
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.044|                           reseau par milliseconde ; avec des trames
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.045|                           standard de 1500 octets, ca revient a peu pres
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.046|                           a 100-120 Mbit/s. En dehors de la lecture
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.047|                           audio, le plafond ne s'applique pas du tout et
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.048|                           votre lien tourne a plein debit. C'est cette
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.049|                           valeur qui protege les threads audio d'etre
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.050|                           affames par le travail DPC reseau.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.051|    4294967295           : 0xFFFFFFFF - la valeur sentinelle documentee «
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.052|                           aucun bridage ». Le plafond disparait
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.053|                           entierement, et avec lui la protection dont
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.054|                           beneficiaient les threads audio face au travail
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.055|                           d'interruption reseau. Votre lien tourne a
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.056|                           plein debit pendant la lecture. Sur une machine
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.057|                           moderne multicoeur ou RSS repartit les DPC
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.058|                           reseau sur plusieurs coeurs, le risque de
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.059|                           famine est bien plus faible qu'a l'epoque ou le
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.060|                           plafond a ete concu, mais il n'est pas nul.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.061|    any integer between 1 and 69 :
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.062|        Un plafond intermediaire : le nombre est en paquets par
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.063|        milliseconde, donc 20 vaut deux fois le plafond d'origine et 70
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.064|        sept fois. C'est legal, mais il n'existe aucune facon raisonnee
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.065|        d'en choisir un - vous piocheriez un chiffre entre « la valeur
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.066|        testee par Microsoft » et « rien », sans moyen de mesurer ou vous
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.067|        avez atterri. Si vous quittez 10, allez a 0xFFFFFFFF et faites un
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.068|        vrai avant/apres.
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.069|    DELETE               : Valeur absente. Contrairement a la plupart des
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.070|                           reglages de ce script, l'absence n'est PAS
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.071|                           l'etat livre ici : une installation propre de
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.072|                           Windows 11 a bel et bien 10 en place. Supprimer
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.073|                           la valeur est un changement, pas une
::T|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.074|                           restauration - ecrivez 10 a la place.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : C'est un arbitrage pur entre debit et protection
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.002|                         de l'audio, et exactement un des cinq profils est
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.003|                         defini par le debit. Serveur leve le plafond :
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.004|                         une machine d'hebergement deplace de gros volumes
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.005|                         et n'a aucun audio interactif a proteger - sur
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.006|                         une machine vraiment sans ecran, le plafond ne
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.007|                         s'engage peut-etre jamais, donc le gain y est en
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.008|                         partie theorique, mais rien n'est perdu non plus.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.009|                         Gaming garde 10 volontairement, a contre-courant
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.010|                         du folklore : le profil gaming est defini par la
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.011|                         latence, un craquement audio EST une panne de
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.012|                         latence, et le trafic de jeu est un filet de
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.013|                         petits paquets UDP qui n'approche jamais les 10
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.014|                         paquets par milliseconde. Bureau et Portable
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.015|                         gardent 10 - aucun enjeu de debit, et le portable
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.016|                         est justement la machine qui fait des visios.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.017|                         Windows garde 10 parce que c'est la valeur
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.018|                         livree.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.019|
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.020|  Problemes connus : La panne concrete a 0xFFFFFFFF est celle que le
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.021|                     plafond existe pour eviter : la famine des threads
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.022|                     audio sous charge DPC reseau, rapportee par les
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.023|                     utilisateurs de MAO sous forme de sous-alimentation
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.024|                     des tampons ASIO et de clics dans Reaper et Cubase
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.025|                     pendant de gros transferts. Quiconque fait de la
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.026|                     musique doit rester a 10.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.027|
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.028|  Non verifie (en)  : The 100-120 Mbit/s figure follows from 10 packets/ms
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.029|                      at 1500-byte frames, but the effective ceiling
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.030|                      depends on your actual frame size and offload
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.031|                      settings, so treat it as an order of magnitude
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.032|                      rather than a number. I also cannot tell you how
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.033|                      often audio starvation actually occurs at 0xFFFFFFFF
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.034|                      on a modern multi-core machine with RSS enabled -
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.035|                      the mechanism is real and documented, the frequency
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.036|                      is not something I have a measurement for.
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.037|
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.038|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.039|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile /v
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.040|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex (REG_DWORD) - written as 10 at
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.041|                    OPTY.bat lines 1520, 1599, 1787, 2842 and 2854;
::X|FR|mmcss.network.throttling.042|                    written as 4294967295 at line 2860 in :net_prof_thr
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.001|THE MMCSS NETWORK CAP DURING AUDIO PLAYBACK
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.002|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.003|  What it is      : NetworkThrottlingIndex caps how many network packets
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.004|                    per millisecond the system processes - but only while
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.005|                    an MMCSS-registered multimedia or audio stream is
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.006|                    playing. It exists so network interrupt work cannot
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.007|                    starve audio threads and produce crackling. When
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.008|                    nothing is playing, the cap does not apply and your
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.009|                    connection is unaffected.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.010|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.011|  Actual effect   : 10 is the shipped value and works out at roughly
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.012|                    100-120 Mbit/s during playback. 4294967295
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.013|                    (0xFFFFFFFF) removes the ceiling entirely, and removes
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.014|                    the protection along with it.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.015|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.016|  Gain            : Strictly conditional on two things being true at once:
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.017|                    your link is faster than about 100 Mbit/s, AND you
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.018|                    transfer large amounts of data while audio is playing.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.019|                    Then lifting the cap gives you your real line rate
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.020|                    instead of a soft ceiling nothing in Windows tells you
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.021|                    about. It is honestly measurable - run a large
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.022|                    download with music playing, before and after. If your
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.023|                    connection is under 100 Mbit/s, or you do not download
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.024|                    during playback, the gain is zero and you keep the
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.025|                    risk.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.026|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.027|  Cost            : Audio dropouts and crackling under heavy network load,
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.028|                    which is the exact failure the cap was introduced to
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.029|                    prevent. Nothing else is affected - this is not a
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.030|                    general network setting and it does not touch latency,
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.031|                    gaming traffic or anything that happens while audio is
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.032|                    silent.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.033|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.034|  Windows default : 10 (0xa), and unusually for a tuning value it is
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.035|                    genuinely present by default on a clean Windows 11
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.036|                    install - so writing 10 restores the shipped state
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.037|                    exactly, and deleting it does not.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.038|
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.039|  Possible values:
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.040|    10                   : 0xa, the shipped value. While an MMCSS-
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.041|                           registered multimedia stream is playing, the
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.042|                           system processes roughly 10 network packets per
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.043|                           millisecond; with standard 1500-byte frames
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.044|                           that works out somewhere around 100-120 Mbit/s.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.045|                           Outside audio playback the cap does not apply
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.046|                           at all and your link runs at full speed. This
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.047|                           is the value that protects audio threads from
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.048|                           being starved by network DPC work.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.049|    4294967295           : 0xFFFFFFFF - the documented 'no throttling'
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.050|                           sentinel. The cap is removed entirely, and with
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.051|                           it the protection audio threads had against
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.052|                           network interrupt work. Your link runs at full
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.053|                           speed during playback. On a modern multi-core
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.054|                           machine with RSS spreading network DPCs across
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.055|                           cores the starvation risk is much lower than it
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.056|                           was when the cap was designed, but it is not
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.057|                           zero.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.058|    any integer between 1 and 69 :
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.059|        An intermediate cap: the number is packets per millisecond, so 20
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.060|        is twice the shipped ceiling, 70 is seven times it. Legal, but
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.061|        there is no principled way to choose one - you would be picking a
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.062|        number between 'the value Microsoft tested' and 'off' with no way
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.063|        to measure where you landed. If you are going to move off 10, move
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.064|        to 0xFFFFFFFF and A/B it honestly.
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.065|    DELETE               : Value absent. Unlike most of the settings in
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.066|                           this script, absent is NOT the shipped state
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.067|                           here - a clean Windows 11 install really does
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.068|                           have 10 present. Deleting it is a change, not a
::T|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.069|                           restoration, so write 10 instead.
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.001|  Why these profiles : This is a pure throughput-versus-audio-protection
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.002|                       trade, and exactly one of the five profiles is
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.003|                       defined by throughput. Server lifts it: a hosting
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.004|                       box moves large amounts of data and has no
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.005|                       interactive audio worth protecting - on a truly
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.006|                       headless machine the cap may never even engage, so
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.007|                       the gain there is partly theoretical, but nothing
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.008|                       is lost either. Gaming keeps 10 on purpose, against
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.009|                       the folklore: the gaming profile is defined by
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.010|                       latency, audio crackling IS a latency failure, and
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.011|                       game traffic is a trickle of small UDP packets that
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.012|                       never comes near 10 packets per millisecond. Office
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.013|                       and Laptop keep 10 - no throughput case, and a
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.014|                       laptop is the machine most likely to be doing video
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.015|                       calls. Windows keeps 10 because that is the shipped
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.016|                       value.
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.017|
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.018|  Known problems  : The concrete failure at 0xFFFFFFFF is the one the cap
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.019|                    exists to prevent: audio thread starvation under
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.020|                    network DPC load, reported by DAW users as ASIO buffer
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.021|                    underruns and clicks in Reaper and Cubase during large
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.022|                    file transfers. Anyone doing music production should
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.023|                    stay at 10.
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.024|
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.025|  Unverified      : The 100-120 Mbit/s figure follows from 10 packets/ms
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.026|                    at 1500-byte frames, but the effective ceiling depends
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.027|                    on your actual frame size and offload settings, so
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.028|                    treat it as an order of magnitude rather than a
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.029|                    number. I also cannot tell you how often audio
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.030|                    starvation actually occurs at 0xFFFFFFFF on a modern
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.031|                    multi-core machine with RSS enabled - the mechanism is
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.032|                    real and documented, the frequency is not something I
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.033|                    have a measurement for.
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.034|
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.035|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.036|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile /v
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.037|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex (REG_DWORD) - written as 10 at
::X|EN|mmcss.network.throttling.038|                    OPTY.bat lines 1520, 1599, 1787, 2842 and 2854;
