:: ---- helper.pwrfix (repair) -------------------------------------
::P|helper.pwrfix|AC+DC|AC+DC|AC|AC|DELETE|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.001|COMMENT L'ASSISTANT DE REPARATION DES BRIDAGES CPU DECIDE CE QU'IL ECRIT
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.002|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.003|  Ce que c est    : Un seul assistant, appele trois fois, pour trois
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.004|                    reglages du sous-groupe processeur de votre mode
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.005|                    d'alimentation actif : le plafond de frequence
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.006|                    maximale (0 = aucun plafond), l'etat maximal du
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.007|                    processeur (100 %) et l'autorisation des etats de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.008|                    repos du CPU (0 = autorises). Il lit les forcages du
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.009|                    mode directement dans le registre plutot que via
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.010|                    powercfg /query, parce que /query est localise et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.011|                    masque les reglages dont les attributs les declarent
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.012|                    caches - PROCFREQMAX est precisement de ceux-la,
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.013|                    /query n'affiche donc rien pour lui meme quand un
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.014|                    plafond est actif.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.015|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.016|  Effet reel      : Pour chaque reglage, il demande d'abord si un forcage
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.017|                    existe reellement sur le mode actif. S'il n'y en a pas
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.018|                    - le mode herite du defaut livre - il le dit et ne
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.019|                    fait rien. Ce n'est que si un vrai forcage est
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.020|                    present, et qu'il differe de la valeur inoffensive,
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.021|                    qu'il ecrit, et uniquement sur le mode que vous
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.022|                    utilisez, pas sur tous.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.023|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.024|  Gain            : Entierement conditionnel, et enorme quand il
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.025|                    s'applique. Un CPU cloue a 1,2 GHz par un plafond de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.026|                    frequence oublie, ou un etat maximal coince a 50 pour
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.027|                    cent, c'est un ralentissement spectaculaire, invisible
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.028|                    tant qu'on ne pense pas a regarder la frequence. Le
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.029|                    lever rend a la machine ses vraies performances. S'il
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.030|                    n'y a aucun plafond - le resultat habituel - la
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.031|                    reponse honnete est que ca ne change rien et n'apporte
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.032|                    rien, et le journal le dit exactement, au lieu de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.033|                    revendiquer une reparation.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.034|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.035|  Cout            : Sur un portable, un plafond a pu etre pose
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.036|                    volontairement, par vous ou par un utilitaire
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.037|                    constructeur, pour maitriser la chaleur, le bruit ou
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.038|                    l'autonomie, et le lever vous rend les trois. Notez
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.039|                    exactement ce qu'ecrit l'assistant actuel : la
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.040|                    decision est prise sur la valeur secteur (AC) et seul
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.041|                    l'index AC est ecrit. Si un forcage batterie (DC)
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.042|                    existe, il est signale dans le journal comme laisse
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.043|                    intact, avec la commande powercfg pour le lever vous-
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.044|                    meme. Une version anterieure decidait sur AC et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.045|                    ecrasait DC avec la meme valeur sans restriction, en
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.046|                    ne consignant que le changement AC - c'est corrige, et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.047|                    la difference compte sur toute machine a batterie.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.048|                    Reautoriser les etats de repos n'est pas un cout du
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.049|                    tout : c'est ce qui permet au CPU de se reposer a
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.050|                    faible consommation et de booster plus haut.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.051|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.052|  Defaut Windows  : Sur le mode Utilisation normale d'origine de Windows
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.053|                    11 25H2, aucun des trois n'a de forcage utilisateur :
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.054|                    l'etat maximal du processeur est a 100 %, il n'y a
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.055|                    aucun plafond de frequence, et les etats de repos sont
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.056|                    autorises. Un plafond non nul est donc toujours
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.057|                    l'oeuvre d'un constructeur, d'un utilitaire ou d'un
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.058|                    tweak anterieur.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.059|
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.060|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.061|    AC                   : Ce que fait l'assistant aujourd'hui. Il lit le
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.062|                           forcage secteur (AC) ; s'il n'y en a pas, il
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.063|                           signale « herite du defaut du mode » et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.064|                           s'arrete. S'il y en a un et qu'il ne vaut pas
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.065|                           deja la valeur inoffensive, il ecrit cette
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.066|                           valeur sur AC seulement, reactive le mode, et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.067|                           consigne « etait bride a X, maintenant Y ». Un
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.068|                           forcage batterie (DC), s'il existe, est ensuite
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.069|                           signale comme « LAISSE TEL QUEL, c'est peut-
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.070|                           etre volontaire », avec la commande powercfg
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.071|                           exacte pour le lever a la main.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.072|    AC+DC                : Ecrire la valeur sans restriction sur les deux
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.073|                           index. Correct sur une machine sans batterie,
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.074|                           ou un forcage DC ne peut etre qu'un residu de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.075|                           vieux script ou d'image clonee. Faux sur tout
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.076|                           ce qui a une batterie, car cela annule en
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.077|                           silence une limite d'autonomie ou de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.078|                           temperature voulue - c'est exactement ce que
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.079|                           faisait une version anterieure de cet
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.080|                           assistant, qui decidait sur la valeur AC et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.081|                           ecrasait DC sans le dire dans le journal.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.082|    REPORT               : Lire les forcages et les afficher, ne rien
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.083|                           modifier. C'est ce que fait l'assistant jumeau
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.084|                           :pwrreport pour les reglages dont le bon defaut
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.085|                           depend du mode, comme PERFBOOSTMODE. Une
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.086|                           reponse defendable pour qui veut un diagnostic
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.087|                           avant toute modification.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.088|    DELETE               : Supprimer les valeurs ACSettingIndex et
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.089|                           DCSettingIndex sous le GUID du reglage, puis
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.090|                           reactiver le mode, pour que le mode herite de
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.091|                           nouveau. C'est l'etat d'usine au bit pres : sur
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.092|                           un Windows 11 propre, aucune des trois cles
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.093|                           n'existe. Ecrire 0 ou 100 reproduit le
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.094|                           comportement mais laisse un forcage explicite
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.095|                           la ou Windows n'en avait aucun.
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.096|    SKIP                 : Ne pas executer les trois verifications du
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.097|                           tout. N'a de sens que si vous avez pose vos
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.098|                           plafonds vous-meme et savez exactement ce qu'il
::T|FR|helper.pwrfix.099|                           y a sur le mode.
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : La ligne de partage porte sur l'index batterie,
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.002|                         rien d'autre : les quatre profils de travail
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.003|                         veulent tous que le plafond secteur disparaisse.
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.004|                         Gaming et Serveur repondent AC+DC parce qu'aucune
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.005|                         des deux machines n'a de batterie - un forcage DC
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.006|                         n'y peut etre qu'un residu d'image clonee ou de
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.007|                         vieux script, il n'y a rien de volontaire a
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.008|                         proteger. Bureau et Portable repondent AC : la
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.009|                         machine de bureau peut etre un portable et OPTY
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.010|                         n'a aucun moyen de le savoir, et la regle du
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.011|                         portable est explicite - les plafonds sur
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.012|                         batterie sont respectes et jamais leves. Windows
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.013|                         repond DELETE, en retirant entierement le forcage
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.014|                         pour que le mode herite : c'est ca l'etat livre,
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.015|                         pas l'ecriture de 0 et de 100. A dire clairement
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.016|                         dans le rapport : OPTY ne contient aucune sonde
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.017|                         de chassis, de batterie ou ACPI, il ne peut donc
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.018|                         pas distinguer une tour de jeu d'un ultrabook
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.019|                         sans ventilateur. La valeur batterie est signalee
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.020|                         et vous est laissee.
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.021|
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.022|  Non verifie (en)  : The audit that produced this card was run against a
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.023|                      build where :pwrfix wrote the DC index whenever a DC
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.024|                      override existed. The source in the repository today
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.025|                      does not: lines 3506-3517 explicitly report the DC
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.026|                      value and leave it alone, with a comment explaining
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.027|                      why. The card below describes the current code. If
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.028|                      the audited binary and the repository have diverged
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.029|                      for other reasons, that is worth checking before
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.030|                      shipping.
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.031|
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.032|  Cible           : :pwrfix at OPTY.bat lines 3485-3517, with :pwrread at
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.033|                    lines 3477-3483. Called three times, at lines 1906 /
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.034|                    1908 / 1912, always with subgroup SUB_PROCESSOR
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.035|                    54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00 on
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.036|                    SCHEME_CURRENT. Reads HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Co
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.037|                    ntrol\Power\User\PowerSchemes\<ActivePowerScheme>\<SUB
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.038|                    P>\<GUID> /v ACSettingIndex and /v DCSettingIndex,
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.039|                    writes with powercfg /setacvalueindex then powercfg
::X|FR|helper.pwrfix.040|                    /setactive SCHEME_CURRENT.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.001|HOW THE CPU-CAP REPAIR HELPER DECIDES WHAT TO WRITE
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.002|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.003|  What it is      : One helper, called three times, for three processor-
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.004|                    subgroup settings on your currently active power plan:
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.005|                    the maximum frequency cap (0 means no cap), the
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.006|                    maximum processor state (100 percent), and whether CPU
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.007|                    idle states are allowed (0 means allowed). It reads
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.008|                    the plan's overrides straight out of the registry
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.009|                    rather than from powercfg /query, because /query is
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.010|                    localised and hides settings whose attributes mark
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.011|                    them hidden - PROCFREQMAX is exactly such a setting,
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.012|                    so /query prints nothing for it even when a cap is
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.013|                    active.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.014|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.015|  Actual effect   : For each setting it first asks whether an override
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.016|                    actually exists on the active scheme. If there is none
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.017|                    - the scheme inherits the shipped default - it says so
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.018|                    and does nothing. Only when a real override is
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.019|                    present, and differs from the harmless value, does it
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.020|                    write, and only to the plan you are currently using,
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.021|                    not to all of them.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.022|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.023|  Gain            : Entirely conditional, and enormous when it applies. A
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.024|                    CPU pinned at 1.2 GHz by a leftover frequency cap, or
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.025|                    a maximum processor state stuck at 50 percent, is a
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.026|                    dramatic slowdown that is invisible unless you know to
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.027|                    go looking at the clock speed. Lifting it gives the
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.028|                    machine its real performance back. If no cap exists -
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.029|                    the usual outcome - the honest answer is that this
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.030|                    changes nothing and gains nothing, and the log says
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.031|                    exactly that rather than claiming a fix.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.032|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.033|  Cost            : On a laptop a cap may have been put there on purpose,
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.034|                    by you or by a vendor utility, to control heat, fan
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.035|                    noise or runtime, and lifting it gives all three back.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.036|                    Note exactly what the current helper writes: the
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.037|                    decision is taken from the mains (AC) value and only
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.038|                    the AC index is written. If a battery (DC) override
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.039|                    exists it is reported in the log as left alone, with
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.040|                    the powercfg command to lift it yourself. An earlier
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.041|                    build decided from AC and overwrote DC with the same
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.042|                    unrestricted value while logging only the AC change -
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.043|                    that is fixed, and the difference matters on any
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.044|                    machine with a battery. Re-allowing idle states is not
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.045|                    a cost at all: that is what lets a CPU idle cheaply
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.046|                    and boost higher.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.047|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.048|  Windows default : On the stock Balanced plan of Windows 11 25H2 none of
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.049|                    the three has a user override: maximum processor state
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.050|                    is 100 percent, there is no maximum-frequency cap, and
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.051|                    idle states are enabled. A non-zero cap is therefore
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.052|                    always something an OEM, a vendor utility or an
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.053|                    earlier tweak wrote.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.054|
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.055|  Possible values:
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.056|    AC                   : What the helper does today. It reads the mains
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.057|                           (AC) override; if there is none it reports
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.058|                           'inherits the scheme default' and stops. If
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.059|                           there is one and it is not already the harmless
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.060|                           value, it writes the harmless value to AC only,
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.061|                           re-activates the plan, and logs 'was capped at
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.062|                           X, now Y'. A battery (DC) override, if one
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.063|                           exists, is then reported as 'LEFT ALONE, that
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.064|                           may be deliberate' with the exact powercfg
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.065|                           command to lift it by hand.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.066|    AC+DC                : Write the unrestricted value to both indexes.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.067|                           Right on a machine with no battery, where a DC
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.068|                           override can only be residue from an old script
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.069|                           or a cloned image. Wrong on anything with a
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.070|                           battery, because it silently undoes a
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.071|                           deliberate runtime or thermal limit - which is
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.072|                           precisely what an earlier build of this helper
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.073|                           did, deciding from the AC value and overwriting
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.074|                           DC without saying so in the log.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.075|    REPORT               : Read the overrides and print them, change
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.076|                           nothing. This is what the sibling helper
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.077|                           :pwrreport does for settings whose correct
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.078|                           default is per-scheme, such as PERFBOOSTMODE. A
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.079|                           defensible answer for anyone who wants a
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.080|                           diagnosis before a change.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.081|    DELETE               : Remove the ACSettingIndex and DCSettingIndex
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.082|                           values under that setting's GUID and re-
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.083|                           activate the plan, so the scheme inherits
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.084|                           again. This is the byte-for-byte factory state:
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.085|                           on a clean Windows 11 none of the three keys
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.086|                           exists. Writing 0 or 100 reproduces the
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.087|                           behaviour but leaves an explicit override where
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.088|                           Windows had none.
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.089|    SKIP                 : Do not run the three checks at all. Only
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.090|                           sensible if you set your caps yourself and know
::T|EN|helper.pwrfix.091|                           exactly what is on the plan.
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.001|  Why these profiles : The split is about the battery index, nothing else
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.002|                       - all four working profiles want the mains cap
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.003|                       gone. Gaming and Server answer AC+DC because
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.004|                       neither machine has a battery, so a DC override on
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.005|                       them can only be residue from a cloned image or an
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.006|                       old script and there is nothing deliberate to
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.007|                       protect. Office and Laptop answer AC: the office
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.008|                       machine might be a laptop and OPTY has no way to
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.009|                       know, and the laptop rule is explicit that on-
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.010|                       battery caps are respected and never lifted.
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.011|                       Windows answers DELETE, removing the override
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.012|                       entirely so the plan inherits - that, not writing 0
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.013|                       and 100, is the shipped state. Worth saying plainly
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.014|                       in the report: OPTY contains no chassis, battery or
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.015|                       ACPI probe of any kind, so it cannot distinguish a
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.016|                       gaming tower from a fanless ultrabook. The battery
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.017|                       value is reported and left for you.
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.018|
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.019|  Unverified      : The audit that produced this card was run against a
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.020|                    build where :pwrfix wrote the DC index whenever a DC
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.021|                    override existed. The source in the repository today
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.022|                    does not: lines 3506-3517 explicitly report the DC
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.023|                    value and leave it alone, with a comment explaining
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.024|                    why. The card below describes the current code. If the
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.025|                    audited binary and the repository have diverged for
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.026|                    other reasons, that is worth checking before shipping.
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.027|
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.028|  Target          : :pwrfix at OPTY.bat lines 3485-3517, with :pwrread at
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.029|                    lines 3477-3483. Called three times, at lines 1906 /
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.030|                    1908 / 1912, always with subgroup SUB_PROCESSOR
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.031|                    54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00 on
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.032|                    SCHEME_CURRENT. Reads HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Co
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.033|                    ntrol\Power\User\PowerSchemes\<ActivePowerScheme>\<SUB
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.034|                    P>\<GUID> /v ACSettingIndex and /v DCSettingIndex,
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.035|                    writes with powercfg /setacvalueindex then powercfg
::X|EN|helper.pwrfix.036|                    /setactive SCHEME_CURRENT.
::
:: ---- dism.restorehealth (repair) --------------------------------
::P|dism.restorehealth|SKIP|RUN|RUN|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.001|DISM - REPARATION DU MAGASIN DE COMPOSANTS
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.002|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.003|  Ce que c est    : Le magasin de composants (C:\Windows\WinSxS) contient
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.004|                    la copie de reference de chaque fichier systeme a
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.005|                    partir de laquelle Windows sait se reparer. OPTY lance
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.006|                    d'abord trois passes en lecture seule, puis la
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.007|                    reparation, puis un nettoyage - volontairement sans
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.008|                    /ResetBase.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.009|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.010|  Effet reel      : /RestoreHealth confronte le magasin a ses manifestes
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.011|                    et remplace tout ce dont l'empreinte ne correspond
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.012|                    pas, en telechargeant les charges utiles depuis
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.013|                    Windows Update quand la copie locale est inutilisable.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.014|                    /StartComponentCleanup elimine ensuite les versions
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.015|                    remplacees ; comme OPTY omet /ResetBase, les mises a
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.016|                    jour recentes restent desinstallables.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.017|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.018|  Gain            : Aucun gain de performance, point. Ce n'est pas une
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.019|                    etape d'optimisation : sur une machine saine, elle
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.020|                    affiche « aucune corruption detectee » et ne change
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.021|                    rien. Le mecanisme qui justifie de la lancer est
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.022|                    etroit mais bien reel : SFC repare les fichiers
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.023|                    systeme en les recopiant depuis le magasin de
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.024|                    composants ; si le magasin est lui-meme corrompu, SFC
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.025|                    n'a plus de source saine et annonce qu'il n'arrive a
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.026|                    rien. DISM est ce qui redonne a SFC de quoi
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.027|                    travailler.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.028|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.029|  Cout            : Du temps. Cinq a trente minutes, parfois davantage,
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.030|                    avec une barre de progression qui stagne longuement a
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.031|                    20 %. Tous les coeurs travaillent et de la bande
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.032|                    passante Windows Update est consommee. Aucun fichier,
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.033|                    profil ou reglage utilisateur n'est touche.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.034|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.035|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet : ce n'est pas un reglage. Windows ne lance
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.036|                    jamais /RestoreHealth de lui-meme ; il n'execute que
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.037|                    la tache planifiee StartComponentCleanup, a
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.038|                    l'inactivite, apres 30 jours de delai de grace.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.039|
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.040|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.041|    RUN                  : Enchaine les cinq passes DISM. Les trois
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.042|                           premieres ne font que lire :
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.043|                           /AnalyzeComponentStore annonce la taille de
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.044|                           WinSxS et si un nettoyage est conseille,
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.045|                           /ScanHealth parcourt tout le magasin et compare
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.046|                           l'empreinte de chaque composant a son
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.047|                           manifeste, /CheckHealth relit l'indicateur de
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.048|                           corruption pose par ces analyses.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.049|                           /RestoreHealth est la seule etape qui ecrit :
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.050|                           elle remplace les composants dont l'empreinte
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.051|                           ne colle pas, en telechargeant les charges
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.052|                           utiles depuis Windows Update quand la copie
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.053|                           locale est elle-meme abimee.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.054|                           /StartComponentCleanup elimine ensuite les
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.055|                           versions de composants remplacees.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.056|    SKIP                 : Rien ne s'execute. Aucun etat n'est laisse
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.057|                           derriere, aucun indicateur pose : DISM est une
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.058|                           operation, pas un reglage, donc sauter l'etape
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.059|                           aujourd'hui ne coute rien demain. Une machine
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.060|                           dont le magasin est corrompu le reste, et le
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.061|                           symptome finit par apparaitre : une mise a jour
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.062|                           Windows qui echoue avec la meme erreur tous les
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.063|                           mois, ou SFC qui signale des degats qu'il
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.064|                           n'arrive pas a reparer.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien ici ne depend du materiel : le partage porte
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.002|                         uniquement sur qui peut s'offrir la demi-heure et
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.003|                         qui ne verrait pas passer une chaine de mise a
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.004|                         jour silencieusement cassee. Serveur et
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.005|                         bureautique le lancent : ces machines sont
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.006|                         censees continuer a se mettre a jour sans
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.007|                         surveillance, et un magasin corrompu se manifeste
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.008|                         des mois plus tard par une mise a jour qui echoue
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.009|                         systematiquement. Jeu et portable sautent l'etape
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.010|                         : elle ne rapporte aucune image par seconde, et
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.011|                         sur un portable c'est trente minutes de calcul
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.012|                         sur tous les coeurs plus du reseau, sur une
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.013|                         machine dont tout le profil tient a l'autonomie.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.014|                         Ici « sauter » veut dire « a lancer a la main,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.015|                         sur secteur, le jour ou vous avez un symptome »,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.016|                         pas « jamais ». La reponse 5 saute aussi, parce
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.017|                         qu'un Windows d'origine ne lance pas DISM.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.018|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.019|  Problemes connus : L'operation elle-meme ne casse rien. En revanche son
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.020|                     propre mode d'echec merite d'etre reconnu : hors
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.021|                     ligne, ou sur une machine ou Windows Update est
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.022|                     bloque par strategie ou redirige vers un WSUS,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.023|                     /RestoreHealth se termine sur 0x800f0906 ou
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.024|                     0x800f081f et « les fichiers sources sont
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.025|                     introuvables ». C'est une source manquante, pas la
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.026|                     preuve que votre Windows est abime : en le pointant
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.027|                     vers une ISO montee avec /Source:wim:, il va au bout.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.028|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.029|  Non verifie (en)  : The exact grace-period behaviour of a manual
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.030|                      /StartComponentCleanup without /ResetBase (whether
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.031|                      it honours the same 30-day window as the scheduled
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.032|                      task) I have not verified on 25H2. It matters only
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.033|                      for how far back you can uninstall updates
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.034|                      afterwards.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.035|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.036|  Cible           : OPTY.bat label :dism, lines 601-611 - dism /Online
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.037|                    /Cleanup-Image /AnalyzeComponentStore, then /Cleanup-
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.038|                    image /ScanHealth, /CheckHealth, /RestoreHealth,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.039|                    /StartComponentCleanup (no /ResetBase)
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.001|DISM COMPONENT STORE REPAIR
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.002|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.003|  What it is      : The component store (C:\Windows\WinSxS) holds the
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.004|                    reference copy of every system file Windows can
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.005|                    restore from. OPTY runs three read-only passes first,
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.006|                    then the repair, then a cleanup - deliberately without
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.007|                    /ResetBase.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.008|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.009|  Actual effect   : /RestoreHealth compares the store against its
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.010|                    manifests and replaces anything whose hash does not
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.011|                    match, pulling payloads from Windows Update when the
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.012|                    local copy is unusable. /StartComponentCleanup then
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.013|                    discards superseded component versions; because OPTY
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.014|                    omits /ResetBase, recently installed updates stay
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.015|                    uninstallable.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.016|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.017|  Gain            : No performance gain whatsoever - this is not a tuning
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.018|                    step, and on a healthy machine it prints 'no component
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.019|                    store corruption detected' and changes nothing. The
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.020|                    mechanism that makes it worth running is narrow but
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.021|                    real: SFC repairs damaged system files by copying them
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.022|                    out of the component store, so a corrupt store leaves
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.023|                    SFC with no clean source and it reports it cannot fix
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.024|                    anything. DISM is what gives SFC something to repair
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.025|                    from.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.026|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.027|  Cost            : Time. Five to thirty minutes, occasionally longer,
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.028|                    with a progress bar that sits at 20 percent for a long
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.029|                    stretch. All cores stay busy and Windows Update
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.030|                    bandwidth is used. No user file, profile or setting is
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.031|                    touched.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.032|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.033|  Windows default : Not a setting. Windows never runs /RestoreHealth on
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.034|                    its own - it only runs the StartComponentCleanup
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.035|                    scheduled task, on idle, after a 30-day grace period.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.036|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.037|  Possible values:
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.038|    RUN                  : Executes the five DISM passes in order. The
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.039|                           first three read only: /AnalyzeComponentStore
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.040|                           reports the WinSxS size and whether a cleanup
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.041|                           is recommended, /ScanHealth walks the whole
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.042|                           store and hashes every component against its
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.043|                           manifest, /CheckHealth reads the corruption
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.044|                           flag those scans set. /RestoreHealth is the
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.045|                           only step that writes: it replaces components
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.046|                           whose hash does not match, downloading
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.047|                           replacement payloads from Windows Update when
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.048|                           the local copy is itself damaged.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.049|                           /StartComponentCleanup then discards superseded
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.050|                           component versions.
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.051|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs. No state is left behind, no flag
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.052|                           is set - DISM is an operation, not a setting,
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.053|                           so skipping it today costs nothing tomorrow. A
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.054|                           machine with a corrupt component store stays
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.055|                           corrupt, and the symptom you will eventually
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.056|                           see is a Windows Update that fails with the
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.057|                           same error every month, or SFC reporting it
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.058|                           found damage it cannot fix.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.001|  Why these profiles : Nothing here depends on hardware, so the split is
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.002|                       purely about who can afford the half hour and who
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.003|                       would not notice a silently broken update path.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.004|                       Server and office run it: those machines are
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.005|                       expected to keep updating unattended, and a corrupt
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.006|                       store surfaces months later as an update that fails
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.007|                       every single time. Gaming and laptop skip it - it
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.008|                       buys no frames, and on a laptop it is thirty
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.009|                       minutes of all-core work plus radio traffic on a
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.010|                       machine whose entire profile is battery life. Skip
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.011|                       here means 'run it by hand, plugged in, the day you
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.012|                       have a symptom', not 'never'. Answer 5 also skips,
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.013|                       because running DISM is not something a stock
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.014|                       Windows does.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.015|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.016|  Known problems  : The operation itself breaks nothing. Its own failure
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.017|                    mode is worth recognising: offline, or on a machine
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.018|                    where Windows Update is blocked by policy or
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.019|                    redirected to a WSUS server, /RestoreHealth ends with
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.020|                    0x800f0906 or 0x800f081f and 'the source files could
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.021|                    not be found'. That is a missing source, not proof
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.022|                    your Windows is damaged - pointing it at a mounted ISO
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.023|                    with /Source:wim: finishes the job.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.024|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.025|  Unverified      : The exact grace-period behaviour of a manual
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.026|                    /StartComponentCleanup without /ResetBase (whether it
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.027|                    honours the same 30-day window as the scheduled task)
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.028|                    I have not verified on 25H2. It matters only for how
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.029|                    far back you can uninstall updates afterwards.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.030|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.031|  Target          : OPTY.bat label :dism, lines 601-611 - dism /Online
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.032|                    /Cleanup-Image /AnalyzeComponentStore, then /Cleanup-
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.033|                    image /ScanHealth, /CheckHealth, /RestoreHealth,
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.034|                    /StartComponentCleanup (no /ResetBase)
::
:: ---- gr.mpo.restore (repair) ------------------------------------
::P|gr.mpo.restore|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.001|REACTIVER LE MULTI-PLANE OVERLAY
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.002|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.003|  Ce que c est    : Le Multi-Plane Overlay, c'est le chemin par lequel le
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.004|                    controleur d'affichage sort le swapchain d'une fenetre
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.005|                    depuis son propre plan materiel, au lieu que DWM
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.006|                    refonde cette fenetre dans le bureau a chaque image.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.007|                    Ces trois valeurs sont la recette tierce pour couper
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.008|                    ce chemin.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.009|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.010|  Effet reel      : Supprime les trois. L'absence est le defaut : ecrire 0
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.011|                    creerait un troisieme etat, pas un retour a l'origine.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.012|                    Effectif apres redemarrage.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.013|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.014|  Gain            : Une passe de composition plein ecran par image que DWM
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.015|                    n'a plus a faire tant qu'une fenetre est au premier
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.016|                    plan, et un etage de tampon en moins avant que l'image
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.017|                    n'atteigne la dalle. Cela se traduit par moins de
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.018|                    consommation GPU et moins de latence en fenetre et en
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.019|                    borderless. Ce ne sont pas des images par seconde en
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.020|                    plus en plein ecran exclusif, ou le MPO n'intervient
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.021|                    tout simplement pas.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.022|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.023|  Cout            : Si le MPO etait vraiment la cause d'un scintillement
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.024|                    sur cette machine, le scintillement revient. C'est
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.025|                    tout le cout.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.026|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.027|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois absentes.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.028|
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.030|    DELETE               : Les trois valeurs sont supprimees. C'est l'etat
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.031|                           d'origine : DWM propose au pilote le swapchain
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.032|                           de chaque fenetre visible, et le pilote decide
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.033|                           image par image s'il peut la faire sortir
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.034|                           depuis son propre plan materiel au lieu de la
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.035|                           fondre dans le bitmap du bureau. Effectif apres
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.036|                           redemarrage.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.037|    OverlayTestMode=5    : La seule valeur reellement publiee par
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.038|                           Microsoft et les deux fabricants de GPU comme
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.039|                           interrupteur d'arret du MPO. DWM cesse de
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.040|                           proposer des plans au pilote : chaque image en
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.041|                           fenetre repasse par une composition complete du
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.042|                           bureau. C'est la valeur qu'ecrit le
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.043|                           contournement anti-scintillement.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.044|    OverlayTestMode=0    : Ce n'est pas un retour a la normale. Zero est
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.045|                           un troisieme etat : un mode de test ecrit, dont
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.046|                           la valeur vaut 0, et non l'absence de mode de
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.047|                           test. C'est precisement le piege pour lequel
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.048|                           :killkey existe : le seul retour a l'origine
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.049|                           est la suppression.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.050|    OverlayMinFPS=0      : Elle accompagne la plupart des recettes
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.051|                           copiees-collees. Elle ne fait pas partie de la
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.052|                           procedure publiee par Microsoft et je n'ai
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.053|                           trouve aucune documentation sur ce que DWM en
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.054|                           fait. La supprimer est correct parce que
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.055|                           l'absence est l'etat d'origine, pas parce qu'on
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.056|                           saurait que 0 est nuisible.
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.057|    DisableOverlays=1    : Elle se trouve sous GraphicsDrivers et non sous
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.058|                           Dwm, et vient de scripts de reglage tiers. Son
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.059|                           intention affichee est d'empecher purement et
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.060|                           simplement le pilote d'utiliser des plans
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.061|                           d'overlay. Meme raisonnement : on la supprime
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.062|                           parce que Windows ne l'ecrit jamais, pas parce
::T|FR|gr.mpo.restore.063|                           que son effet serait documente.
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq suppriment, et c'est le tableau honnete,
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.002|                         pas un tableau paresseux. Ces valeurs, Windows ne
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.003|                         les ecrit jamais : sur chaque profil, le bon etat
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.004|                         sur le disque est « disparu », il n'y a aucun
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.005|                         arbitrage jeu contre bureautique a faire sur des
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.006|                         residus. Meme sur un serveur sans ecran, ou rien
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.007|                         n'est compose vers une dalle, retirer une valeur
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.008|                         que le systeme n'a jamais posee ne coute rien et
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.009|                         evite qu'elle ne piege celui qui rebranchera un
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.010|                         ecran plus tard. La seule machine qui devrait
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.011|                         repondre autrement est celle ou quelqu'un a coupe
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.012|                         le MPO exprES et ou cela a vraiment regle un
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.013|                         scintillement toujours present : ce cas releve de
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.014|                         la carte « contournement anti-scintillement »,
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.015|                         pas de celle-ci.
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.016|
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.017|  Problemes connus : Aucun du fait de la suppression. La consequence du
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.018|                     MPO actif, c'est le scintillement et les flashs noirs
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.019|                     en multi-ecran a frequences differentes pour lesquels
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.020|                     la recette de desactivation a ete ecrite ;
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.021|                     l'essentiel des bugs de pilote a l'origine du
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.022|                     probleme est corrige depuis des annees, d'ou l'ordre
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.023|                     correct : supprimer d'abord, tester ensuite.
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.024|
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.025|  Non verifie (en)  : The claim that disabling MPO also costs Auto HDR and
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.026|                      Optimized Windowed Mode is consistent with those
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.027|                      features being built on the flip and overlay
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.028|                      presentation path, but I have not verified it
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.029|                      feature-by-feature on current drivers. It is stated
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.030|                      here as a cost of disabling, not as a gain of this
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.031|                      card, so nothing in this card depends on it.
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.032|
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.033|  Cible           : reg delete HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm /v
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.034|                    OverlayTestMode /f ; same key /v OverlayMinFPS ; reg
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.035|                    delete
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.036|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.037|                    /v DisableOverlays /f - OPTY.bat lines 1623-1625,
::X|FR|gr.mpo.restore.038|                    restore path
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.001|RE-ENABLE MULTI-PLANE OVERLAY
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.002|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.003|  What it is      : Multi-Plane Overlay is the path where the display
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.004|                    controller scans a window's swapchain out of its own
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.005|                    hardware plane instead of DWM blending that window
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.006|                    into the desktop every frame. These three values are
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.007|                    the third-party recipe for switching that path off.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.008|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes all three. Absence is the default - writing 0
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.010|                    would be a third state, not a restore. Takes effect
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.011|                    after a reboot.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.012|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.013|  Gain            : One full-screen composite pass per frame that DWM no
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.014|                    longer has to perform while a window is on top, and
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.015|                    one less stage of buffering before the panel sees the
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.016|                    frame. That shows up as lower GPU power and lower
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.017|                    latency in windowed and borderless play. It is not
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.018|                    extra FPS in exclusive fullscreen, where MPO is not in
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.019|                    the path at all.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.020|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.021|  Cost            : If MPO genuinely was the cause of a flicker on this
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.022|                    machine, the flicker comes back. That is the whole
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.023|                    cost.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.024|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.025|  Windows default : All three absent.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.026|
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.028|    DELETE               : The three values are removed. This is the
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.029|                           shipped state: DWM offers each visible window's
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.030|                           swapchain to the driver, and the driver decides
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.031|                           per frame whether it can be scanned out from
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.032|                           its own hardware plane instead of being blended
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.033|                           into the desktop bitmap. Effective after a
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.034|                           reboot.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.035|    OverlayTestMode=5    : The one value Microsoft and both GPU vendors
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.036|                           actually published as the MPO kill switch. DWM
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.037|                           stops offering planes to the driver, so every
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.038|                           windowed frame goes through a full desktop
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.039|                           composite pass. This is the value the flicker
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.040|                           workaround writes.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.041|    OverlayTestMode=0    : Not a restore. Zero is a third state - a
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.042|                           written test mode that happens to be
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.043|                           numerically 0, not the absence of a test mode.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.044|                           This is exactly the trap :killkey exists for:
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.045|                           the only way back to stock is deletion.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.046|    OverlayMinFPS=0      : Rides along in most copy-pasted disable
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.047|                           recipes. It is not part of Microsoft's
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.048|                           published procedure and I could not find
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.049|                           documentation of what DWM does with it.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.050|                           Deleting it is correct because absent is the
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.051|                           shipped state, not because 0 is known to be
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.052|                           harmful.
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.053|    DisableOverlays=1    : Lives under GraphicsDrivers rather than Dwm and
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.054|                           comes from third-party tuning scripts. Its
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.055|                           stated intent is to stop the driver using
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.056|                           overlay planes at all. Same reasoning: delete
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.057|                           it because Windows never writes it, not because
::T|EN|gr.mpo.restore.058|                           its effect is documented.
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete, and that is the honest table
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.002|                       rather than a lazy one. These values are not
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.003|                       something Windows ever writes, so on every profile
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.004|                       the correct on-disk state is 'gone' - there is no
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.005|                       gaming-versus-office judgement to make about
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.006|                       residue. Even on a headless server, where nothing
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.007|                       is composited to a panel, removing a value the OS
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.008|                       never set costs nothing and stops it ambushing
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.009|                       whoever plugs a monitor in later. The one machine
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.010|                       that should answer differently is the one where
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.011|                       somebody disabled MPO on purpose and it genuinely
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.012|                       fixed a flicker that is still there - and that
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.013|                       belongs on the flicker-workaround card, not on this
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.014|                       one.
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.015|
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.016|  Known problems  : None caused by deleting them. The consequence of MPO
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.017|                    being on is the mixed-refresh multi-monitor flicker
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.018|                    and black flashing that the disable recipe was written
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.019|                    for; most of the driver bugs behind it were fixed
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.020|                    years ago, which is why deleting first and testing is
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.021|                    the right order.
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.022|
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.023|  Unverified      : The claim that disabling MPO also costs Auto HDR and
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.024|                    Optimized Windowed Mode is consistent with those
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.025|                    features being built on the flip and overlay
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.026|                    presentation path, but I have not verified it feature-
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.027|                    by-feature on current drivers. It is stated here as a
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.028|                    cost of disabling, not as a gain of this card, so
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.029|                    nothing in this card depends on it.
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.030|
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.031|  Target          : reg delete HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm /v
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.032|                    OverlayTestMode /f ; same key /v OverlayMinFPS ; reg
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.033|                    delete
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.034|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.035|                    /v DisableOverlays /f - OPTY.bat lines 1623-1625,
::X|EN|gr.mpo.restore.036|                    restore path
