:: ---- rad.gpu.preemption.killkey (cleanup) ------------------------
::P|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|RETABLIR L'ETAT D'ORIGINE DE LA PREEMPTION GPU
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : EnablePreemption, sous GraphicsDrivers\Scheduler : une
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                    valeur qui apparait sur les machines passees entre les
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                    mains d'un script de reglage GPU. Windows ne cree pas
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|                    cette cle.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|  Effet reel      : Supprime la valeur si elle est presente, laissant
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|                    l'ordonnanceur GPU sur le comportement de preemption
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|                    propre au pilote.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|  Gain            : Remettre la machine dans l'etat d'une installation
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    neuve, rien de plus, et autant le dire franchement. Ce
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    n'est pas une valeur documentee par Microsoft, et
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    aucune mesure publiee ne montre que la mettre a 0
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    rapporte des images par seconde, ni que la supprimer
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    en coute. La raison de supprimer, c'est que l'absence
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                    est ce que livre Windows, pas que le retrait vous
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|                    rapporte quelque chose. Si c'etait le cas, cette carte
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                    donnerait un chiffre.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|  Cout            : Aucun. Rien, sur une machine normale, ne lit cette cle
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|                    volontairement.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|  Defaut Windows  : Absente.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.028|    DELETE               : La valeur est supprimee et l'ordonnanceur GPU
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.029|                           reste sur la granularite de preemption que le
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.030|                           pilote declare savoir faire - le seul etat
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.031|                           qu'une installation neuve de Windows ait jamais
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.032|                           connu, puisque Windows ne cree pas cette cle.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.033|    0                    : Ce qu'ecrivent les scripts de reglage, avec
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.034|                           l'argument que cela supprime du cout
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.035|                           d'ordonnancement. La preemption est le
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.036|                           mecanisme qui permet au GPU d'interrompre un
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.037|                           long travail de dessin ou de calcul en cours de
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.038|                           route pour laisser passer autre chose : le
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.039|                           bureau, une file plus prioritaire, la
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.040|                           reinitialisation du chien de garde. Si la
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.041|                           valeur fait bien ce que ces scripts pretendent,
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.042|                           la contrepartie est un bureau qui saccade sous
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.043|                           charge GPU et un TDR qui a moins de prise. Je
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.044|                           n'ai trouve aucune mesure publiee montrant un
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.045|                           gain quelconque : considerez l'affirmation
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.046|                           comme non etayee dans les deux sens, et
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.047|                           contentez-vous de supprimer la valeur.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.048|    1                    : Ecrire 1 n'est pas non plus le retour a
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.049|                           l'origine, meme si cela se lit comme «
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.050|                           preemption activee ». Windows livre cette cle
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.051|                           absente : 1 est donc un forcage ecrit qui se
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.052|                           trouve coincider avec le defaut du pilote - et
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.053|                           qui continuerait de le forcer si un pilote
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.054|                           futur voulait autre chose. C'est la suppression
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.055|                           qui restaure.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq suppriment, et il n'y a sincerement
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|                         aucun argument par profil a faire valoir : la
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|                         valeur est un residu de script, de la meme facon
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                         sur tous les types de machines, et le bon etat
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                         est celui que livre Windows. Inventer une raison
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|                         pour que le serveur ou le portable la conserve
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|                         reviendrait a inventer une difference a partir de
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                         rien. La reponse 5 fait ici exactement la meme
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|                         chose que les reponses 1 a 4, ce qui est bien ce
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|                         qu'on attend d'une carte dont tout le contenu
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|                         tient en « remettre comme c'etait ».
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|  Non verifie (en)  : The existing card asserted that without preemption a
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                      long shader cannot be interrupted, the desktop
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                      stutters and TDR recovery has nothing to work with.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                      That is the mechanism preemption provides in
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                      general, but I have not verified that
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                      EnablePreemption=0 on a current WDDM 3.x driver
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|                      actually disables it - the key is undocumented and
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                      drivers may ignore it. I have therefore stated it
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|                      conditionally rather than as fact. The deletion
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|                      itself is correct regardless, since absence is the
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|                      shipped state.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|  Cible           : call :killkey HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Gr
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|                    aphicsDrivers\Scheduler EnablePreemption - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|                    line 1927
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|RESTORE GPU PREEMPTION TO THE SHIPPED STATE
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|  What it is      : EnablePreemption under GraphicsDrivers\Scheduler, a
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                    value that appears on machines that have been through
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                    a GPU tuning script. Windows does not create this key.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value if it is there, leaving the GPU
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                    scheduler on the driver's own preemption behaviour.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|  Gain            : Returning the machine to the state a fresh install is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|                    in - nothing more, and it is worth being blunt about
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|                    that. This is not a Microsoft-documented value, and
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    there is no published measurement showing that setting
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    it to 0 gains frames or that deleting it costs any.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    The reason to delete is that absence is what Windows
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    ships, not that removal buys you something. If it did,
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    this card would say a number.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|  Cost            : None. Nothing on a normal machine reads this key
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                    deliberately.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|  Windows default : Absent.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|    DELETE               : The value is removed and the GPU scheduler is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|                           left on whatever preemption granularity the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|                           driver reports it can do - which is the only
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.028|                           state a fresh Windows install has ever been in,
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.029|                           because Windows does not create this key.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.030|    0                    : What tuning scripts write, with the claim that
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.031|                           it removes scheduling overhead. Preemption is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.032|                           the mechanism that lets the GPU interrupt a
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.033|                           long draw or compute job mid-flight so
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.034|                           something else - the desktop, a higher-priority
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.035|                           queue, the watchdog's reset - can get in. If
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.036|                           the value does what those scripts claim, the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.037|                           trade is a stuttering desktop under GPU load
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.038|                           and a TDR reset with less to work with. I have
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.039|                           found no published measurement showing it gains
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.040|                           anything, so treat the claim as unsupported in
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.041|                           both directions and just delete it.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.042|    1                    : Setting 1 is not the restore either, even
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.043|                           though it reads like 'preemption on'. Windows
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.044|                           ships this key absent, so 1 is a written
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.045|                           override that happens to agree with the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.046|                           driver's own default - and it would keep
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.047|                           overriding it if a future driver wanted
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.048|                           something else. Deletion is the restore.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete, and there is genuinely no per-
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|                       profile argument to make: the value is script
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|                       residue on every kind of machine equally, and the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                       correct state is the one Windows ships. Inventing a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                       reason for the server or the laptop to keep it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|                       would be inventing a difference out of nothing.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|                       Answer 5 does the same thing as answers 1 to 4
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                       here, which is exactly what you would expect from a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|                       card whose whole content is 'put it back how it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|                       came'.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|  Unverified      : The existing card asserted that without preemption a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    long shader cannot be interrupted, the desktop
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    stutters and TDR recovery has nothing to work with.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    That is the mechanism preemption provides in general,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    but I have not verified that EnablePreemption=0 on a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    current WDDM 3.x driver actually disables it - the key
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                    is undocumented and drivers may ignore it. I have
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|                    therefore stated it conditionally rather than as fact.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                    The deletion itself is correct regardless, since
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|                    absence is the shipped state.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|  Target          : call :killkey HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Gr
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|                    aphicsDrivers\Scheduler EnablePreemption - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|                    line 1927
