::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.001|RE-ENABLE MULTI-PLANE OVERLAY
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.002|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.003|  What it is      : The undo for the flicker workaround, reached from the
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.004|                    display menu: it removes the three values rather than
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.005|                    zeroing them.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.006|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes OverlayTestMode, OverlayMinFPS and
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.008|                    DisableOverlays. Reboot required.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.009|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.010|  Gain            : DWM stops doing a full-screen composite pass per frame
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.011|                    while a window is on top, and a covering borderless
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.012|                    window can be scanned straight out of a hardware
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.013|                    plane. Lower GPU power and one less stage of latency
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.014|                    in windowed and borderless play, and variable refresh
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.015|                    rate works in borderless again. Nothing changes in
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.016|                    exclusive fullscreen, where MPO was never in the path.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.017|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.018|  Cost            : If MPO genuinely was the cause of a flicker on this
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.019|                    machine, it comes back - in which case the flicker
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.020|                    card is where you put the workaround back,
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.021|                    deliberately.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.022|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.023|  Windows default : All three absent, MPO on. This is the default.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.024|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.025|  Possible values:
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.026|    DELETE               : The three values are removed and the
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.027|                           presentation path goes back to what Windows
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.028|                           ships: DWM offers each window's swapchain to
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.029|                           the driver, and the driver decides frame by
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.030|                           frame whether it can be scanned out from a
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.031|                           hardware plane. Reboot to take effect.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.032|    0 on any of the three :
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.033|        Does not undo the workaround. All three have absence as their
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.034|        default, so a zero is a written override that reads as zero - a
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.035|        third state. This is the single most common mistake in scripts
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.036|        that claim to 're-enable MPO', and it is why this menu entry
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.037|        deletes instead of writing.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.038|    SKIP                 : Leaves the machine wherever it is. Only
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.039|                           sensible if you disabled MPO on purpose, the
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.040|                           flicker really did stop, and you have not since
::T|EN|dt.mpo.on.041|                           changed cable, monitor or driver.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete. This is not a preference dressed
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.002|                       up as one: no profile is better off with the
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.003|                       hardware path suppressed, and the values are ones
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.004|                       Windows never writes, so the correct state is
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.005|                       identical everywhere. The server profile deletes as
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.006|                       well - a headless box neither gains nor loses, but
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.007|                       leaving stale values for whoever attaches a monitor
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.008|                       next is worse than spending one reg delete. If you
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.009|                       have just come here from disabling MPO on purpose
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.010|                       and the flicker is genuinely gone, that is the one
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.011|                       case for skipping, and it is a decision about your
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.012|                       monitors, not about your profile.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.013|
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.014|  Known problems  : None from re-enabling. This is the first thing to try
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.015|                    on a second-hand or 'already optimised' machine where
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.016|                    borderless games have lost VRR or feel a frame behind.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.017|
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.018|  Target          : reg delete HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm /v
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.019|                    OverlayTestMode /f ; same key /v OverlayMinFPS ; reg
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.020|                    delete
::X|EN|dt.mpo.on.021|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
