::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.014|                    machine, aucune cle de strategie, aucun autre compte
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.015|                    touche.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.016|
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.017|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable. Les ecarts d'images par seconde
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.018|                    rapportes tournent autour de 0 a 3 %, ce qui sur
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.019|                    n'importe quel jeu moderne tient dans la variation
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.020|                    d'une execution a l'autre, et le mecanisme invoque - «
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.021|                    ca arrete l'enregistrement en arriere-plan » - est
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.022|                    tout simplement faux sur une machine par defaut,
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.023|                    puisque l'enregistrement en arriere-plan est desactive
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.024|                    d'origine. La description exacte est : un composant de
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.025|                    capture de moins qui s'arme au lancement d'un jeu, et
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.026|                    la certitude que la Game Bar n'ecrira pas de video sur
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.027|                    votre disque. Ce sont deux raisons valables d'en
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.028|                    vouloir. Aucune des deux n'est un gain de FPS.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.029|
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.030|  Cout            : Vous perdez l'enregistreur de clips de Windows pour ce
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.031|                    compte : WIN+ALT+R et le bouton des 30 dernieres
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.032|                    secondes. La Game Bar, son compteur d'images et ses
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.033|                    widgets s'ouvrent toujours avec WIN+G.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.034|
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.035|  Defaut Windows  : 1.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.036|
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.037|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.038|    0                    : La Game Bar cesse de s'enregistrer comme cible
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.039|                           de capture pour ce compte. Le crochet de
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.040|                           capture ne s'arme pas au lancement d'un jeu ou
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.041|                           d'une appli plein ecran. Rien d'autre ne change
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.042|                           dans la Game Bar : WIN+G, le compteur d'images,
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.043|                           les widgets et le mixeur audio s'ouvrent
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.044|                           toujours.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.045|    1                    : La valeur livree : la capture est disponible.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.046|                           Disponible est le mot important - cela signifie
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.047|                           que le crochet peut s'armer, pas que quoi que
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.048|                           ce soit enregistre. Ce qui decide si un
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.049|                           enregistrement a reellement lieu en arriere-
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.050|                           plan, c'est HistoricalCaptureEnabled, une autre
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.051|                           valeur, livree a 0.
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.052|    DELETE               : Windows interprete l'absence comme active et
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.053|                           recree toute la cle GameConfigStore a la
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.054|                           prochaine ouverture de session : supprimer
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.055|                           revient donc a 1, mais en s'en remettant au
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.056|                           systeme. OPTY ecrit 1 a la place, conformement
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.057|                           a la regle du projet : un bon defaut s'ecrit
::T|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.058|                           explicitement plutot qu'il ne se suppose.
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Volontairement identique aux deux autres cartes
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.002|                         Game DVR - 0 pour jeu et serveur, 1 pour
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.003|                         bureautique, portable et Windows - parce que le
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.004|                         meme chiffre doit signifier la meme intention
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.005|                         quel que soit le menu par lequel on arrive a la
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.006|                         valeur. Le jeu repond 0 parce que quiconque prend
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.007|                         la capture au serieux utilise OBS, ShadowPlay ou
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.008|                         ReLive, et qu'avoir un element de moins accroche
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.009|                         a un swapchain plein ecran au lancement est une
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.010|                         preference legitime, meme sans mesure a l'appui.
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.011|                         Le serveur repond 0 parce qu'il n'y a personne au
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.012|                         clavier. Bureautique et portable repondent 1 : la
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.013|                         bureautique veut ses fonctions actives, et le
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.014|                         portable ne gagne rien en autonomie avec 0 -
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.015|                         pretendre le contraire reviendrait a inventer une
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.016|                         difference. La reponse 5 ecrit 1.
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.017|
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.018|  Non verifie (en)  : This card and mo-gamedvr-off describe the same
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.019|                      :map_only block from two angles - reg-gamedvr-
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.020|                      enabled is line 2108 alone, mo-gamedvr-off is lines
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.021|                      2108 and 2113 together. Their tables are
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.022|                      deliberately identical, but they must not reach the
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.023|                      user as two separate questions about the same value;
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.024|                      the harness should merge them or drop one. The
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.025|                      HistoricalCaptureEnabled value name should be
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.026|                      confirmed on the live machine, since the honesty
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.027|                      argument in the gain field rests on it.
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.028|
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.029|  Cible           : reg add HKEY_CURRENT_USER\System\GameConfigStore /v
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.030|                    GameDVR_Enabled /t REG_DWORD /d 00000000 /f - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|reg.gamedvr.enabled.031|                    label :map_only, line 2108
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.001|GAME DVR OFF FOR THIS USER (GAMEDVR_ENABLED = 0)
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.002|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.003|  What it is      : The per-user switch under HKCU\System\GameConfigStore.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.004|                    It is one of three separate Game DVR values and it is
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.005|                    worth keeping them apart: GameDVR_Enabled decides
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.006|                    whether Game Bar registers as a capture target at all,
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.007|                    AppCaptureEnabled gates the capture feature in
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.008|                    Settings, and HistoricalCaptureEnabled - the one
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.009|                    people actually mean when they say 'it records in the
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.010|                    background' - ships at 0.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.011|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.012|  Actual effect   : Writes 0 for the account running OPTY. Nothing
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.013|                    machine-wide, no policy key, no other account
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.014|                    affected.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.015|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.016|  Gain            : No measurable gain. Reported frame-rate differences
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.017|                    cluster at 0-3%, which on any modern game is inside
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.018|                    run-to-run variance, and the mechanism people cite for
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.019|                    it - 'it stops recording in the background' - is
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.020|                    simply wrong on a default machine, because background
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.021|                    recording is off out of the box. The accurate
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.022|                    description is: one fewer capture component arming
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.023|                    itself at game start, and a certainty that Game Bar is
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.024|                    not going to write video to your drive. Both are
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.025|                    reasons someone might want it. Neither is FPS.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.026|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.027|  Cost            : You lose the Windows clip recorder for this account:
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.028|                    WIN+ALT+R and the last-30-seconds button. Game Bar,
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.029|                    its FPS counter and its widgets still open with WIN+G.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.030|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.031|  Windows default : 1.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.032|
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.033|  Possible values:
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.034|    0                    : Game Bar stops registering as a capture target
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.035|                           for this account. The capture hook does not arm
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.036|                           when a game or full-screen app starts. Nothing
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.037|                           else in the Game Bar changes: WIN+G, the FPS
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.038|                           counter, the widgets, the audio mixer all still
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.039|                           open.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.040|    1                    : The shipped value: capture is available.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.041|                           Available is the operative word - it means the
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.042|                           hook can arm, not that anything is recording.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.043|                           What decides whether recording actually happens
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.044|                           in the background is HistoricalCaptureEnabled,
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.045|                           a different value, which ships at 0.
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.046|    DELETE               : Windows treats the absent value as enabled and
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.047|                           recreates the whole GameConfigStore key at the
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.048|                           next sign-in, so deleting behaves like 1 but
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.049|                           relies on the OS getting round to it. OPTY
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.050|                           writes 1 instead, per the project rule that a
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.051|                           good default is written explicitly rather than
::T|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.052|                           assumed.
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.001|  Why these profiles : Identical to the other two Game DVR cards on
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.002|                       purpose - 0 for gaming and server, 1 for office,
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.003|                       laptop and Windows - because the same digit has to
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.004|                       mean the same intent no matter which menu you
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.005|                       reached the value from. Gaming answers 0 because
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.006|                       anyone serious about capture is using OBS,
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.007|                       ShadowPlay or ReLive, and one fewer thing hooking a
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.008|                       fullscreen swapchain at launch is a legitimate
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.009|                       preference even with no benchmark behind it. Server
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.010|                       answers 0 because there is nobody at the keyboard.
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.011|                       Office and laptop answer 1: office wants features
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.012|                       on, and the laptop gets no battery benefit from 0,
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.013|                       so claiming one would be inventing a difference.
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.014|                       Answer 5 writes 1.
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.015|
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.016|  Unverified      : This card and mo-gamedvr-off describe the same
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.017|                    :map_only block from two angles - reg-gamedvr-enabled
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.018|                    is line 2108 alone, mo-gamedvr-off is lines 2108 and
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.019|                    2113 together. Their tables are deliberately
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.020|                    identical, but they must not reach the user as two
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.021|                    separate questions about the same value; the harness
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.022|                    should merge them or drop one. The
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.023|                    HistoricalCaptureEnabled value name should be
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.024|                    confirmed on the live machine, since the honesty
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.025|                    argument in the gain field rests on it.
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.026|
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.027|  Target          : reg add HKEY_CURRENT_USER\System\GameConfigStore /v
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.028|                    GameDVR_Enabled /t REG_DWORD /d 00000000 /f - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|reg.gamedvr.enabled.029|                    label :map_only, line 2108
