:: ---- gr.mouse.defaults (preference) ---------------------------------
::P|gr.mouse.defaults|SKIP|SKIP|1|1|1|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.001|VALEURS DE SOURIS DANS LA PASSE DE RESTAURATION GLOBALE
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.002|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.003|  Ce que c est    : Les quatre mêmes valeurs REG_SZ que la restauration
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.004|                    autonome, mais écrites depuis le bloc de restauration
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.005|                    globale qui remet aussi en place les réglages jeu,
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.006|                    debloat et services. Le contrôle des courbes
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.007|                    SmoothMouse et l'application à chaud font partie de la
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.008|                    même passe.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.009|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.010|  Effet reel      : Écrit MouseSensitivity=10, MouseSpeed=1,
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.011|                    MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10 — la courbe
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.012|                    d'accélération Windows — sans question spécifique à la
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.013|                    souris, puisque l'utilisateur a déjà accepté une
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.014|                    restauration complète.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.015|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.016|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable, et il ne faut pas le vendre
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.017|                    comme tel. L'objectif, c'est une machine qui se
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.018|                    comporte de bout en bout comme un Windows d'origine —
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.019|                    précisément ce que demande quelqu'un qui lance une
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.020|                    restauration globale.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.021|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.022|  Cout            : L'accélération revient sur le bureau, et une position
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.023|                    de curseur de vitesse choisie exprès est remise au
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.024|                    cran du milieu. Bon à savoir : la plupart des jeux
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.025|                    utilisent le raw input et ignorent totalement ces
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.026|                    quatre valeurs — cela change donc la sensation sur le
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.027|                    bureau, et pas grand-chose d'autre.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.028|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.029|  Defaut Windows  : 10 / 1 / 6 / 10, les quatre présentes en REG_SZ.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.030|                    Précision du pointeur cochée.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.031|
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.033|    MouseSpeed=1 (with MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10) :
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.034|        Ce qu'écrit le bloc : l'accélération revient, l'état d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.035|        Identique à la restauration autonome du sous-menu souris — mêmes
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.036|        quatre lignes reg add, mêmes valeurs.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.037|    MouseSpeed=0 (with both thresholds at 0) :
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.038|        Accélération coupée. Ce bloc ne l'écrit jamais : la restauration
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.039|        globale ne va jamais que vers l'état d'origine.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.040|    MouseSpeed=2         : Le coude de quadruplement hérité d'avant XP.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.041|                           Non écrit ici, et produit par aucune interface
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.042|                           Windows.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.043|    MouseSensitivity=10  : Ramené de force au cran du milieu. Comme cela
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.044|                           s'exécute au sein d'une passe globale « tout
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.045|                           restaurer » sans question séparée, un
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.046|                           utilisateur qui avait délibérément mis le
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.047|                           curseur sur 7 ou 14 perd ce choix sans qu'on
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.048|                           lui ait parlé de la souris.
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.049|    SKIP                 : La partie souris de la restauration globale est
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.050|                           écartée ; le reste de la passe s'exécute quand
::T|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.051|                           même.
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Identique à la carte de restauration autonome, et
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.002|                         c'est la réponse honnête plutôt qu'une différence
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.003|                         fabriquée : ce sont littéralement les quatre
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.004|                         mêmes lignes reg add sous une autre étiquette.
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.005|                         Les profils 3, 4 et 5 écrivent le jeu de valeurs
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.006|                         d'origine. Le 1 jeu écarte la partie souris pour
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.007|                         qu'une restauration globale n'annule pas en
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.008|                         silence un pointeur 1:1. Le 2 serveur passe : des
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.009|                         valeurs HKCU de souris n'ont aucun sens sur une
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.010|                         machine devant laquelle personne ne s'assoit. Si
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.011|                         votre implémentation pilote toute la restauration
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.012|                         avec un seul chiffre, la partie souris doit
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.013|                         respecter ce chiffre au lieu de s'exécuter
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.014|                         systématiquement.
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.015|
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.016|  Non verifie (en)  : Same caveat as the standalone restore:
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.017|                      HKEY_USERS\.DEFAULT proves what a new account gets,
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.018|                      not necessarily what this particular machine shipped
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.019|                      with if an OEM utility ever wrote to Control
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.020|                      Panel\Mouse.
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.021|
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.022|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values MouseSensitivity,
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.023|                    MouseSpeed, MouseThreshold1, MouseThreshold2, all
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.024|                    REG_SZ. Written by the global restore-everything block
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.025|                    at OPTY.bat lines 1688-1694 as 10 / 1 / 6 / 10, then
::X|FR|gr.mouse.defaults.026|                    call :mousecurve and call :mouseapply.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.001|MOUSE VALUES INSIDE THE GLOBAL RESTORE-EVERYTHING PASS
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.002|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.003|  What it is      : The same four REG_SZ values as the standalone restore,
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.004|                    but written from the bulk restore block that also puts
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.005|                    gaming, debloat and service settings back. It runs the
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.006|                    SmoothMouse curve check and the live-apply call as
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.007|                    part of the same pass.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.008|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.009|  Actual effect   : Writes MouseSensitivity=10, MouseSpeed=1,
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.010|                    MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10 — the Windows
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.011|                    acceleration curve — with no mouse-specific prompt,
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.012|                    because the user already agreed to a full restore.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.013|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.014|  Gain            : No measurable gain, and it should not be sold as one.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.015|                    The point is a machine that behaves like a stock
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.016|                    Windows install end to end, which is exactly what
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.017|                    someone running a global restore has asked for.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.018|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.019|  Cost            : Acceleration returns on the desktop, and a
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.020|                    deliberately chosen pointer-speed slider position is
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.021|                    reset to the middle notch. Worth knowing: most games
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.022|                    use raw input and ignore all four values entirely, so
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.023|                    this changes the desktop feel and very little else.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.024|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.025|  Windows default : 10 / 1 / 6 / 10, all present as REG_SZ. Enhance
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.026|                    pointer precision ticked.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.027|
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.029|    MouseSpeed=1 (with MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10) :
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.030|        What the block writes: acceleration back on, the stock state.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.031|        Identical to the standalone restore in the mouse submenu — same
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.032|        four reg add lines, same values.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.033|    MouseSpeed=0 (with both thresholds at 0) :
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.034|        Acceleration off. This block never writes it — the global restore
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.035|        only ever moves toward the stock state.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.036|    MouseSpeed=2         : Legacy quadrupling knee from the pre-XP model.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.037|                           Not written here and not produced by any
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.038|                           Windows UI.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.039|    MouseSensitivity=10  : Forced back to the middle notch. Because this
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.040|                           runs inside a bulk 'restore everything' pass
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.041|                           with no separate prompt, a user who
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.042|                           deliberately set the slider to 7 or 14 loses
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.043|                           that choice without being asked about the mouse
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.044|                           specifically.
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.045|    SKIP                 : The mouse portion of the global restore is left
::T|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.046|                           out; the rest of the restore pass still runs.
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.001|  Why these profiles : Identical to the standalone restore card, which is
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.002|                       the honest answer rather than a manufactured
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.003|                       difference — it is literally the same four reg add
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.004|                       lines in a different label. 3 office, 4 laptop and
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.005|                       5 Windows write the stock set. 1 gaming skips the
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.006|                       mouse part of the restore so a global restore does
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.007|                       not silently undo a 1:1 pointer. 2 server skips
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.008|                       because HKCU mouse values mean nothing on a box
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.009|                       nobody sits at. If your implementation drives the
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.010|                       whole restore from one digit, the mouse portion
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.011|                       should honour that digit rather than run
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.012|                       unconditionally.
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.013|
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.014|  Unverified      : Same caveat as the standalone restore:
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.015|                    HKEY_USERS\.DEFAULT proves what a new account gets,
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.016|                    not necessarily what this particular machine shipped
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.017|                    with if an OEM utility ever wrote to Control
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.018|                    Panel\Mouse.
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.019|
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.020|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values MouseSensitivity,
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.021|                    MouseSpeed, MouseThreshold1, MouseThreshold2, all
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.022|                    REG_SZ. Written by the global restore-everything block
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.023|                    at OPTY.bat lines 1688-1694 as 10 / 1 / 6 / 10, then
::X|EN|gr.mouse.defaults.024|                    call :mousecurve and call :mouseapply.
::
:: ---- mouse.smoothcurve.delete (repair) --------------------------
::P|mouse.smoothcurve.delete|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|REVIEW|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|SUPPRIMER LES COURBES SMOOTHMOUSE QUI NE FONT PAS EXACTEMENT 40 OCTETS
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|  Ce que c est    : SmoothMouseXCurve et SmoothMouseYCurve sont les tables
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                    d'accélération REG_BINARY sous HKCU\Control
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                    Panel\Mouse — les courbes qu'utilise le code de
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                    balistique du pointeur quand « Améliorer la précision
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                    du pointeur » est actif. Une paire d'origine fait 40
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|                    octets chacune, que reg query affiche en 80 caractères
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|                    hexadécimaux. OPTY v03.x en écrivait des versions
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|                    tronquées de 17 et 20 octets.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|  Effet reel      : reg query affiche le blob, et le garde-fou ne conserve
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|                    la valeur que si la chaîne hexadécimale fait
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|                    exactement 80 caractères. Tout le reste est supprimé :
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|                    les blobs courts laissés par une ancienne version
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|                    d'OPTY, mais aussi tout blob de plus de 40 octets. La
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|                    valeur disparue, Windows retombe sur sa courbe interne
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|                    : le pointeur se comporte correctement dès cet
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|                    instant.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|  Gain            : Cela retire une valeur malformée qu'une version
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                    antérieure de ce même script a créée. Qu'une courbe
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                    tronquée ait jamais produit un symptôme perceptible
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|                    n'est pas établi — n'attendez aucun changement au
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|                    pointeur. Sur une machine qui n'a jamais vu la v03.x,
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|                    rien n'est supprimé. La description honnête, c'est un
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|                    rangement après un de nos anciens bugs, pas la
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|                    correction d'un problème de pointeur que vous auriez.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|  Cout            : Une courbe personnalisée faite main est détruite si
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|                    elle ne fait pas exactement 40 octets. Les courbes
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.032|                    personnalisées font 40 octets parce que c'est le
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.033|                    format, donc le risque est quasi théorique — mais le
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.034|                    test porte sur une longueur exacte, pas sur «
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.035|                    inférieur à », et cette nuance mérite d'être dite
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.036|                    plutôt que passée sous silence.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.037|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.038|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs présentes, 40 octets chacune.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.039|                    L'absence est également un état valide : Windows
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.040|                    dispose d'un repli interne et fonctionne très bien
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.041|                    sans elles.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.042|
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.043|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.044|    blob is exactly 40 bytes (80 hex characters) :
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.045|        Conservé, intact. C'est la longueur d'origine, et aussi celle de
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.046|        toute courbe personnalisée légitime : le garde-fou rend donc
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.047|        l'opération totalement neutre sur une machine saine.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.048|    blob is shorter than 40 bytes :
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.049|        Supprimé. C'est le cas pour lequel la réparation existe : OPTY
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.050|        v03.x écrivait des blobs tronqués de 17 et 20 octets. Un blob
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.051|        court n'est une structure de courbe valide sous aucun angle.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.052|    blob is longer than 40 bytes :
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.053|        Supprimé également — et c'est une correction de l'ancienne carte,
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.054|        qui annonçait un test « inférieur à 40 octets ». Le garde-fou
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.055|        teste une longueur exacte : tout ce qui ne fait pas exactement 80
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.056|        caractères hexadécimaux dégage. En pratique aucun producteur connu
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.057|        n'écrit de courbe plus longue, mais le comportement mérite d'être
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.058|        décrit exactement.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.059|    value absent         : Un état parfaitement valide. Windows dispose
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.060|                           d'une courbe de repli interne : le pointeur se
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.061|                           comporte correctement même sans ces valeurs.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.062|                           Elles réapparaissent dès que quelque chose les
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.063|                           réécrit — l'onglet Options du pointeur le fait
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.064|                           quand on coche ou décoche « Améliorer la
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.065|                           précision du pointeur ».
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.066|    SKIP                 : Laisse ce qui s'y trouve, y compris un blob
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.067|                           tronqué écrit par une ancienne version d'OPTY.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq sont identiques, et c'est la bonne
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|                         réponse, pas un trou dans l'analyse. Le garde-fou
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|                         rend l'opération neutre sur une machine saine :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                         aucun profil ne gagne à laisser une valeur
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                         malformée en place, y compris le 5 Windows,
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                         puisque supprimer un blob tronqué rapproche du
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                         comportement d'origine au lieu de l'en éloigner.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|                         Le 2 serveur l'exécute aussi : le contrôle coûte
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|                         un reg query, et une machine sans écran a pu
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|                         hériter de la même mauvaise valeur lors d'un
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|                         ancien passage.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|  Problemes connus : Le seul producteur de courbes tronquées que je peux
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|                     nommer est OPTY v03.x lui-même. Je ne peux citer ni
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|                     application ni symptôme causé par elles, donc cette
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|                     carte n'en revendique aucun.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|  Non verifie (en)  : Two claims I could not verify and will not repeat as
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|                      fact. The old card said Windows 'regenerates a
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|                      correct default curve at the next logon'; what I can
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|                      support is that the built-in fallback curve is used
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                      when the values are absent — whether the registry
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                      values are physically re-created at logon, or only
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|                      when something writes them, I did not test. And I
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|                      have no reproduction of a truncated curve producing
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|                      observable pointer misbehaviour, so the 'repairs
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|                      erratic pointer behaviour' line from the old card is
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|                      unsupported.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values SmoothMouseXCurve
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|                    and SmoothMouseYCurve (REG_BINARY). Guarded delete in
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.032|                    :mousecurve / :curvecheck, OPTY.bat lines 2348-2362.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.033|                    The guard is `if "%CV:~80,1%"=="" if not
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.034|                    "%CV:~79,1%"=="" goto :eof` — keep only when the hex
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.035|                    string is exactly 80 characters.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|REMOVE SMOOTHMOUSE CURVES THAT ARE NOT EXACTLY 40 BYTES
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|  What it is      : SmoothMouseXCurve and SmoothMouseYCurve are the
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                    REG_BINARY acceleration lookup tables under
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                    HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — the curves the pointer-
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                    ballistics code uses when Enhance pointer precision is
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                    on. A stock pair is 40 bytes each, which reg query
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|                    prints as 80 hex characters. OPTY v03.x used to write
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|                    truncated 17 and 20 byte versions.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|  Actual effect   : reg query prints the blob, and the guard keeps the
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|                    value only when the hex string is exactly 80
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|                    characters long. Anything else is deleted — the short
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|                    blobs an old OPTY left behind, and also any blob
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|                    longer than 40 bytes. With the value gone, Windows
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|                    falls back to its built-in curve, so pointer behaviour
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|                    is correct from that moment on.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|  Gain            : It removes a malformed value that an earlier version
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|                    of this same script created. Whether a truncated curve
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|                    ever produced a symptom you could feel is not
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                    established — do not expect the pointer to change. On
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                    a machine that never ran v03.x, nothing is deleted at
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|                    all. The honest description is tidying up after an old
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|                    bug of ours, not a fix for a pointer problem you have.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|  Cost            : A hand-tuned custom curve is destroyed if it is not
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|                    exactly 40 bytes. Custom curves are 40 bytes because
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|                    that is the format, so this is close to theoretical —
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|                    but it is an exact-length test, not a 'shorter than'
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|                    test, and that distinction should be stated rather
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.032|                    than glossed over.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.033|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.034|  Windows default : Both values present at 40 bytes each. Absent is also a
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.035|                    valid state: Windows has a built-in fallback and works
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.036|                    fine without them.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.037|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.038|  Possible values:
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.039|    blob is exactly 40 bytes (80 hex characters) :
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.040|        Kept, untouched. This is the stock length and also the length of
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.041|        any legitimately customised curve, so the guard makes the whole
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.042|        operation a no-op on a healthy machine.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.043|    blob is shorter than 40 bytes :
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.044|        Deleted. This is the case the repair exists for: OPTY v03.x wrote
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.045|        truncated 17 and 20 byte blobs. A short blob is not a valid curve
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.046|        structure under any reading.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.047|    blob is longer than 40 bytes :
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.048|        Also deleted — and this is a correction to the old card, which
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.049|        claimed the test was 'shorter than 40 bytes'. The guard is an
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.050|        exact-length test: anything that is not exactly 80 hex characters
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.051|        goes. In practice no known writer produces a longer curve, but the
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.052|        behaviour should be described accurately.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.053|    value absent         : A perfectly valid state. Windows has a built-in
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.054|                           fallback curve, so pointer behaviour is correct
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.055|                           with the values gone. The registry values
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.056|                           reappear the next time something writes them —
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.057|                           the Pointer Options tab does when you tick or
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.058|                           untick Enhance pointer precision.
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.059|    SKIP                 : Leaves whatever is there, including a truncated
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.060|                           blob an old OPTY wrote.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|  Why these profiles : All five identical, and that is the correct answer
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|                       rather than a gap in the analysis. The guard makes
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|                       this a no-op on a healthy machine, so no profile
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                       benefits from leaving a malformed value in place —
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                       including 5 Windows, since deleting a truncated
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                       blob moves toward shipped behaviour, not away from
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                       it. 2 server runs it too: the check costs one reg
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|                       query and a headless box can have inherited the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|                       same bad value from an old run.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|  Known problems  : The only writer of truncated curves I can name is OPTY
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|                    v03.x itself. I cannot name an application and a
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|                    symptom caused by them, so this card claims none.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|  Unverified      : Two claims I could not verify and will not repeat as
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|                    fact. The old card said Windows 'regenerates a correct
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|                    default curve at the next logon'; what I can support
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|                    is that the built-in fallback curve is used when the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|                    values are absent — whether the registry values are
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|                    physically re-created at logon, or only when something
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|                    writes them, I did not test. And I have no
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                    reproduction of a truncated curve producing observable
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                    pointer misbehaviour, so the 'repairs erratic pointer
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|                    behaviour' line from the old card is unsupported.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values SmoothMouseXCurve
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|                    and SmoothMouseYCurve (REG_BINARY). Guarded delete in
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|                    :mousecurve / :curvecheck, OPTY.bat lines 2348-2362.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|                    The guard is `if "%CV:~80,1%"=="" if not
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|                    "%CV:~79,1%"=="" goto :eof` — keep only when the hex
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|                    string is exactly 80 characters.
