:: ---- rad.defender.policy.purge (repair) -------------------------
::P|rad.defender.policy.purge|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|RETIRER LES STRATEGIES QUI TUENT DEFENDER
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.003|  Ce que c est    : Six valeurs de strategie (REG_DWORD) que les scripts
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.004|                    de debloat ecrivent pour maintenir Microsoft Defender
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.005|                    eteint : DisableAntiSpyware et DisableAntiVirus sous
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.006|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.007|                    plus DisableRealtimeMonitoring,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.008|                    DisableBehaviorMonitoring, DisableOnAccessProtection
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.009|                    et DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable dans la sous-cle Real-
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.010|                    Time Protection. Ce sont des strategies : elles
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.011|                    passent au-dessus de l'application Securite Windows et
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.012|                    grisent ses boutons.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.013|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.014|  Effet reel      : Supprime les six valeurs. Les cles restent (une cle
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.015|                    vide ne gene personne, et supprimer des cles n'est pas
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.016|                    le role de ce script). Securite Windows cesse
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.017|                    d'afficher un etat gere et son bouton de protection en
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.018|                    temps reel redevient utilisable. Aucun redemarrage :
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.019|                    Defender prend le changement en compte immediatement.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.020|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.021|  Gain            : Honnetement, tout depend d'une chose invisible d'ici.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.022|                    Si la protection contre les falsifications est ACTIVE
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.023|                    - le cas par defaut sur un Windows 11 grand public -
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.024|                    Defender ignorait deja ces valeurs, l'analyse en temps
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.025|                    reel tournait deja, et le seul changement mesurable
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.026|                    est cosmetique : Securite Windows abandonne la
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.027|                    banniere « parametres geres par votre organisation »
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.028|                    et le bouton grise revient. Si elle est COUPEE, le
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.029|                    gain est bien reel : la protection en temps reel etait
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.030|                    vraiment eteinte et redemarre. Dans les deux cas,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.031|                    aucun gain de performance : ceci coute des
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.032|                    performances, ca n'en rapporte pas.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.033|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.034|  Cout            : L'analyse en temps reel consomme des entrees/sorties
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.035|                    disque. Compilation, extraction de grosses archives,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.036|                    premier lancement d'un jeu : c'est un peu plus lent,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.037|                    quelques pour cent en general, davantage sur un disque
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.038|                    lent. La reponse a ca est une exclusion de dossier,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.039|                    pas un antivirus eteint. Avec un antivirus tiers,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.040|                    celui-ci s'enregistre aupres de Securite Windows et
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.041|                    Defender bascule tout seul en mode passif : vous
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.042|                    n'aurez jamais deux moteurs qui se marchent dessus.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.043|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.044|  Defaut Windows  : Les six absentes. Le programme d'installation de
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.045|                    Windows n'en ecrit aucune ; toute valeur presente
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.046|                    vient d'un script ou d'une GPO.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.047|
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.048|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.049|    1                    : La strategie dit "eteins ce composant".
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.050|                           DisableAntiSpyware=1 etait l'ancien
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.051|                           interrupteur general du moteur Defender ;
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.052|                           Microsoft l'a deprecie et les machines dont la
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.053|                           protection contre les falsifications est active
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.054|                           l'ignorent purement et simplement. Les quatre
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.055|                           valeurs de Real-Time Protection, elles, mordent
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.056|                           encore quand la protection contre les
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.057|                           falsifications est coupee :
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.058|                           DisableRealtimeMonitoring supprime l'analyse a
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.059|                           l'acces, DisableBehaviorMonitoring coupe le
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.060|                           moteur comportemental,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.061|                           DisableOnAccessProtection coupe l'analyse a
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.062|                           l'ouverture et a la fermeture des fichiers,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.063|                           DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable supprime l'analyse
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.064|                           de rattrapage lancee quand la protection temps
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.065|                           reel est reactivee. Effet ou pas, leur simple
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.066|                           presence grise le bouton dans Securite Windows
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.067|                           et affiche "gere par votre organisation".
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.068|    0                    : Le piege. Ecrire 0 ne restaure PAS le defaut :
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.069|                           cela cree un troisieme etat, une strategie qui
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.070|                           dit "force a l'allumage". Le composant tourne,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.071|                           mais le bouton reste grise dans Securite
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.072|                           Windows et la machine continue de se declarer
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.073|                           geree. Tout ce qui voudra ensuite basculer la
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.074|                           protection temps reel (l'installeur d'un
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.075|                           antivirus tiers, une mise a jour de Defender,
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.076|                           vous) se heurtera a votre propre strategie.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.077|    DELETE               : Le vrai defaut : aucune strategie. Defender
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.078|                           obeit a l'application Securite Windows, les
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.079|                           boutons redeviennent cliquables, et l'etat que
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.080|                           vous reglez dans l'interface est l'etat reel.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.081|                           C'est ce que le script applique pour les cinq
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.082|                           profils.
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils suppriment, y compris le profil
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|                         5, parce que l'absence est vraiment ce que
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.003|                         Windows livre. Aucun usage de machine ne justifie
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.004|                         une strategie cachee qui desactive l'antivirus en
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.005|                         silence, et la machine qui seede des torrents et
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.006|                         expose Plex sur Internet est la derniere qui
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.007|                         devrait l'avoir. Meme avec un antivirus tiers
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.008|                         assume, vous voulez ces valeurs parties : votre
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.009|                         antivirus ecarte Defender par son propre
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.010|                         enregistrement en mode passif, pas par ces cles,
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.011|                         et les laisser signifie que Defender ne pourra
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.012|                         pas revenir le jour ou vous desinstallerez
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.013|                         l'autre produit. Ce tableau n'a aucune variation,
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.014|                         et en inventer une serait malhonnete.
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.015|
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.016|  Problemes connus : Rien de connu ne casse en les supprimant. La casse
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.017|                     est dans l'autre sens : avec ces valeurs en place et
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.018|                     la protection contre les falsifications coupee,
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.019|                     Securite Windows reste bloque sur un « La protection
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.020|                     contre les virus et menaces est desactivee » rouge
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.021|                     qu'aucun bouton de l'interface ne peut effacer, et
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.022|                     certains installeurs d'antivirus tiers abandonnent
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.023|                     parce qu'ils n'arrivent pas a interroger l'etat de
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.024|                     Defender.
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.025|
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.026|  Non verifie (en)  : With Tamper Protection ON, Defender already ignores
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.027|                      all six, so the deletion may change nothing you can
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.028|                      observe apart from the Windows Security page
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.029|                      dropping its "managed" badge. I cannot tell from the
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.030|                      registry alone whether Tamper Protection is on for a
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.031|                      given machine; it lives under
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.032|                      HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.033|                      Defender\Features\TamperProtection, which is itself
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.034|                      tamper-protected and unreliable to read. Deleting
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.035|                      the six is correct hygiene either way.
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.036|
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.037|  Cible           : reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.038|                    Defender" /v DisableAntiSpyware and /v
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.039|                    DisableAntiVirus; reg delete
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.040|                    "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.041|                    Defender\Real-Time Protection" /v
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.042|                    DisableRealtimeMonitoring, /v
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.043|                    DisableBehaviorMonitoring, /v
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.044|                    DisableOnAccessProtection, /v
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.045|                    DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable. All six REG_DWORD.
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.046|                    OPTY.bat lines 1766-1771 in :reassert_defaults. Values
::X|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.047|                    only - the keys themselves are never deleted.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|REMOVE THE DEFENDER KILL POLICIES
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.003|  What it is      : Six policy REG_DWORDs that debloat scripts write to
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.004|                    keep Microsoft Defender permanently off:
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.005|                    DisableAntiSpyware and DisableAntiVirus under
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.006|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender,
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.007|                    plus DisableRealtimeMonitoring,
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.008|                    DisableBehaviorMonitoring, DisableOnAccessProtection
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.009|                    and DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable under its Real-Time
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.010|                    Protection subkey. Being policy, they sit above the
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.011|                    Windows Security app and grey its switches out.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.012|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.013|  Actual effect   : Deletes all six values. The keys stay (empty keys are
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.014|                    harmless and deleting user-owned keys is not this
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.015|                    script's job). Windows Security stops reporting a
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.016|                    managed state and its real-time protection switch
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.017|                    becomes usable again. No reboot: Defender picks the
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.018|                    change up on the fly.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.019|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.020|  Gain            : Honestly, it depends on one thing you cannot see from
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.021|                    here. If Tamper Protection is ON - the default on a
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.022|                    normal consumer Windows 11 - Defender was already
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.023|                    ignoring these values, real-time scanning was already
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.024|                    running, and the only measurable change is cosmetic:
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.025|                    Windows Security drops the "your settings are managed
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.026|                    by your organisation" banner and the greyed-out switch
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.027|                    comes back. If Tamper Protection is OFF, the gain is
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.028|                    real and large: real-time protection was genuinely
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.029|                    disabled and starts working again. There is no
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.030|                    performance gain in either direction - this costs
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.031|                    performance, it does not buy it.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.032|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.033|  Cost            : Real-time scanning costs disk I/O. Compiling,
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.034|                    extracting large archives and first-launch
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.035|                    shader/asset work get slower, typically a few percent,
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.036|                    more on a slow disk. The answer to that is a folder
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.037|                    exclusion, not a disabled antivirus. If you run a
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.038|                    third-party AV it registers with Windows Security and
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.039|                    Defender drops into passive mode by itself - you will
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.040|                    not end up with two scanners fighting.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.041|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.042|  Windows default : All six absent. Windows Setup never writes any of
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.043|                    them; every one you find came from a script or a GPO.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.044|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.045|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.046|    1                    : Policy says "switch this component off".
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.047|                           DisableAntiSpyware=1 was the old master kill
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.048|                           switch for the whole Defender engine; Microsoft
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.049|                           deprecated it and machines with Tamper
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.050|                           Protection on ignore it outright. The four
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.051|                           Real-Time Protection values still bite when
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.052|                           Tamper Protection is off:
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.053|                           DisableRealtimeMonitoring stops on-access
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.054|                           scanning entirely, DisableBehaviorMonitoring
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.055|                           stops the behavioural engine,
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.056|                           DisableOnAccessProtection stops scanning on
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.057|                           file open/close, DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.058|                           stops the catch-up scan that runs when real-
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.059|                           time protection is re-armed. Whatever the value
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.060|                           does or does not do, its presence makes Windows
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.061|                           Security grey the switch out and print "managed
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.062|                           by your organisation".
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.063|    0                    : The trap. Writing 0 does NOT restore the
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.064|                           default - it creates a third state: a policy
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.065|                           that says "forced on". The component runs, but
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.066|                           the switch stays greyed out in Windows Security
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.067|                           and the machine still reports itself as
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.068|                           managed. Anything that later needs to toggle
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.069|                           real-time protection (a third-party AV
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.070|                           installer, a Defender update, you) is blocked
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.071|                           by your own policy.
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.072|    DELETE               : The real default: no policy at all. Defender
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.073|                           obeys the Windows Security app, the switches
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.074|                           are usable, and the state you set in the UI is
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.075|                           the state you get. This is what the script
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.076|                           writes for every profile.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|  Why these profiles : All five profiles delete, profile 5 included,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|                       because absent is genuinely what Windows ships.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.003|                       There is no machine role where a hidden policy that
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.004|                       silently disables the antivirus is the right state,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.005|                       and the box that seeds torrents and serves Plex to
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.006|                       the internet is the last one that should have it.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.007|                       If you deliberately run a third-party AV you still
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.008|                       want these gone: your AV takes Defender out of the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.009|                       way through its own passive-mode registration, not
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.010|                       through these keys, and leaving them behind means
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.011|                       Defender cannot come back if you ever uninstall the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.012|                       other product. This is a table with no variation in
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.013|                       it, and inventing one would be dishonest.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.014|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.015|  Known problems  : Nothing known breaks by deleting them. The breakage
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.016|                    runs the other way: with these values left in place
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.017|                    and Tamper Protection off, Windows Security shows a
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.018|                    permanent red "Virus & threat protection is off" state
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.019|                    that no button in the UI can clear, and third-party AV
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.020|                    installers occasionally abort because they cannot
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.021|                    query Defender's status.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.022|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.023|  Unverified      : With Tamper Protection ON, Defender already ignores
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.024|                    all six, so the deletion may change nothing you can
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.025|                    observe apart from the Windows Security page dropping
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.026|                    its "managed" badge. I cannot tell from the registry
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.027|                    alone whether Tamper Protection is on for a given
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.028|                    machine; it lives under
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.029|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.030|                    Defender\Features\TamperProtection, which is itself
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.031|                    tamper-protected and unreliable to read. Deleting the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.032|                    six is correct hygiene either way.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.033|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.034|  Target          : reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.035|                    Defender" /v DisableAntiSpyware and /v
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.036|                    DisableAntiVirus; reg delete
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.037|                    "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.038|                    Defender\Real-Time Protection" /v
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.039|                    DisableRealtimeMonitoring, /v
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.040|                    DisableBehaviorMonitoring, /v
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.041|                    DisableOnAccessProtection, /v
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.042|                    DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable. All six REG_DWORD.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.043|                    OPTY.bat lines 1766-1771 in :reassert_defaults. Values
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.044|                    only - the keys themselves are never deleted.
