::P|rad.uac.on|1/5/1|1/5/1|1/5/1|1/5/1|1/5/1|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.001|REACTIVER LE CONTROLE DE COMPTE D'UTILISATEUR (UAC)
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.002|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.003|  Ce que c est    : L'UAC, c'est l'invite d'elevation. Trois valeurs sous 
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policie
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.005|                    s\System decident s'il existe (EnableLUA), comment il
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.006|                    demande (ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin) et si la boite
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.007|                    est dessinee sur le bureau securise isole
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.008|                    (PromptOnSecureDesktop).
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.009|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 1 / 5 / 1, exactement la combinaison que le
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.011|                    curseur du Panneau de configuration appelle sa
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.012|                    position par defaut. ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin et
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.013|                    PromptOnSecureDesktop s'appliquent tout de suite.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.014|                    EnableLUA non : tant que vous n'avez pas redemarre, la
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.015|                    valeur affiche 1 alors que la session en cours se
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.016|                    comporte encore comme si l'UAC etait coupe. L'ecriture
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.017|                    est inconditionnelle - sur une machine neuve ou
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.018|                    inconnue, c'est l'ecriture qui rend la chose vraie,
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.019|                    meme si la valeur y etait deja.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.020|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.021|  Gain            : Considerable, et ce n'est pas un chiffre de
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.022|                    performance. Avec EnableLUA=0, tout processus lance
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.023|                    par un administrateur recoit le jeton admin complet
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.024|                    des le depart : plus rien n'a besoin de demander, ni
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.025|                    un installeur telecharge, ni une macro, ni un chargeur
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.026|                    de mods. Cela coupe aussi la virtualisation fichier et
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.027|                    registre et empeche toute application empaquetee de
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.028|                    demarrer - d'ou les machines sans UAC qui n'ouvrent ni
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.029|                    les Parametres ni Securite Windows.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.030|                    PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 compte pour une raison precise
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.031|                    et plus etroite : sur le bureau normal, n'importe quel
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.032|                    processus lance sous votre compte peut se superposer a
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.033|                    la boite de consentement, la capturer, ou lui envoyer
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.034|                    des evenements.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.035|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.036|  Cout            : Vous revoyez les invites de consentement lors des
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.037|                    installations et des changements systeme. C'est tout
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.038|                    le cout. L'UAC ne ralentit pas la machine - le jeton
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.039|                    filtre est construit une fois a l'ouverture de
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.040|                    session, pas a chaque operation - et il ne coute ni
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.041|                    images par seconde, ni debit, ni batterie.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.042|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.043|  Defaut Windows  : 1 / 5 / 1, sur toutes les editions de Windows 11. Les
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.044|                    trois sont livrees presentes : restaurer le defaut est
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.045|                    donc une ecriture, pas une suppression.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.046|
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.047|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.048|    EnableLUA=1          : L'UAC existe. Un administrateur ouvre sa
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.049|                           session avec un jeton filtre et doit s'elever -
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.050|                           par consentement ou par identifiants - pour
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.051|                           recuperer le jeton complet. La virtualisation
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.052|                           fichier et registre est active (un vieux
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.053|                           programme qui ecrit dans son propre dossier
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.054|                           Program Files est redirige en silence vers
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.055|                           %LOCALAPPDATA%\VirtualStore au lieu d'echouer),
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.056|                           et les applications empaquetees du Store
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.057|                           peuvent demarrer, parce qu'AppContainer a
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.058|                           besoin des niveaux d'integrite. C'est la valeur
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.059|                           livree. La modifier exige un redemarrage.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.060|    EnableLUA=0          : L'UAC n'existe pas. Chaque processus lance par
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.061|                           un administrateur recoit le jeton admin complet
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.062|                           des le depart : rien n'a besoin de demander,
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.063|                           donc rien ne demande - un installeur
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.064|                           telecharge, une macro Office, un chargeur de
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.065|                           mods. La virtualisation est coupee et toutes
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.066|                           les applications empaquetees refusent de
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.067|                           demarrer : c'est pour cela qu'une machine en
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.068|                           EnableLUA=0 n'ouvre ni les Parametres, ni
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.069|                           Securite Windows, ni le Store. C'est la valeur
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.070|                           que le script repare.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.071|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=0 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.072|        Elevation sans invite. L'UAC est techniquement encore la mais
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.073|        accorde chaque demande en silence. C'est ce que laisse la position
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.074|        « Ne jamais m'avertir » du curseur, et c'est le pire des deux
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.075|        mondes : la barriere a disparu mais vous croyez encore l'avoir.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.076|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=1 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.077|        Demande les identifiants sur le bureau securise. Vous retapez
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.078|        votre mot de passe a chaque elevation. Cette valeur force le
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.079|        bureau securise quelle que soit PromptOnSecureDesktop.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.080|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=2 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.081|        Demande un consentement sur le bureau securise pour tout, y
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.082|        compris les binaires signes par Windows. C'est le cran haut du
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.083|        curseur, « M'avertir toujours ». Cette valeur force elle aussi le
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.084|        bureau securise.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.085|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=3 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.086|        Demande les identifiants, sur le bureau normal sauf si
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.087|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 la ramene sur le bureau securise.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.088|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=4 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.089|        Demande un consentement pour tout, sur le bureau normal sauf si
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.090|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 en decide autrement.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.091|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=5 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.092|        Demande un consentement pour les binaires non-Windows. Le defaut
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.093|        livre : les executables signes Microsoft a auto-elevation passent
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.094|        sans boite de dialogue, tout le reste demande. Combine a
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.095|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1, c'est la position mediane du curseur que
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.096|        le Panneau de configuration appelle « par defaut ».
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.097|    PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.098|        La boite de consentement est dessinee sur un bureau isole :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.099|        l'ecran s'assombrit et le reste de la session est mis de cote.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.100|        Aucun processus en droits utilisateur ne peut se superposer a la
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.101|        boite, la capturer, ni lui envoyer un clic ou une frappe simulee.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.102|        Valeur livree.
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.103|    PromptOnSecureDesktop=0 :
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.104|        La boite s'affiche sur votre bureau normal. Plus rapide et moins
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.105|        brutal - et n'importe quel programme lance sous votre compte peut
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.106|        desormais se superposer a elle ou cliquer a votre place. La seule
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.107|        raison legitime de la choisir : les outils de prise en main a
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.108|        distance qui ne voient pas le bureau securise (VNC simple, Chrome
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.109|        Remote Desktop) affichent un ecran noir ou fige quand une invite
::T|FR|rad.uac.on.110|        apparait. RDP n'a pas ce probleme.
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Identique sur les cinq, et le profil 5 est
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.002|                         identique aussi puisque 1/5/1 est litteralement
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.003|                         ce que Windows livre. Aucun usage ne gagne a
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.004|                         couper l'UAC : ca ne coute ni images, ni debit,
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.005|                         ni autonomie, donc gaming, serveur et portable
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.006|                         n'ont rien a echanger contre. Il y a une vraie
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.007|                         exception, mais ce n'est pas un profil : si vous
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.008|                         pilotez une machine sans ecran avec un outil de
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.009|                         prise en main qui ne sait pas afficher le bureau
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.010|                         securise - VNC simple, Chrome Remote Desktop -
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.011|                         l'invite apparait comme un ecran noir ou fige.
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.012|                         RDP n'est pas concerne, il affiche le bureau
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.013|                         securise dans sa propre session. Dans ce cas
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.014|                         precis, mettez PromptOnSecureDesktop=0 a la main
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.015|                         et laissez les deux autres a 1 et 5 ; ne coupez
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.016|                         pas l'UAC.
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.017|
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.018|  Problemes connus : Concret, et ca mord quand on rallume l'UAC apres
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.019|                     avoir installe des logiciels sans lui : les
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.020|                     programmes qui ecrivent leur configuration dans leur
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.021|                     propre dossier Program Files ou dans HKLM le
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.022|                     faisaient pour de vrai, et se retrouvent maintenant
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.023|                     rediriges vers %LOCALAPPDATA%\VirtualStore - ils ont
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.024|                     l'air d'avoir perdu leurs reglages. Les vieux jeux et
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.025|                     les petits utilitaires font ca. La solution est de
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.026|                     donner a votre compte le droit d'ecriture sur ce
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.027|                     dossier, pas de recouper l'UAC. Deuxieme point : le
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.028|                     redemarrage n'est pas decoratif. EnableLUA=1 ecrit
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.029|                     sans redemarrage, la session garde l'ancien
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.030|                     comportement et rien de ce que vous observez n'aura
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.031|                     change.
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.032|
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.033|  Cible           : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policie
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.034|                    s\System : EnableLUA (REG_DWORD, 1),
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.035|                    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin (REG_DWORD, 5),
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.036|                    PromptOnSecureDesktop (REG_DWORD, 1). Written through
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.037|                    :regset at OPTY.bat lines 1774, 1775 and 1779. All
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.038|                    three are writes, never deletes - Windows ships them
::X|FR|rad.uac.on.039|                    present.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.001|TURN USER ACCOUNT CONTROL BACK ON
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.002|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.003|  What it is      : UAC is the elevation prompt. Three values under HKLM\S
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.004|                    OFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Syst
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.005|                    em decide whether it exists at all (EnableLUA), how it
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.006|                    asks (ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin) and whether the
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.007|                    dialog is drawn on the isolated secure desktop
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.008|                    (PromptOnSecureDesktop).
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.009|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.010|  Actual effect   : Writes 1 / 5 / 1, which is exactly the combination the
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.011|                    Control Panel slider calls its default position.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.012|                    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin and PromptOnSecureDesktop
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.013|                    take effect immediately. EnableLUA does not: until you
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.014|                    reboot, the value reads 1 while the running session
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.015|                    still behaves as if UAC were off. The write is
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.016|                    unconditional - on a fresh or foreign machine writing
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.017|                    it is what makes it true, even if it already held that
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.018|                    value.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.019|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.020|  Gain            : Large, and it is not a performance number. With
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.021|                    EnableLUA=0 every process an administrator starts gets
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.022|                    the full admin token at launch, so nothing ever has to
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.023|                    ask: not an installer you downloaded, not a macro, not
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.024|                    a mod loader. It also switches off file and registry
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.025|                    virtualisation and stops every packaged app from
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.026|                    launching, which is why machines with UAC off cannot
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.027|                    open Settings or Windows Security.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.028|                    PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 matters for a narrower,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.029|                    concrete reason: on the normal desktop any process
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.030|                    running as you can draw over the consent dialog,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.031|                    capture it, or send input to it.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.032|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.033|  Cost            : You see consent prompts again when installing software
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.034|                    or changing system settings. That is the entire cost.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.035|                    UAC does not slow the machine down - the filtered
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.036|                    token is built once at logon, not per operation - and
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.037|                    it costs nothing in frames, throughput or battery.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.038|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.039|  Windows default : 1 / 5 / 1, on every Windows 11 edition. All three ship
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.040|                    present, so restoring the default is a write, not a
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.041|                    delete.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.042|
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.043|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.044|    EnableLUA=1          : UAC exists. An administrator logs on with a
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.045|                           filtered token and has to elevate - by consent
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.046|                           or by credentials - to get the full one. File
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.047|                           and registry virtualisation is active (an old
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.048|                           program writing to its own Program Files folder
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.049|                           is silently redirected to
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.050|                           %LOCALAPPDATA%\VirtualStore instead of
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.051|                           failing), and packaged Store/UWP apps can
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.052|                           launch, because AppContainer needs integrity
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.053|                           levels to exist. This is the shipped value.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.054|                           Changing it requires a reboot.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.055|    EnableLUA=0          : UAC does not exist. Every process an
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.056|                           administrator starts receives the full admin
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.057|                           token at launch: nothing has to ask, so nothing
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.058|                           does - a downloaded installer, an Office macro,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.059|                           a mod loader. Virtualisation is off, and every
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.060|                           packaged app refuses to start, which is why a
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.061|                           machine with EnableLUA=0 cannot open Settings,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.062|                           Windows Security or the Store. This is the
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.063|                           value the script repairs.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.064|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=0 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.065|        Elevate without prompting. UAC is technically still on but grants
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.066|        every elevation request silently. This is what the "Never notify"
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.067|        slider position leaves behind, and it is the worst of both worlds:
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.068|        the security boundary is gone but you still believe you have one.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.069|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=1 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.070|        Prompt for credentials on the secure desktop. You retype your
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.071|        account password for every elevation. Secure desktop is forced by
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.072|        this value regardless of PromptOnSecureDesktop.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.073|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=2 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.074|        Prompt for consent on the secure desktop for everything, including
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.075|        Windows' own signed binaries. This is the top slider notch,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.076|        "Always notify". Secure desktop is forced by this value too.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.077|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=3 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.078|        Prompt for credentials, on the normal desktop unless
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.079|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 puts it back on the secure one.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.080|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=4 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.081|        Prompt for consent for everything, on the normal desktop unless
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.082|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 says otherwise.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.083|    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=5 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.084|        Prompt for consent for non-Windows binaries. The shipped default:
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.085|        Windows' own signed, auto-elevating executables go through without
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.086|        a dialog, everything else asks. Combined with
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.087|        PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 this is the middle slider position the
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.088|        Control Panel calls the default.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.089|    PromptOnSecureDesktop=1 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.090|        The consent dialog is drawn on a separate, isolated desktop - the
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.091|        screen dims and the rest of the session is frozen out. No user-
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.092|        level process can overlay the dialog, screenshot it, or synthesise
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.093|        a click or keystroke at it. Shipped default.
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.094|    PromptOnSecureDesktop=0 :
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.095|        The dialog appears on your normal desktop. Faster and less
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.096|        jarring, and any program running as you can now draw over it or
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.097|        click it for you. The one legitimate reason to choose it: remote-
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.098|        control tools that cannot see the secure desktop (plain VNC,
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.099|        Chrome Remote Desktop) show a black or frozen screen when a prompt
::T|EN|rad.uac.on.100|        fires. RDP does not have this problem.
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.001|  Why these profiles : Identical on all five, and profile 5 is identical
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.002|                       too because 1/5/1 is literally what Windows ships.
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.003|                       No role gains from turning UAC off: it costs no
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.004|                       frames, no throughput and no battery, so gaming,
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.005|                       server and laptop have nothing to trade away for
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.006|                       it. There is one real exception, and it is not a
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.007|                       profile: if you drive a headless box with a remote-
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.008|                       control tool that cannot render the secure desktop
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.009|                       - plain VNC, Chrome Remote Desktop - the prompt
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.010|                       shows up as a black or frozen screen. RDP is
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.011|                       unaffected, it renders the secure desktop inside
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.012|                       its own session. If that is your situation, set
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.013|                       PromptOnSecureDesktop=0 by hand and leave the other
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.014|                       two at 1 and 5; do not turn UAC off.
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.015|
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.016|  Known problems  : Concrete, and it bites when you turn UAC back on after
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.017|                    software was installed with it off: programs that
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.018|                    write their settings into their own Program Files
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.019|                    folder or into HKLM were doing it for real, and now
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.020|                    get redirected into %LOCALAPPDATA%\VirtualStore, so
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.021|                    they look like they lost their configuration. Older
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.022|                    games and small utilities do this. Fix it by granting
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.023|                    your account write access to that folder, not by
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.024|                    switching UAC off again. Second, the reboot is not
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.025|                    cosmetic: with EnableLUA=1 written and no reboot, the
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.026|                    session keeps the old behaviour and nothing you
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.027|                    observe will have changed.
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.028|
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.029|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policie
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.030|                    s\System : EnableLUA (REG_DWORD, 1),
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.031|                    ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin (REG_DWORD, 5),
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.032|                    PromptOnSecureDesktop (REG_DWORD, 1). Written through
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.033|                    :regset at OPTY.bat lines 1774, 1775 and 1779. All
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.034|                    three are writes, never deletes - Windows ships them
::X|EN|rad.uac.on.035|                    present.
::
:: ---- rad.hvci.killkey (risky) ----------------------------------
::P|rad.hvci.killkey|0|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.001|EFFACER LES FORCAGES HVCI ET VBS
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.002|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : Deux REG_DWORD qui epinglent la pile de securite par
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.004|                    hyperviseur en position eteinte : DeviceGuard\Scenario
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.005|                    s\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled, ce que
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.006|                    Securite Windows appelle l'integrite de la memoire, et
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.007|                    DeviceGuard\EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity, la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.008|                    couche VBS sur laquelle l'integrite de la memoire
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.009|                    repose.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.010|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.011|  Effet reel      : La reponse 1 ecrit 0 sur les deux : l'integrite de la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.012|                    memoire et VBS restent eteints. Les reponses 2 a 5
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.013|                    suppriment les deux valeurs : la machine revient a ce
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.014|                    que son materiel, son firmware et son image decident
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.015|                    reellement, et le bouton Isolation du noyau reprend la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.016|                    main. Dans les deux cas, rien ne bouge avant le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.017|                    redemarrage.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.018|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.019|  Gain            : En supprimant : l'integrite de la memoire redevient
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.020|                    activable, et ce qu'elle apporte est precis -
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.021|                    l'hyperviseur valide les pages de code noyau avant
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.022|                    qu'elles puissent s'executer, si bien qu'un pilote
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.023|                    signe mais vulnerable ne peut plus servir de porte
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.024|                    d'entree pour charger du code noyau non signe. C'est
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.025|                    la technique BYOVD, celle qu'utilisent la plupart des
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.026|                    malwares noyau et la plupart des chargeurs de triche.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.027|                    En forcant 0 : un gain d'images reel, mais plus petit
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.028|                    et plus etroit que ce que racontent les forums. Le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.029|                    mecanisme existe (avec VBS actif, le systeme tourne
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.030|                    sous un hyperviseur, les acces memoire passent par une
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.031|                    traduction d'adresse a deux niveaux, et HVCI ajoute un
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.032|                    travail de verification sur les pages de code noyau),
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.033|                    mais les mesures publiees vont de rien du tout a une
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.034|                    dizaine de pour cent, et c'est quasi nul des que vous
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.035|                    etes limite par le GPU. Mesurez votre jeu avant de
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.036|                    supposer. Et si WSL2, Docker, Hyper-V ou le bac a
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.037|                    sable Windows sont installes, l'hyperviseur tourne de
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.038|                    toute facon et vous ne recuperez presque rien.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.039|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.040|  Cout            : Laisser VBS revenir demarre l'hyperviseur. Les
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.041|                    anciennes versions de VMware Workstation et
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.042|                    VirtualBox, certains emulateurs Android et d'autres
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.043|                    montages de virtualisation imbriquee tournent tres mal
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.044|                    ou refusent de demarrer quand il est actif - et c'est
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.045|                    tres souvent exactement la raison pour laquelle
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.046|                    quelqu'un l'avait desactive. Comme le changement ne
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.047|                    prend effet qu'au prochain redemarrage, vous
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.048|                    decouvrirez le conflit a ce moment-la, pas maintenant.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.049|                    Par ailleurs, un pilote noyau ancien ou non signe peut
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.050|                    refuser de se charger une fois l'integrite de la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.051|                    memoire active ; Securite Windows nomme le pilote
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.052|                    fautif quand il bloque l'activation. Si un outil de VM
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.053|                    cesse de fonctionner apres coup : mettez-le a jour -
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.054|                    VMware Workstation actuel et VirtualBox 7 passent par
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.055|                    la plateforme d'hyperviseur Windows et cohabitent avec
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.056|                    VBS - ou recoupez l'integrite de la memoire dans
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.057|                    Securite Windows.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.058|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.059|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs absentes. Ce que cela produit n'est
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.060|                    pas fixe : une installation propre sur du materiel
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.061|                    eligible demarre generalement avec VBS et l'integrite
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.062|                    de la memoire actives, une machine mise a niveau
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.063|                    depuis Windows 10 demarre generalement avec les deux
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.064|                    eteints. L'absence reste le defaut ; l'etat qui en
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.065|                    decoule est une question de materiel et d'image, pas
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.066|                    de registre.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.067|
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.068|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.069|    0                    : Ecrit sur les deux valeurs, c'est un « reste
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.070|                           eteint » explicite.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.071|                           HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.072|                           empeche l'hyperviseur de valider les pages de
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.073|                           code noyau ;
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.074|                           EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0 demonte la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.075|                           couche VBS elle-meme, et emporte au passage
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.076|                           Credential Guard et le reste de l'isolation du
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.077|                           noyau. Effet au prochain demarrage. Reserve
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.078|                           importante : si Hyper-V, WSL2, Docker Desktop,
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.079|                           le bac a sable Windows ou la plateforme de
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.080|                           machine virtuelle sont installes, l'hyperviseur
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.081|                           demarre quand meme pour eux et le surcout CPU
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.082|                           que vous vouliez supprimer reste en grande
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.083|                           partie la.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.084|    1                    : Un « force a l'allumage » explicite, et le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.085|                           script ne l'ecrit jamais volontairement. Le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.086|                           bouton de Securite Windows verifie la
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.087|                           compatibilite des pilotes avant d'activer
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.088|                           l'integrite de la memoire ; une ecriture
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.089|                           registre, non. Forcer 1 sur une machine dont un
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.090|                           pilote noyau est incompatible signifie que ce
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.091|                           pilote ne se charge pas au demarrage - et si
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.092|                           c'est un pilote de stockage ou de reseau, vous
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.093|                           l'apprenez de la pire facon. Passez par la page
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.094|                           Isolation du noyau de Securite Windows, pas par
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.095|                           cette cle.
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.096|    DELETE               : Les deux valeurs absentes : le vrai defaut
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.097|                           Windows. L'etat reel decoule alors de l'image,
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.098|                           du firmware (VT-x/AMD-V avec SLAT, et le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.099|                           demarrage securise pour l'ensemble des
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.100|                           fonctions) et des pilotes presents, et le
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.101|                           bouton Isolation du noyau de Securite Windows
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.102|                           reprend la main. A savoir : une valeur voisine,
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.103|                           Locked=1 dans la meme cle Scenarios, epingle
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.104|                           l'integrite de la memoire pour que l'interface
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.105|                           ne puisse plus la changer ; le script ne
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.106|                           l'ecrit ni ne la supprime, donc si le bouton
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.107|                           reste grise apres coup, c'est la qu'il faut
::T|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.108|                           regarder.
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Seul le profil gaming differe, et il differe pour
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.002|                         quelque chose de mesurable, pas d'affirme. 1
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.003|                         ecrit 0/0 parce que le surcout VBS est reel dans
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.004|                         les jeux limites par le CPU et qu'un PC de jeu
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.005|                         est l'endroit ou l'echange se defend - en sachant
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.006|                         que c'est une baisse de securite que vous
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.007|                         choisissez, pas un gain gratuit. 2, 3, 4 et 5
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.008|                         suppriment. Serveur : la machine ingere du
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.009|                         contenu non fiable venu d'Internet toute la
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.010|                         journee et la disponibilite est le sujet, donc
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.011|                         elle garde la protection ; si elle heberge des VM
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.012|                         sous VMware ou VirtualBox, repondez 1 - ce
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.013|                         conflit est la seule chose qui justifie vraiment
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.014|                         de couper VBS sur un serveur. Bureautique : rien
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.015|                         a echanger, le cout CPU est invisible hors des
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.016|                         jeux. Portable : je n'ai trouve aucun gain
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.017|                         d'autonomie mesurable a couper VBS, et un
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.018|                         portable est la machine la plus susceptible
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.019|                         d'etre volee, donc il garde - pretendre un gain
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.020|                         de batterie ici serait exactement le genre de
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.021|                         phrase invérifiable que cette reecriture cherche
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.022|                         a supprimer. 5 supprime parce que l'absence est
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.023|                         l'etat livre.
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.024|
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.025|  Problemes connus : Concret, et dans les deux sens. L'integrite de la
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.026|                     memoire qui refuse de s'activer en nommant un pilote
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.027|                     incompatible : classique avec les vieux pilotes
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.028|                     d'overclocking et de monitoring des fabricants de
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.029|                     cartes meres (utilitaires ASUS, Gigabyte, MSI) et les
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.030|                     anciens pilotes de controle RGB. Dans l'autre sens,
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.031|                     VirtualBox 6.0/6.1 et VMware Workstation anterieur a
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.032|                     16.x retombent sur un mode d'emulation tres lent ou
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.033|                     n'arrivent plus a demarrer une VM une fois
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.034|                     l'hyperviseur en place.
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.035|
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.036|  Non verifie (en)  : Two things I cannot substantiate. First, whether
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.037|                      these values ship absent or present depends on the
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.038|                      image: a clean Windows 11 install on qualifying
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.039|                      hardware normally comes up with VBS and Memory
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.040|                      Integrity on and no override written, while a
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.041|                      machine upgraded from Windows 10 normally comes up
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.042|                      with them off - so deleting the overrides may well
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.043|                      leave Memory Integrity off, and the card should not
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.044|                      promise it comes back. Second, the script's own
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.045|                      comment at line 1567 claims some anti-cheats
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.046|                      (Vanguard, FACEIT) require Memory Integrity ON. I
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.047|                      could not substantiate that. What those anti-cheats
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.048|                      actually require on Windows 11 is Secure Boot and
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.049|                      TPM 2.0, which are firmware settings and have
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.050|                      nothing to do with this key. Treat "anticheat needs
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.051|                      HVCI" as unverified folklore until someone tests it.
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.052|
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.053|  Cible           : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard\Scen
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.054|                    arios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity /v Enabled
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.055|                    (REG_DWORD) and
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.056|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard /v
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.057|                    EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity (REG_DWORD). Deleted
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.058|                    through :killkey at OPTY.bat lines 1792-1793
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.059|                    (:reassert_defaults) and 1652-1653 (:debloat_restore);
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.060|                    the matching reg add /d 0 pair is at lines 1571-1572.
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.061|                    Sibling value Locked under the same Scenarios key is
::X|FR|rad.hvci.killkey.062|                    NOT touched.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.001|CLEAR THE HVCI AND VBS OVERRIDES
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.003|  What it is      : Two REG_DWORDs that pin the hypervisor security stack
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.004|                    off: DeviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeInteg
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.005|                    rity\Enabled, which is what Windows Security calls
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.006|                    Memory Integrity, and
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.007|                    DeviceGuard\EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity, which
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.008|                    is the VBS layer Memory Integrity runs on top of.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.009|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.010|  Actual effect   : Answer 1 writes 0 to both, so Memory Integrity and VBS
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.011|                    stay off. Answers 2 to 5 delete both values, so the
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.012|                    machine goes back to what its hardware, firmware and
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.013|                    image actually decide and the Core Isolation toggle
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.014|                    governs again. Either way nothing changes until you
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.015|                    reboot.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.016|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.017|  Gain            : Deleting: Memory Integrity becomes switchable again,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.018|                    and what it buys is specific - the hypervisor
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.019|                    validates kernel-mode code pages before they can
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.020|                    execute, so a signed but vulnerable driver cannot be
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.021|                    used as a doorway to load unsigned kernel code. That
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.022|                    is the bring-your-own-vulnerable-driver technique
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.023|                    behind most kernel-level malware and most kernel cheat
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.024|                    loaders. Forcing 0: a frame rate gain that is real but
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.025|                    smaller and narrower than the forums claim. The
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.026|                    mechanism is genuine (with VBS active the OS runs
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.027|                    under a hypervisor, so memory access goes through
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.028|                    second-level address translation, and HVCI adds
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.029|                    verification work on kernel code pages), but published
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.030|                    measurements range from nothing to about ten percent,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.031|                    and it is close to nothing whenever you are GPU-bound.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.032|                    Measure your own game before you assume. And if WSL2,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.033|                    Docker, Hyper-V or Windows Sandbox is installed, the
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.034|                    hypervisor runs anyway and you recover very little.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.035|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.036|  Cost            : Letting VBS come back starts the hypervisor. Older
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.037|                    VMware Workstation and VirtualBox versions, some
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.038|                    Android emulators and other nested-virtualisation
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.039|                    setups run badly or refuse to start with it active -
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.040|                    which is very often exactly why someone disabled it in
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.041|                    the first place. And because the change only takes
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.042|                    effect at the next reboot, you discover the conflict
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.043|                    then, not now. Separately, an unsigned or old kernel
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.044|                    driver may fail to load once Memory Integrity is on;
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.045|                    Windows Security names the driver when it blocks the
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.046|                    toggle. If a VM tool stops working afterwards, update
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.047|                    it - current VMware Workstation and VirtualBox 7 use
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.048|                    the Windows Hypervisor Platform and coexist with VBS -
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.049|                    or turn Memory Integrity back off in Windows Security.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.050|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.051|  Windows default : Both values absent. What that produces is not fixed: a
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.052|                    clean install on qualifying hardware usually comes up
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.053|                    with VBS and Memory Integrity on, a machine upgraded
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.054|                    from Windows 10 usually comes up with them off. Absent
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.055|                    is still the default; the state that follows is a
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.056|                    hardware and image question, not a registry one.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.057|
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.058|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.059|    0                    : Written to both values, this is an explicit
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.060|                           "stay off".
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.061|                           HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0 stops
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.062|                           the hypervisor validating kernel-mode code
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.063|                           pages; EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.064|                           tears down the VBS layer itself, which also
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.065|                           takes Credential Guard and the rest of Core
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.066|                           Isolation with it. Takes effect at the next
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.067|                           boot. Important caveat: if Hyper-V, WSL2,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.068|                           Docker Desktop, Windows Sandbox or Virtual
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.069|                           Machine Platform is installed, the hypervisor
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.070|                           still launches for those, and the CPU overhead
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.071|                           you were trying to remove largely stays.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.072|    1                    : An explicit "force on", and the script
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.073|                           deliberately never writes it. The Windows
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.074|                           Security toggle checks driver compatibility
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.075|                           before enabling Memory Integrity; a registry
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.076|                           write does not. Forcing 1 on a machine with an
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.077|                           incompatible kernel driver means that driver
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.078|                           fails to load at boot - and if it is a storage
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.079|                           or network driver, you find out the hard way.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.080|                           Use the Core Isolation page in Windows Security
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.081|                           to turn it on, not this key.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.082|    DELETE               : Both values absent - the real Windows default.
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.083|                           The effective state then comes from the image,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.084|                           the firmware (VT-x/AMD-V plus SLAT and, for the
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.085|                           full feature set, Secure Boot) and the driver
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.086|                           set, and the Core Isolation toggle in Windows
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.087|                           Security governs it again. Note that a sibling
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.088|                           value, Locked=1 under the same Scenarios key,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.089|                           pins Memory Integrity so the UI cannot change
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.090|                           it; the script does not write or remove it, so
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.091|                           if the toggle is still greyed out after this,
::T|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.092|                           that is what to look at.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : Only gaming differs, and it differs for something
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.002|                       measurable rather than asserted. 1 writes 0/0
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.003|                       because the VBS overhead is real in CPU-bound
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.004|                       titles and a gaming desktop is where you might
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.005|                       reasonably trade it - be clear that this is a
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.006|                       security downgrade you are choosing, not a free
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.007|                       win. 2, 3, 4 and 5 all delete. Server: the box
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.008|                       takes untrusted content in from the internet all
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.009|                       day and uptime is the point, so it keeps the
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.010|                       protection; if it hosts VMs under VMware or
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.011|                       VirtualBox, answer 1 instead - that conflict is the
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.012|                       one thing that actually justifies switching VBS off
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.013|                       on a server. Office: nothing to trade, the CPU cost
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.014|                       is invisible outside games. Laptop: I found no
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.015|                       measurable battery gain from switching VBS off, and
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.016|                       a laptop is the machine most likely to be stolen,
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.017|                       so it keeps it - claiming a battery win here would
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.018|                       be exactly the kind of unsupported line this
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.019|                       rewrite exists to remove. 5 deletes because absent
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.020|                       is the shipped state.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.021|
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.022|  Known problems  : Concrete and both directions. Memory Integrity
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.023|                    refusing to switch on and naming an incompatible
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.024|                    driver is common with older motherboard-vendor
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.025|                    overclocking and monitoring drivers (ASUS, Gigabyte,
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.026|                    MSI utilities) and old RGB control drivers. In the
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.027|                    other direction, VirtualBox 6.0/6.1 and VMware
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.028|                    Workstation before 16.x drop to a very slow emulation
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.029|                    path or fail to start a VM once the hypervisor is up.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.030|
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.031|  Unverified      : Two things I cannot substantiate. First, whether these
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.032|                    values ship absent or present depends on the image: a
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.033|                    clean Windows 11 install on qualifying hardware
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.034|                    normally comes up with VBS and Memory Integrity on and
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.035|                    no override written, while a machine upgraded from
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.036|                    Windows 10 normally comes up with them off - so
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.037|                    deleting the overrides may well leave Memory Integrity
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.038|                    off, and the card should not promise it comes back.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.039|                    Second, the script's own comment at line 1567 claims
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.040|                    some anti-cheats (Vanguard, FACEIT) require Memory
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.041|                    Integrity ON. I could not substantiate that. What
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.042|                    those anti-cheats actually require on Windows 11 is
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.043|                    Secure Boot and TPM 2.0, which are firmware settings
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.044|                    and have nothing to do with this key. Treat "anticheat
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.045|                    needs HVCI" as unverified folklore until someone tests
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.046|                    it.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.047|
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.048|  Target          : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard\Scen
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.049|                    arios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity /v Enabled
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.050|                    (REG_DWORD) and
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.051|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard /v
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.052|                    EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity (REG_DWORD). Deleted
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.053|                    through :killkey at OPTY.bat lines 1792-1793
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.054|                    (:reassert_defaults) and 1652-1653 (:debloat_restore);
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.055|                    the matching reg add /d 0 pair is at lines 1571-1572.
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.056|                    Sibling value Locked under the same Scenarios key is
::X|EN|rad.hvci.killkey.057|                    NOT touched.
